  // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): trace policy constant 1 of 3, RETAINED.
  // GATE: test_display_fence.cjs drives the trace-scoped never-name list.
  var TRACE_NEVER_NAME = [
    'processed jd',
    /^studyroom-collect-/,
    'items'
  ];

  // The per-source phrasing, consulted ONLY when NO folder is speakable, and
  // keyed on the DAY'S DOMINANT SOURCE. Anything else — absent, empty,
  // `null`, unmapped, or an inherited property name — resolves to `elsewhere
  // in your vault`, so an unknown adapter is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a missing
  // one.
  //
  // ⚠ TWO OF THESE FOUR BRANCHES ARE DEAD ON THIS LIBRARY. Measured live
  // sources: `folder-drop` 2,256, `obsidian-vault` 878, `librarian` 1. No
  // `apple-notes` and no `apple-photos` item exists. They are kept for
  // CORRECTNESS — an adapter that lands later must not emit `undefined` — but
  // A GATE WHOSE ONLY POSITIVE CASE IS ONE OF THEM IS VACUOUS BY
  // CONSTRUCTION. Do not write one; the tests that touch them say so in their
  // own assertion messages.
  //
  // Frozen, so two composes can never observe a partially-built map.
  //
  // ⚠ RETAINED DELIBERATELY — 26.91-10 (F-3). Unreferenced by the render
  // path as of this wave (the composed sentence is off the page); kept whole
  // for Phase 26.95, which re-wires the trace if it returns. Deleting it, or
  // narrowing its gates so they pass with no surface to guard, is forbidden.
  // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): trace policy constant 2 of 3, RETAINED.
  var TRACE_SOURCE_PHRASE = Object.freeze({
    'obsidian-vault': 'your vault',
    'folder-drop': 'what you dropped in',
    'apple-notes': 'your phone notes',
    'apple-photos': 'your photos'
  });

  // The folder-name cap, applied BEFORE the sentence is composed so the tail
  // (`, and elsewhere in your vault.`) ALWAYS survives. Capping AFTER
  // composition would eat the law-5 tail on long names and turn its absence
  // into a tell about name length.
  //
  // LENGTH IS MEASURED IN JAVASCRIPT STRING UNITS (UTF-16 code units), stated
  // because that convention is what decides what a 24-glyph CJK name does:
  // each of those glyphs is one unit, so 24 of them pass whole. Measured
  // names in range: `chinese` (7), `ai-skills` (9), `Hubs` (4),
  // `emotional-management` (20); the longest observed exceeds the cap, and
  // that is what the cap is for.
  //
  // ⚠ RETAINED DELIBERATELY — 26.91-10 (F-3). Unreferenced by the render
  // path as of this wave (the composed sentence is off the page); kept whole
  // for Phase 26.95, which re-wires the trace if it returns. It is applied
  // BEFORE composition so the law-5 tail always survives, and that ordering
  // is the property its gate holds. Deleting it, or narrowing its gates so
  // they pass with no surface to guard, is forbidden.
  // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): trace policy constant 3 of 3, RETAINED.
  // Applied BEFORE composition so the law-5 tail always survives.
  var TRACE_FOLDER_CAP = 24;

  // 26.91-07 (PART B; D-03 / D-04 / D-05). ONE sentence for ONE day's
  // ALREADY-GUARDED arrivals, or '' when there are none:
  //
  //     the librarian brought in {kinds} from {place}.
  //
  // The owner's own framing: *"at day 07/21 librarian imported these notes
  // and photos... just gives the user a brief snapshot about the history."*
  // A TRACE, NOT A DOOR — one glanceable line, never a feed, never a list.
  //
  // PURE OVER ITS ARGUMENTS. It touches no store, reads no clock and CALLS
  // NO GUARD: the fence already ran inside packArrivalDays, and re-running
  // it here would be a SECOND implementation of a law-5 surface. This
  // function composes what the fence already let through.
  //
  // `{kinds}` IS ALWAYS PLURAL, whatever the cardinality. A plural-sensitive
  // string is a count in disguise — *a note* versus *some notes* leaks
  // 1-vs-many — and this line is built count-free the way `new in the room`
  // was (law 3; she vetoed *"2,762 more await"* on 2026-07-27, and no exact
  // count may appear on any front-facing surface). The order is FIXED
  // notes-then-photographs regardless of which arrived first or in what
  // proportion, so the sentence's shape cannot leak arrival order OR
  // proportion either.
  //
  // `{place}` names the speakable folder with the MOST surviving items on
  // the day, ties broken LEXICOGRAPHICALLY. Deterministic — no clock, no
  // Math.random, and no dependence on the order the arrivals arrive in.
  // Naming ONE folder reveals no number.
  //
  // ⚠ THE LAW-5 PROPERTY WORTH STATING OUT LOUD, because it is the
  // strongest property in part B and a later reader WILL be tempted to
  // "improve" it: `, and elsewhere in your vault` appears for the SAME
  // REASON whether the other folders were FENCED or merely NOT THE LARGEST.
  // A reader cannot tell which. Its ABSENCE is equally ambiguous — a day
  // whose only other folder was fenced reads EXACTLY like a day that only
  // ever had one folder. The tail is therefore not a tell that something
  // was hidden; it is ordinary sentence tail. BOTH DIRECTIONS ARE DRIVEN in
  // tests/test_blessings_notebook.cjs (6f). DO NOT distinguish the two
  // cases.
  //
  // `origin_path` is NEVER read here — it is not even in the model
  // packArrivalDays emits. See that function for the measurement.
  //
  // ⚠ RETAINED DELIBERATELY, AND THIS IS THE ANCHOR NOTICE — 26.91-10 (F-3).
  //
  // paintTracePage NO LONGER CALLS THIS FUNCTION. The owner asked for the
  // composed sentence to come off the page (26.91-UAT.md, gap F-3): she
  // already knows the librarian syncs when she enters the room, so the line
  // was redundant, and the need it answered belongs to Phase 26.95. Only the
  // CALL SITE went. The composer, its three TRACE_* constants and
  // NB_TRACE_GEOM.line all stay, each with this notice at its own site.
  //
  // IT IS NOT DEAD CODE — IT IS A FENCE WAITING FOR ITS SURFACE. Phase 26.95
  // re-wires the trace, and what it re-wires is exactly this: the
  // never-name list, the per-source phrasing, the 24-unit folder cap and the
  // law-5 tail that makes a fenced folder read like a folder that was never
  // there.
  //
  // ⚠ THE ONE THING A LATER PLAN MUST NOT DO. Deleting these symbols as
  // "dead code", or narrowing their gates so the gates pass with no surface
  // to guard, is FORBIDDEN. A gate that silently skips once its subject
  // vanishes is this project's named defect class in its purest form, and a
  // gate that cannot go red at all is the same defect wearing a green light.
  // G-F3 (tests/test_blessings_notebook.cjs) therefore asserts this fence
  // still FIRES rather than that it is quiet, and it drives the GUARDED
  // PIPELINE (packArrivalDays -> composeArrivalTrace) rather than this
  // function alone — because this function is PURE OVER ONE ARGUMENT, so
  // re-calling it with the same array after a status flip returns a
  // byte-identical string and could never go red.
  // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): RETAINED, and already call-less since F-3.
  // paintTracePage stopped calling this in wave 10; the shipped paint now
  // does not reach paintTracePage either. Kept whole for 26.95 — count-free,
  // {kinds} always plural, lexicographic tie-break, D-05's
  // /^studyroom-collect-/ rule. GATE: (G-F3/b) proves it still returns a
  // REAL non-empty sentence, and (G-F3/b2) proves that sentence reaches no
  // painted node when the retained painter is driven directly.
  function composeArrivalTrace(dayEntries) {
    var list = dayEntries || [];
    if (!list.length) { return ''; }
    // 26.91-08: ONE speakability test, reading the ONE trace-scoped
    // never-name list declared above (D-05's `/^studyroom-collect-/` rule
    // moved INTO it in this wave rather than staying inline beside it).
    // Comparison is EXACT and CASE-SENSITIVE on the stored value: no trim,
    // no fold, no normalisation.
    function speakable(name) {
      var f = String(name || '');
      if (!f) { return ''; }
      for (var r = 0; r < TRACE_NEVER_NAME.length; r++) {
        var rule = TRACE_NEVER_NAME[r];
        if (rule instanceof RegExp ? rule.test(f) : rule === f) { return ''; }
      }
      return f;
    }
    var i;
    var hasNotes = false;
    var hasPhotos = false;
    var counts = {};
    for (i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
      if (list[i].kind === 'photographs') { hasPhotos = true; }
      else { hasNotes = true; }
      var f = speakable(list[i].folder);
      if (f) {
        counts[f] = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(counts, f)
          ? counts[f] + 1 : 1;
      }
    }
    var kinds = (hasNotes && hasPhotos) ? 'notes and photographs'
      : (hasPhotos ? 'photographs' : 'notes');
    // SORTED FIRST, then strictly-greater — so the winner is the largest
    // folder and, on an exact tie, the lexicographically smaller NAME. The
    // sort is what removes the dependence on key insertion order, which is
    // an engine detail rather than a rule.
    var names = Object.keys(counts).sort();
    var win = '';
    for (i = 0; i < names.length; i++) {
      if (win === '' || counts[names[i]] > counts[win]) { win = names[i]; }
    }
    var place;
    if (win === '') {
      // 26.91-08: NOTHING is speakable, so — and ONLY so — the day's
      // DOMINANT source decides the phrasing. Same winner rule as folders
      // (sorted first, then strictly-greater), so a tie resolves
      // lexicographically rather than by key insertion order.
      var srcCounts = {};
      for (i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
        var s = String((list[i] && list[i].source) || '');
        if (!s) { continue; }
        srcCounts[s] = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(srcCounts, s)
          ? srcCounts[s] + 1 : 1;
      }
      var srcNames = Object.keys(srcCounts).sort();
      var domSrc = '';
      for (i = 0; i < srcNames.length; i++) {
        if (domSrc === '' || srcCounts[srcNames[i]] > srcCounts[domSrc]) {
          domSrc = srcNames[i];
        }
      }
      // hasOwnProperty, never a bare lookup: a bare lookup would let a
      // source literally named `constructor` reach Object.prototype and
      // emit a function body onto the page.
      place = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(TRACE_SOURCE_PHRASE,
        domSrc) ? TRACE_SOURCE_PHRASE[domSrc] : 'elsewhere in your vault';
    } else {
      var onlyWinner = true;
      for (i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
        if (speakable(list[i].folder) !== win) { onlyWinner = false; break; }
      }
      // 26.91-08: THE CAP RUNS HERE, BEFORE THE SENTENCE IS COMPOSED, so the
      // tail always survives. It is applied to the DISPLAY name only — the
      // winner selection, the never-name test and the `onlyWinner` scan all
      // run on the UNCAPPED stored value, so capping can never merge two
      // folders or unmask a never-named one.
      var shown = (win.length > TRACE_FOLDER_CAP)
        ? win.slice(0, TRACE_FOLDER_CAP) + '…' : win;
      place = onlyWinner
        ? 'your ' + shown + ' folder'
        : 'your ' + shown + ' folder, and elsewhere in your vault';
    }
    return 'the librarian brought in ' + kinds + ' from ' + place + '.';
  }

  // The populated-month roster, newest first — months exist ONLY
  // because blessings are in them; an empty month is not a view at
  // all (D-13). roster[0] is the default month.
  function blessingsMonthRoster(entries) {
    var list = entries || [];
    var seen = {};
    var roster = [];
    for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
      var key = list[i].monthKey;
      if (!seen[key]) { seen[key] = true; roster.push(key); }
    }
    return roster;
  }

  // D-C, AMENDED BY 26.91 D-01 (owner ruling of record, 2026-08-07). The
  // amendment is written here rather than replacing the original, because
  // the original's TWO DOCUMENTED PROPERTIES both survive it verbatim:
  //
  //   PURITY — it derives ONLY from already-guarded input handed in as
  //   ARGUMENTS (never a raw-ledger scan and never a raw item-store scan),
  //   so a day structurally exists ONLY while surviving input falls on it
  //   and an empty day is never in the set.
  //
  //   SELF-HEALING — it is re-derived on every paint, so a day self-heals
  //   OUT the instant its last surviving entry is fenced (the caller
  //   re-packs through the guard on every render; law 5 P0).
  //
  // WHAT D-01 CHANGED: the day-set used to EQUAL the calendar's lit-day
  // set — the distinct local days holding at least one SURVIVING blessing.
  // It is now the UNION of those lit days and the IMPORT days from
  // packArrivalDays. `lit` is an ATTRIBUTE OF AN ENTRY, not the shape of
  // the collection: a day is `lit: true` when she welcomed something on it
  // and `lit: false` when only the librarian filled it.
  //
  // WHY A PROMOTE AND NOT AN ADD-ALONGSIDE, in one line: the page-flip
  // order is the FIRST consumer that would have to sort the two kinds
  // against each other, so a second parallel roster would need an
  // interleave rule immediately — and two rosters that must agree are two
  // rosters that can disagree.
  //
  // AND NOTHING ELSE CHANGED. Still newest-first across months. Still ONE
  // entry per day (a day that is both collapses to one, `lit: true` —
  // welcoming wins). Still `monthKey` carried per entry so a cross-month
  // day step can switch the calendar's month. Still NO per-day count of
  // any kind (law 3).
  //
  // Import days are NEVER passed to blessingsMonthGrid. That boundary is
  // the entire mechanism keeping `lit` meaning exactly *you welcomed
  // something* — an import day is reachable by the prev/next PAGE FLIP,
  // and its calendar cell stays unlit and untappable (D-02, D-13/D-35).
  function blessingsDayRoster(entries, arrivals) {
    var lit = entries || [];
    var imported = arrivals || [];
    var seen = {};
    var newest = {};
    var order = [];
    var i;
    // A day is created UNLIT and lit is raised in exactly ONE place, on
    // every lit entry — so "welcoming wins" holds whichever side of the
    // union is seen first, and there is no second mechanism that could
    // disagree with this one. Written the other way first (lit set at
    // creation from `!!isLit`, then re-raised on a later hit) and mutation
    // testing showed the re-raise was DEAD CODE under the shipped loop
    // order: breaking it changed nothing and no gate went red. One
    // mechanism, reachable, or it is not a mechanism.
    function note(key, mk, when, isLit) {
      if (!(key in seen)) {
        seen[key] = { day: key, monthKey: mk, lit: false };
        newest[key] = when;
        order.push(key);
      }
      if (isLit) { seen[key].lit = true; }
      if (when > newest[key]) { newest[key] = when; }
    }
    for (i = 0; i < lit.length; i++) {
      note(lit[i].dayLabel, lit[i].monthKey, lit[i].ms, true);
    }
    // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): THE PROMOTE PATH. The owner
    // ruling of 2026-08-09 (A-14) removed the `arrivals` ARGUMENT from the
    // shipped call, so on the render path this loop runs zero times and the
    // returned roster is the calendar's lit-day set again — D-C's original
    // equality RESTORED, not re-derived. The path is NOT deleted: the
    // two-argument call keeps working whole, and 26.95 inherits it.
    // GATES THAT PROVE IT STILL FIRES: (G-B1), (G-B5) and (G-F1/1) all call
    // this function WITH arrivals and pin the promoted roster BY VALUE; the
    // shipped one-argument result is pinned separately by (G-17/dayset).
    for (i = 0; i < imported.length; i++) {
      note(imported[i].dayLabel, imported[i].monthKey, imported[i].ms, false);
    }
    // newest-first by the day's OWN millisecond value, never by input
    // order — the two input arrays are independently sorted and must not
    // be able to disagree about the merged order. Every ms within one
    // local day is strictly inside that day's interval, so ordering days
    // by their newest ms IS ordering them by date. The label tiebreak is
    // unreachable in practice (two distinct days cannot share an ms) and
    // exists so the comparator is total rather than clever.
    order.sort(function (a, b) {
      if (newest[b] !== newest[a]) { return newest[b] - newest[a]; }
      return a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0;
    });
    var roster = [];
    for (i = 0; i < order.length; i++) { roster.push(seen[order[i]]); }
    return roster;
  }

  // The month-grid model (D-13): one cell per day of the month. EVERY
  // cell is {day, lit} and NOTHING else — lit and unlit alike. A lit cell
  // means at least one SURVIVING blessing landed that local day. No today
  // field, no per-day count, no distinguishing mark of any kind — the
  // lit/unlit distinction is the calendar's entire vocabulary (law 3;
  // D-13/D-35 verbatim-binding). After D-1 that law holds STRUCTURALLY
  // rather than by convention: a lit cell and an unlit cell now carry the
  // identical key set, so there is no field left through which a third
  // mark could arrive.
  //
  // ---- D-1, CLOSED 26.91-13. WHY THERE IS NO SPREAD-INDEX FIELD HERE ----
  //
  // Until 26.91-13 a lit cell also carried a third property holding
  // `Math.ceil(i / 2)` over the flat newest-first entry index — a second,
  // WRONG, and entirely UNREAD derivation of the navigation target.
  // NOTHING consumed it. `paintNotebookCalendar`'s lit-cell click handler
  // (see below) builds the day's label from the displayed month plus the
  // cell's own `day` and then scans the REAL spread list for the first
  // entry whose `day` matches. That day-label scan over
  // `buildBlessingSpreads`' actual output is the correct derivation, and
  // it was already the only one in use.
  //
  // The two disagreed. Measured on the zero-overlap fixture before the
  // removal: for 06/03 the formula computed 1 while the executed tap
  // landed on spread 4 — and the tap was right, because
  // `buildBlessingSpreads` chunks day-aligned and mints spreads for
  // import-only days the flat entry index cannot see at all. After
  // 26.91-10 (one page per spread) `ceil(i / 2)` is not the right shape
  // for anything whatsoever.
  //
  // A parallel derivation of a value the navigation already computes
  // correctly is the shape this phase has spent ten plans removing, so it
  // is removed rather than corrected. `deferred-items.md` originally
  // routed D-1 to a follow-up phase; the owner OVERRODE that routing on
  // 2026-08-08 and pulled it into 26.91 gap closure — see
  // `26.91-CONTEXT.md` A-13. Do not look for the follow-up phase; there
  // is not one.
  function blessingsMonthGrid(entries, monthKey) {
    var parts = String(monthKey || '').split('-');
    var y = parseInt(parts[0], 10);
    var m = parseInt(parts[1], 10);
    if (!isFinite(y) || !isFinite(m)) { return null; }
    var daysInMonth = new Date(y, m, 0).getDate();
    var litDay = {};
    var list = entries || [];
    for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
      if (list[i].monthKey !== monthKey) { continue; }
      litDay[new Date(list[i].ms).getDate()] = true;
    }
    var days = [];
    for (var d = 1; d <= daysInMonth; d++) {
      days.push({ day: d, lit: d in litDay });
    }
    return {
      monthKey: monthKey,
      firstWeekday: new Date(y, m - 1, 1).getDay(),
      days: days
    };
  }

  // ---- the notebook painters --------------------------------------------------

  // One rendered caption line in scene px — .station-caption's own
  // font-size (7) x its line-height (1.3). Named once so the F5 clamp can
  // be DERIVED from whichever why-box is in play instead of hard-coding a
  // line count beside a height that can move independently of it.
  var NOTEBOOK_CAPTION_LINE_PX = 7 * 1.3;

  var STATION_NOTEBOOK = { view: 0, month: '' };
  var STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM = {
    pageX: { left: 18, right: 202 },  // page interior left edges, scene px
    date: { dx: 8, y: 24, w: 144, h: 10 },
    title: { dx: 8, y: 38, w: 144, h: 20 },
    photo: { dx: 44, y: 62, w: 72, h: 54 },
    whyImage: { dx: 8, y: 122, w: 144, h: 46 },
    // 26.87 UAT F5: was h:46, which ended at y=108 on a page whose nav sits
    // at y=196 — it clipped mid-word with nearly HALF the page still blank.
    // 108 runs the why down to y=170, stopping just above the bottom-left
    // decoration at y=178. The IMAGE variant keeps its 46: it starts at
    // y=122 under the polaroid and has no room to grow.
    whyText: { dx: 8, y: 62, w: 144, h: 108 },
    deco: { tr: { dx: 132, y: 8 }, bl: { dx: 8, y: 178 } },
    invite: { x: 96, y: 92, w: 192, h: 32 },
    monthLabel: { x: 48, y: 38, w: 104, h: 12 },
    navPrev: { x: 26, y: 38, w: 16, h: 12 },
    navNext: { x: 158, y: 38, w: 16, h: 12 },
    grid: { x0: 30, y0: 56, cellW: 18, cellH: 14, pitchX: 21, pitchY: 17 },
    prev: { x: 4, y: 196, w: 20, h: 16 },
    next: { x: 360, y: 196, w: 20, h: 16 }
  };

  // 26.91-07 (PART B) — the arrival trace page's two slots.
  //
  // BY REFERENCE, NEVER RE-TYPED. Part B introduces ZERO new scene-px
  // values: this declaration holds no numeric literal at all, because a
  // second copy of a slot is the drift this project keeps finding.
  //
  //   `line` is `whyText` (144 x 108 scene px) because the trace IS the
  //   page's prose and it belongs where a why goes. At .station-caption's
  //   7px / 1.3 that is 11 lines of line box = 1,584 px, against a worst
  //   composable line MEASURED at 497 px (24 CJK at the cap) — about a
  //   THIRD of the slot. The 26.9 `invite` box (192 x 32) would have put
  //   the same worst case at 86% of a slot that clips SILENTLY
  //   (.station-caption carries overflow: hidden), which is why the live
  //   backstop G-B-trace measures it rather than trusting the margin.
  //
  //   `day` is `date` VERBATIM, `h:10` exception and all, because dates
  //   land where dates land. A second spelling at h:12 would be a new
  //   value bought for nothing — and this page's day label is
  //   LOAD-BEARING: it is the answer to *why am I on a page for a day the
  //   calendar shows as empty?*
  // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): the trace geometry, RETAINED — by
  // reference, zero numeric literals. Its consumer paintTracePage is off the
  // render path; neither is deleted.
  var NB_TRACE_GEOM = {
    day: STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM.date,
    // ⚠ `line` RETAINED DELIBERATELY — 26.91-10 (F-3). paintTracePage no
    // longer paints a sentence, so this slot currently places nothing. It
    // is kept whole for Phase 26.95, which re-wires the trace if it
    // returns, and it is the slot test_live_render.cjs's 91f / G-B-trace
    // block measures the RETAINED composer's worst-case line against — a
    // measurement that never depended on the app rendering that line, which
    // is why that block is left byte-unchanged by this wave. Deleting this
    // member, or narrowing that gate so it passes with no surface to guard,
    // is forbidden.
    line: STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM.whyText
  };

  // Inline scene-px placement for NON-interactive page furniture — no
  // shared hover/focus chrome ever dresses a thing that is not a
  // button (the unlit calendar cells ride this same helper; any shared
  // affordance class on them would be a law-3 distinction).
  function placeNotebookInert(el, x, y, w, h) {
    el.style.position = 'absolute';
    el.style.left = 'calc(' + x + ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
    el.style.top = 'calc(' + y + ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
    el.style.width = 'calc(' + w + ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
    el.style.height = 'calc(' + h + ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
  }

  function renderNotebookStation() {
    var scene = $('station-scene');
    if (!scene) { return; }
    // 26.9-03: a raise always lands in READING mode. Closing the station
    // is the one exit and there is no second door, so the flag must not
    // survive a close-and-reopen — and an idempotent re-raise leaves one
    // mode and one set of nodes, never two.
    NBDESIGN = false;
    NB_SEL = null;               // 26.9-05: nothing is selected on a raise
    NB_PEN = false;              // 26.9-07: and the pen is down on a raise
    NB_WRITE = false;            // 26.91-02: and so is `write`. A raise
                                 // always lands with NO tool armed.
    NB_PEN_GROUP = null;
    document.body.classList.remove('nb-design');
    // 26.91-18: DISARM PATH 3 OF 3 — a raise lands with NO tool armed, so it
    // lands with no armed cursor. Same obligation as the mode class beside it.
    document.body.classList.remove('nb-armed', 'nb-armed-pen', 'nb-armed-write');
    // idempotent: a re-raise replaces the sub-scene, never stacks a
    // second set (the renderShelfStation posture).
    while (scene.firstChild) { scene.removeChild(scene.firstChild); }
    var bg = document.createElement('img');
    // 26.8-05 (D-14 own skin): the station PREFERS the contracted
    // notebook-station.png (384x216, cream pages warmer/rougher than
    // the journal's — the different-book read) and swaps ONCE to the
    // journal's spread frame when the art is absent — the UI-SPEC
    // pre-declared freeze fallback, taken deliberately this wave
    // (art-last, never blocking: the differentiation read slips, the
    // machinery is intact either way). When the sprite lands on disk
    // this fallback simply stops firing — zero code changes at art
    // time. Behavior is identical on either route.
    bg.src = 'assets/room/notebook-station.png';
    bg.addEventListener('error', function onBgErr() {
      bg.removeEventListener('error', onBgErr);
      bg.src = 'assets/room/spread-frame-book.png?v=26.5-08b';
    });
    bg.alt = '';
    bg.className = 'station-bg';
    scene.appendChild(bg);
    // 26.999 night (her ruling): she is looking at the pages RIGHT NOW —
    // stamp the device's seen-mark and put the plain covers back on both
    // windows at once, so the rose bookmark never lingers on a book she
    // has just read (no refetch: the clearing is the point, not a probe).
    stampNotebookSeen();
    applyNotebookMark(false);
    // 26.9-03: the decoration day map is fetched WITH the ledger, so the
    // record is in hand before the station paints — the editor has no
    // in-flight state to design, by construction (UI-SPEC E3/E5 loading).
    // Its read is fail-open: loadDecorations resolves to {} on any
    // failure, so an unreachable store is an undecorated book, never an
    // error surface, and Promise.all cannot be rejected by it.
    Promise.all([apiGet('/api/items'), apiGet('/api/blessings'),
      loadDecorations()])
      .then(function (rs) {
        var itemsRes = rs[0];
        var ledgerRes = rs[1];
        // no store snapshot → no fence resolution → the drawn book
        // stays blank, quietly: the fence never renders an entry it
        // could not re-resolve (law 5 fails safe).
        if (!itemsRes.ok) { return; }
        ROOM.items = (itemsRes.data && itemsRes.data.items) || {};
        var meta = (itemsRes.data && itemsRes.data.meta) || {};
        ROOM.meta = meta;
        // RV-1 hydration exactly as the journal station: a station-
        // first open must never overwrite the stored cycle.
        SHELF.cycle = meta.cycle || { number: 1, shown_ids: [] };
        if (!Array.isArray(SHELF.cycle.shown_ids)) {
          SHELF.cycle.shown_ids = [];
        }
        SHELF.filters = Array.isArray(meta.filters) ? meta.filters : [];
        SHELF.coverOffers = (meta.cover_offers &&
          typeof meta.cover_offers === 'object') ? meta.cover_offers : {};
        // the ledger reads fail-open (D-11): the server GET already
        // answers the empty wrapper on an unreadable file; an off-
        // shape or missed read lands the same way here — the empty
        // book, never an error surface.
        var ledger = (ledgerRes.ok && ledgerRes.data &&
          Array.isArray(ledgerRes.data.blessings))
          ? { blessings: ledgerRes.data.blessings } : { blessings: [] };
        var entries = packBlessingsToc(ledger, ROOM.items, SHELF.filters,
          StudyCore.guardSurface);
        var roster = blessingsMonthRoster(entries);
        if (roster.indexOf(STATION_NOTEBOOK.month) === -1) {
          STATION_NOTEBOOK.month = roster.length ? roster[0] : '';
        }
        paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
      }).catch(function () {
        // unreachable room: the drawn book stays blank, quietly
        // (fail-open presentation).
      });
  }

  // D-C — the spread list the in-book ‹/› step across.
  //
  // 26.91-10 (F-1) — D-C's NAVIGATION CLAUSE IS AMENDED, DELIBERATELY, ON
  // THE AUTHORITY OF THE OWNER'S UAT FINDING F-1 (26.91-UAT.md).
  //
  //   BEFORE: *the DAY is the navigation unit* — one press of `›` moved to
  //   the neighbouring day, and a day holding several blessings chunked
  //   into consecutive same-day spreads two pages at a time.
  //
  //   AFTER: *the PAGE is the navigation unit* — one press of `›` moves to
  //   the neighbouring PAGE, which may be the same day. Her design reads
  //   *pick a date on the left, read it on the right*, and a multi-page day
  //   is paged ON THE RIGHT ONLY, so a spread now holds exactly ONE page.
  //   That is also what D-02 buys her read forward: import days join the
  //   prev/next page flip, in date order.
  //
  // The month grid is no longer something a spread CARRIES. It is the
  // permanent left half of every spread and is painted unconditionally by
  // paintNotebookSpread, so the spread-level grid discriminator this
  // function used to emit is REMOVED rather than left constant-true. A
  // field that can only ever hold one value carries no information, and
  // shipping a new one in the same phase that deletes an old one would be
  // incoherent.
  //
  // D-C'S TWO OTHER PROPERTIES SURVIVE UNTOUCHED, and they are the ones
  // that matter for law 5:
  //   * PURE — derives only from the already-guarded entries and their
  //     already-guarded arrivals, never from a raw scan.
  //   * RE-RESOLVES THROUGH THE FENCE ON EVERY PAINT — the set is built
  //     inside the paint, never cached at module scope, so a day whose last
  //     surviving entry was just fenced silently leaves navigation.
  // An empty day is still never a spread at all (law 3 — walking through
  // unused days would foreground absence).
  //
  // 26.91-07 (PART B): it also takes the day roster's ALREADY-GUARDED
  // ARRIVALS, because the trace page is a page and pages are built here.
  function buildBlessingSpreads(entries, dayRoster, arrivals) {
    var list = entries || [];
    var roster = dayRoster || [];
    var arrived = arrivals || [];
    var spreads = [];
    for (var di = 0; di < roster.length; di++) {
      var d = roster[di];
      var de = [];
      for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
        if (list[i].dayLabel === d.day) { de.push(list[i]); }
      }
      // 26.91-07 (PART B): THE TRACE IS A PAGE, and it carries a
      // DISCRIMINATING FIELD (`trace: true`) plus that day's already-guarded
      // arrival entries, so paintNotebookSpread branches on the PAGE and
      // never on a day-level `lit` flag. The two coincide today; a
      // page-level condition survives a future day shape that does not, and
      // wave 6 deliberately demoted `lit` to an ATTRIBUTE OF AN ENTRY —
      // re-coupling the two concepts on the render path would undo that.
      //
      // 26.91-10 (F-3) — THE TRACE PAGE IS MINTED ONLY FOR A DAY THAT
      // CONTRIBUTED NO SURVIVING BLESSING PAGE. Wave 7 appended it as the
      // day's LAST page in both cases, which was right while it carried a
      // composed sentence. F-3 takes that sentence off (D-03: the one
      // glanceable line this page was built to carry is what she asked to
      // have removed, and there is no smaller version of it that is not a
      // version of it), so on a LIT day the page would now be a bare date
      // wedged between two blessing pages — a page with no reason to exist.
      //
      // WHAT STAYS IS WAVE 6'S STRUCTURAL WORK, AND IT STAYS WHOLE: an
      // import-only day is still in the day roster, still openable, and
      // still joined to the prev/next page flip in date order. Only the
      // sentence went; the day did not.
      var dayArrivals = [];
      for (i = 0; i < arrived.length; i++) {
        if (arrived[i].dayLabel === d.day) { dayArrivals.push(arrived[i]); }
      }
      // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): THE TRACE-MINT BRANCH. The
      // owner ruling of 2026-08-09 (A-14) removed the `arrivals` ARGUMENT
      // from the shipped call, so on the render path `arrived` is empty,
      // this branch mints nothing and the shipped spread list holds NO trace
      // page at all. The branch is NOT deleted — the three-argument call
      // still mints one, and that is what distinguishes DORMANT from BROKEN.
      // GATES: (G-17/dormant) asserts BOTH halves in the SAME RUN — no trace
      // page on the shipped call, AND this branch still minting one when the
      // builder is driven directly with arrivals.
      if (!de.length && dayArrivals.length) {
        de.push({ trace: true, day: d.day, monthKey: d.monthKey,
          arrivals: dayArrivals });
      }
      var made = 0;
      // ONE PAGE PER SPREAD, in the shipped newest-first entry order, days
      // in the shipped roster order. The two-page chunking is GONE, not
      // disabled: her design has no second reading in which two days — or
      // two pages — share a spread.
      for (var p = 0; p < de.length; p++) {
        spreads.push({ day: d.day, monthKey: d.monthKey, pages: [de[p]] });
        made++;
      }
      // 26.91-06 (D-01/D-02): an IMPORT-ONLY day holds zero surviving
      // blessings, so the chunking loop above emits nothing for it and the
      // day would be silently unreachable — present in the roster, absent
      // from the flip. It gets its one page here, so the prev/next page
      // flip really does step onto it in date order.
      //
      // 26.91-07: on the SHIPPED call this branch no longer fires, because
      // a day is in the roster only if it holds a blessing or an arrival
      // and an arrival now mints the trace page above. It is KEPT and
      // DRIVEN (6c-d) rather than deleted, for the two-argument call: a
      // caller that omits `arrivals` still gets a reachable day. Unreachable
      // defensive code that no mutation can redden is this project's named
      // defect class, so the two-argument path is exercised by name.
      //
      // 26.91-10: DELIBERATELY RETAINED through the one-page rewrite. It is
      // the only producer of a page-less spread, and paintNotebookSpread's
      // `NB_PLACE = null` clear is what keeps the tray's taps inert on one.
      // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): THE PAGE-LESS-SPREAD BRANCH,
      // deliberately kept for the THIRD wave running. It is the only
      // producer of a page-less spread and paintNotebookSpread's
      // `NB_PLACE = null` clear is what keeps the tray's taps inert on one.
      // Unreachable on the shipped call for a NEW reason after 2026-08-09 —
      // the roster is now the lit-day set, and every lit day contributes at
      // least one blessing page — but reachable and DRIVEN on the
      // two-argument path (6c-d). Unreachable defensive code that no
      // mutation can redden is this project's named defect class, so the
      // path that reaches it is exercised by name rather than assumed.
      if (made === 0) {
        spreads.push({ day: d.day, monthKey: d.monthKey, pages: [] });
      }
    }
    return spreads;
  }

  // Paint ONE spread + the day-step buttons. Idempotent per flip:
  // fixtures are cleared and re-painted; the background art stays put.
  // All flips are taps — nothing self-advances.
  function paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster) {
    // 26.9-03: the repaint closure for this spread — the shipped refill
    // idiom. A mode toggle, a placement or a committed line re-renders
    // from state already in hand; nothing here re-fetches.
    NB_REPAINT = function () {
      paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
    };
    Array.prototype.slice.call(scene.children).forEach(function (el) {
      if (!el.classList.contains('station-bg')) { scene.removeChild(el); }
    });
    // 26.9-07: bind or RELEASE the pen's one scene listener. Called here,
    // before the zero-blessings early return below, so that the release
    // happens on every path a repaint can take — including the one where
    // the last surviving blessing of the open day was just fenced.
    attachPenCapture(scene);
    var g = STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM;
    function place(el, r) {
      el.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      el.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      el.style.setProperty('--w', String(r.w));
      el.style.setProperty('--h', String(r.h));
    }
    if (!entries.length) {
      // zero blessings: the invite line in the spread interior and NO
      // calendar grid (a bare all-unlit month reads as a gap — silence
      // instead; law 3). Must invite, never guilt.
      var invite = document.createElement('div');
      invite.className = 'caption-hand station-caption';
      invite.style.textAlign = 'center';
      invite.textContent = 'a book for what you welcome. bless something, and it will keep a page here.';
      place(invite, g.invite);
      scene.appendChild(invite);
      return;
    }
    // The lit-day set + its day-aligned spreads re-derive from the
    // ALREADY-GUARDED entries on EVERY paint (law 5 P0) — never cached at
    // module scope, so a day whose last surviving blessing was just fenced
    // silently leaves navigation (its spread simply stops existing).
    //
    // ===== 26.91-17 — SC-3 RETIRED BY OWNER RULING, 2026-08-09 ===========
    //
    // HER RULING (26.91-CONTEXT.md A-14, UAT §F-15), severity verbatim:
    // *"quite critical … it is hard to notice if I am reading the date I
    // select or not."* Her design: **remove the arrow when it is the last
    // page of the day** — the flip becomes DAY-SCOPED (see the two doors
    // below). This is implemented here, never re-decided here; the record
    // landed in plan 16, before this byte moved.
    //
    // THE RULING IS TWO CHANGES AND THEY SHIP IN ONE COMMIT. Day-scoping
    // the flip ALONE would leave every import-only day minted and reachable
    // by nothing: an import-only day holds exactly one spread, so it has no
    // in-day neighbour to step to, and its calendar cell is unlit and
    // untappable by her own D-01. Measured live in plan 16 rather than
    // argued — four spreads of nine, 44% of the array, with no door in and
    // no door out. So the roster call changes with the predicate, and the
    // notebook's day-set EQUALS the calendar's lit-day set again. D-C's
    // original equality is RESTORED, not re-derived.
    //
    // REMOVED FROM THE RENDER PATH (four things, and only these four):
    //   1. the arrival pack call — packArrivalDays(...) from this paint
    //   2. the roster promotion — the `arrivals` argument to
    //      blessingsDayRoster
    //   3. the trace-page mint — the `arrivals` argument to
    //      buildBlessingSpreads
    //   4. the trace page's paint — paintTracePage's reachability from here
    //
    // RETAINED WHOLE AND DORMANT FOR PHASE 26.95 — nothing below is
    // deleted, disarmed or retired, and each carries a retention notice at
    // its OWN site naming 26.95 and the gate that proves it can still fire:
    //   packArrivalDays and its verbatim guard contract · blessingsDayRoster's
    //   promote path · buildBlessingSpreads' trace-mint branch · its
    //   page-less-spread branch · paintTracePage · composeArrivalTrace ·
    //   TRACE_NEVER_NAME / TRACE_SOURCE_PHRASE / TRACE_FOLDER_CAP ·
    //   NB_TRACE_GEOM · and every gate over all of them.
    //
    // WHY RETENTION IS RIGHT HERE AND WAS *NOT* RIGHT FOR THE DEAD FIELD
    // WAVE 13 DELETED: `viewOfPage` and the cell's third property described
    // a model that NO LONGER EXISTED AT ALL — a second, wrong derivation of
    // something the app had stopped doing. These symbols describe a model
    // that still exists and has a NAMED FUTURE CONSUMER (Phase 26.95, which
    // inherits part B whole). Dead code is deleted; dormant code with a
    // consumer is retained and gated. The distinction is the reason, not a
    // preference.
    //
    // THE ARRIVAL PACK CALL IS REMOVED RATHER THAN LEFT UNREAD. A call
    // whose result no consumer reads is the dead-value shape wave 13 has
    // just finished removing, and keeping it "to keep the fence warm" would
    // be that shape wearing a good intention. The law-5 folder fence stays
    // ARMED — it now guards no live surface, exactly as F-3 did with the
    // composer, and it is proven still able to FIRE by mutation inside the
    // packer (G-F3/c and the G-B2 fence pair). A fence nobody can redden is
    // not a fence.
    //
    // LAW 3 IS UNTOUCHED AND SURVIVES THIS MORE STRONGLY, NOT LESS: an
    // unlit calendar cell stays painted and untappable, `lit` still means
    // exactly *you welcomed something*, and no absence copy, placeholder,
    // count or third calendar state is authored anywhere for the pages that
    // leave. F-3 removed a sentence rather than shortening it for the same
    // reason.
    var dayRoster = blessingsDayRoster(entries);
    var spreads = buildBlessingSpreads(entries, dayRoster);
    if (STATION_NOTEBOOK.view >= spreads.length) {
      STATION_NOTEBOOK.view = spreads.length - 1;
    }
    if (STATION_NOTEBOOK.view < 0) { STATION_NOTEBOOK.view = 0; }
    var v = STATION_NOTEBOOK.view;
    var spread = spreads[v];
    // 26.9-03 (D-13): every write on this spread is scoped to ITS day —
    // the canvas is the DAY (D-05) while the ownership key stays the PAGE
    // (D-06). A-3's day-scoped reset and the page-scoped storage key are
    // NOT in conflict and must not be "reconciled" by moving the key.
    // 26.9-06 (D-15): THE STACK CLEARS ON DAY CHANGE. A day is the
    // notebook's unit of arrangement — the reset is day-scoped and a
    // snapshot is one day's record — so a stack that survived a page flip
    // would offer to undo an act on a day she is no longer looking at.
    if (NB_DAY !== spread.day) {
      NB_UNDO.length = 0;
      NB_REDO.length = 0;
      NB_RESET_ARMED = false;
    }
    NB_DAY = spread.day;
    // 26.9-04: cleared here and set by whichever branch paints a blessing
    // page, so a spread with no page (unreachable today, but the tin does
    // not know that) leaves the tray's taps inert rather than landing a
    // mark on the page she was looking at three flips ago.
    NB_PLACE = null;
    // 26.91-07 (PART B): ONE dispatch for *what kind of page is this*,
    // rather than the same `if (page.trace)` written three times beside
    // three appendChild sites. A TRACE PAGE IS NOT A BLESSING PAGE: it
    // paints two inert nodes, it takes NO `NB_PLACE` target and it carries
    // no decorations — even on a lit day. `placeX0` is null on the one
    // page slot that has never taken a placement target (the right-hand
    // page of a two-page spread), so the shipped asymmetry is stated once
    // instead of being implied by which branch happens to assign.
    function paintOnePage(page, side, placeX0) {
      if (page.trace) {
        paintTracePage(scene, page, side);
        return;
      }
      if (placeX0 !== null) {
        NB_PLACE = { itemId: page.itemId, x0: placeX0 };
      }
      paintBlessingPage(scene, page, side,
        ROOM.items, SHELF.filters, StudyCore.guardSurface);
    }
    // 26.91-10 (F-1), HER DESIGN, VERBATIM AND BINDING: *the calendar lives
    // permanently on the LEFT page; selecting a date fills the RIGHT page; a
    // day holding more than one thing is paged on the right only; the
    // calendar never leaves.* Reviewing a day is always **pick a date on the
    // left, read it on the right**.
    //
    // So the grid is painted on EVERY spread, unconditionally, before the
    // page. The two-branch fork it used to sit inside is gone, along with
    // the spread-level grid discriminator that fork read.
    //
    // ⚠ The removed member is named BY CONCEPT here, never by its literal
    // token. This block's own gate is a region-scoped count of that token
    // at zero, and a comment that quotes the token its gate forbids is a
    // self-invalidating gate — which is how a negative grep in this repo
    // has failed before.
    //
    // ⚠ THE BLAST RADIUS OF A PAINT FAILURE GROWS HERE, AND THAT IS
    // ACCEPTED RATHER THAN OVERLOOKED. Before, a throw inside
    // paintNotebookCalendar broke ONE spread and every other spread still
    // painted. Now it breaks EVERY spread. The alternative is a conditional
    // calendar, and a conditional calendar IS the F-1 defect. The mitigation
    // is that G-F1 asserts a real day-cell count at EVERY spread index over
    // the executed render, so a paint that throws or draws an empty
    // container reddens at index 0 rather than reaching her.
    paintNotebookCalendar(scene, entries, roster, spreads);
    // ONE page, always on the right, always at origin 192 — which was
    // already the right page's placement origin on the shipped spread-0
    // branch, so NB_PLACE's `x0` is now a single value rather than one of
    // three.
    if (spread.pages[0]) { paintOnePage(spread.pages[0], 'right', 192); }
    // 26.91-17 (F-15, her ruling of 2026-08-09): THE FLIP IS DAY-SCOPED.
    // The calendar chooses the DAY; the doors turn PAGES INSIDE IT. A press
    // can no longer change the date, so F-1's promise — *pick a date on the
    // left, read it on the right* — holds on EVERY press rather than on the
    // first one.
    //
    // ONLY THE PREDICATE CHANGES. The shipped neighbour-exists door pattern
    // is preserved exactly: an arrow renders only when its neighbour
    // exists, there is no disabled control and no end-of-book copy, and a
    // day holding ONE page renders NEITHER door. That pattern is what makes
    // the day boundary legible without inventing a visual state — running
    // out of arrows IS the signal, and it is the same signal the ends of
    // the book have always given.
    //
    // BOTH DOORS ARE SCOPED, NOT ONLY THE FORWARD ONE. Her ruling names
    // *the arrow*, singular, and the forward door is the one she pressed —
    // but scoping only that one would leave the backward door able to walk
    // her onto a different date, which is the same defect facing the other
    // way. The symmetric reading is taken deliberately; it is stated in the
    // plan's SUMMARY and put to her at the seal rather than assumed to be
    // invisible.
    //
    // The target spread's month assignment is DERIVED from the target
    // spread rather than re-typed, so it survives any future widening of
    // this predicate. Under day-scoping it is necessarily the same month —
    // that is an observation, not an invitation to delete a correct derived
    // assignment.
    if (v > 0 && spreads[v - 1].day === spread.day) {
      var prev = document.createElement('button');
      prev.type = 'button';
      prev.className = 'station-fixture station-flip';
      // 26.91-17 (Rule 1): the accessible name said `previous day` and that
      // is now FALSE — under day-scoping this control can never change the
      // date. An accessible name that describes a behaviour the control no
      // longer has is the same shape (91c) closes on the visible side: a
      // control claiming to be something it is not. Corrected to what it
      // does. This is NOT replacement copy for what left the page — nothing
      // speaks absence, no count and no placeholder is authored.
      prev.setAttribute('aria-label', 'previous page');
      prev.textContent = '‹';
      place(prev, g.prev);
      prev.addEventListener('click', function () {
        STATION_NOTEBOOK.view = v - 1;
        STATION_NOTEBOOK.month = spreads[v - 1].monthKey;
        paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
      });
      scene.appendChild(prev);
    }
    if (v + 1 < spreads.length && spreads[v + 1].day === spread.day) {
      var next = document.createElement('button');
      next.type = 'button';
      next.className = 'station-fixture station-flip';
      // 26.91-17 (Rule 1): same correction as the backward door above.
      next.setAttribute('aria-label', 'next page');
      next.textContent = '›';
      place(next, g.next);
      next.addEventListener('click', function () {
        STATION_NOTEBOOK.view = v + 1;
        STATION_NOTEBOOK.month = spreads[v + 1].monthKey;
        paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
      });
      scene.appendChild(next);
    }
    // 26.9-03 (D-13): THE DOOR. One quiet row on the open spread, in the
    // shipped .station-caption-add register — the same lowercase
    // underlined invitation every other quiet row in this app uses. The
    // second implementation of a quiet row would be the bug.
    //
    // Room design mode wins outright (D-14): with DESIGN on, the station
    // cannot be open at all, so this row simply never renders — the guard
    // is belt to the shipped station-click handler's suspenders.
    //
    // 26.91-07 — UI-SPEC Open Decision #2, OWNER RULING `read-only-import-
    // day`, taken 2026-08-07 (26.91-CONTEXT.md A-9): the row renders ONLY
    // when THIS SPREAD holds a page that can actually be decorated. On an
    // import-only day's spread the band shows `‹` and `›` and NOTHING ELSE.
    // That is honest — it is a page you can only read — and a control that
    // appears and does nothing is the F-6 shape, in the phase built to fix
    // F-6.
    //
    // THE CONDITION IS ON THE SPREAD'S CONTENTS, NEVER ON A DAY-LEVEL `lit`
    // FLAG. The two coincide today and a page-level condition survives a
    // future day shape that does not — and wave 6 deliberately demoted
    // `lit` to an attribute of an ENTRY, so re-coupling the two concepts
    // here would undo that on the render path.
    //
    // WHY the decision is what it is, recorded because the argument that
    // was written down first was FALSE: `server.py` does NOT forbid
    // decorating a page-less day (`validate_decorations` enforces only a
    // non-empty string; the ownership-key line above it is a COMMENT). The
    // reason that carries is that there is no defensible ownership key for
    // a page with no item — D-06 keys a decoration on the BLESSING's
    // itemId. HER ACCEPTED COST, recorded: import days become the only
    // pages in the notebook that cannot be decorated.
    var decoratable = false;
    for (var pi = 0; pi < spread.pages.length; pi++) {
      if (spread.pages[pi] && !spread.pages[pi].trace) { decoratable = true; }
    }
    if (!DESIGN && decoratable) {
      var arrange = document.createElement('button');
      arrange.type = 'button';
      // 26.9-06 (Rule 1, a real defect): .station-caption-add alone is
      // `position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0` with no top/left/size from
      // --x/--y/--w/--h — it was written for a row pinned to the FOOT OF A
      // CAPTION CELL, and this row is appended straight to the scene. So
      // the slot the painter sets never reached the screen and the row sat
      // at the scene's bottom-left corner. .station-nb-row is the one rule
      // that makes a band row honour its slot, and every band row wears it.
      arrange.className =
        'station-caption-add station-nb-row station-arrange-row';
      arrange.textContent = NBDESIGN ? 'done arranging' : 'arrange this day';
      arrange.setAttribute('aria-label',
        NBDESIGN ? 'done arranging' : 'arrange this day');
      // 26.9-06: the row moves to the band's EXIT slot while arranging. The
      // entry slot (140-244) overlaps both the reset row (100-176) and the
      // exit row (184-280), so the reading row and the arranging row cannot
      // share a slot once the arranging controls exist beside them.
      place(arrange, NBDESIGN ? NB_BAND.exit : NB_BAND.entry);
      arrange.addEventListener('click', function () {
        setNotebookDesign(!NBDESIGN);
      });
      scene.appendChild(arrange);
    }
    // 26.9-06: the band's arranging controls (undo/redo). Painted before the
    // tin so the tray, which opens above the band, stays topmost.
    renderNotebookBand(scene);
    // 26.9-04 (D-16): the tin, at its band slot, and the tray when it is
    // open — in-scene, above the tin, never the right-dock panel. Painted
    // LAST so the tray sits over the page furniture; it renders only inside
    // the notebook's design mode.
    renderTinTray(scene, ROOM.items, SHELF.filters);
  }

  // ONE auto-composed blessing page (D-12): date line, the title as
  // the re-open button, the polaroid for image items, the why in her
  // handwriting register, one seeded decoration mark. Node-by-node,
  // textContent ONLY for every ledger-derived string (Suite-2 seam
  // discipline); the item's content never renders on a page.
  // 26.9-04: items / filters / guard are threaded THROUGH to her layer,
  // unchanged, so the render-time fence is an argument at every level
  // rather than a global reached for at the bottom. packBlessingsToc's
  // contract, applied one layer deeper.
  function paintBlessingPage(scene, entry, side, items, filters, guard) {
    var g = STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM;
    var x0 = g.pageX[side];
    // 26.9-09: BEFORE the photo decision below, and only while arranging.
    // Entering the mode is what makes the photograph a mark she can move;
    // nothing moves at the moment of promotion.
    ensurePagePhoto(entry, side);
    function place(el, r) {
      el.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      el.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      el.style.setProperty('--w', String(r.w));
      el.style.setProperty('--h', String(r.h));
    }
    // the date line — the ledger's own day (D-11), quiet chrome.
    var date = document.createElement('div');
    date.className = 'caption-hand station-caption';
    date.style.fontSize = 'calc(6px * var(--k))';
    date.style.color = 'var(--ink-soft)';
    date.textContent = entry.dayLabel;
    place(date, {
      x: x0 + g.date.dx, y: g.date.y, w: g.date.w, h: g.date.h
    });
    scene.appendChild(date);
    // the title IS the re-open button — markOpened + the shipped
    // verbatim spread (D-15 re-open; law 4: content opens there, never
    // here). Chrome truncation: up to 2 lines, then ellipsis.
    var title = document.createElement('button');
    title.type = 'button';
    title.className = 'station-fixture station-toc-line caption-hand';
    title.setAttribute('data-id', entry.itemId);
    title.setAttribute('aria-label', entry.title || 'a blessed thing');
    title.style.whiteSpace = 'normal';
    title.style.display = '-webkit-box';
    title.style.webkitBoxOrient = 'vertical';
    title.style.webkitLineClamp = '2';
    title.textContent = entry.title || 'a blessed thing';
    place(title, {
      x: x0 + g.title.dx, y: g.title.y, w: g.title.w, h: g.title.h
    });
    title.addEventListener('click', function () {
      // 26.9-03 (T3-3): while arranging, clicks arrange — they never
      // open. Belt to the pointer-events rule's suspenders, matching the
      // room's own `if (DESIGN) { return; }` posture exactly.
      if (NBDESIGN) { return; }
      openContainerItem(entry.itemId);
    });
    scene.appendChild(title);
    // the polaroid — image items only; the photo displays whole
    // (object-fit contain via .station-photo), never cropped, no
    // filter, no pixelation (content imagery, law 4). Text items
    // simply omit the slot.
    //
    // 26.9-09 (D-01's other half): ...UNLESS the polaroid has been promoted
    // to a record, in which case the decoration painter draws it and this
    // slot must not draw a second copy. Everything INSIDE this block is
    // byte-unchanged; the condition is the whole edit. On a reset day
    // livePagePhoto answers null, so the pinned slot paints again and the
    // reset restores the page in full.
    if (entry.isImage && !livePagePhoto(entry.dayLabel, entry.itemId)) {
      var photo = document.createElement('div');
      photo.className = 'station-photo';
      photo.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      placeNotebookInert(photo, x0 + g.photo.dx, g.photo.y,
        g.photo.w, g.photo.h);
      // the shipped album-cell img pattern exactly — escapeAttr at the
      // sink (Suite 2 seam evidence).
      photo.innerHTML = '<img loading="lazy" src="/lib/' +
        escapeAttr(encodeURIComponent(entry.itemId)) + '" alt="">';
      scene.appendChild(photo);
    }
    // the why — ledger chrome in her handwriting register, capped by
    // the cell's own height (~5 lines, overflow hidden — the why is
    // chrome, not item content; law 4 untouched). The librarian's whys
    // alone carry the quiet suffix; user and default whys carry NO
    // attribution.
    var why = document.createElement('div');
    why.className = 'caption-hand station-caption';
    why.textContent = entry.why;
    if (entry.author === 'librarian') {
      var suffix = document.createElement('span');
      suffix.style.color = 'var(--ink-soft)';
      suffix.textContent = ' — the librarian';
      why.appendChild(suffix);
    }
    var wy = entry.isImage ? g.whyImage : g.whyText;
    // 26.87 UAT F5, the second half. Growing the box is not enough — a why
    // longer than ANY box still has to end somewhere, and raw
    // `overflow: hidden` ends it mid-word with no signal that anything was
    // cut. The title above already solves this with a line clamp; the why
    // reuses that idiom rather than inventing a second one, so both page
    // elements truncate the same visible way.
    //
    // THE CLAMP IS DERIVED FROM THE BOX, never typed twice: a hand-written
    // line count is a second source of truth that silently disagrees the
    // first time either geometry moves. NOTEBOOK_CAPTION_LINE_PX mirrors
    // .station-caption's own font-size x line-height (7 x 1.3).
    why.style.whiteSpace = 'normal';
    why.style.display = '-webkit-box';
    why.style.webkitBoxOrient = 'vertical';
    why.style.webkitLineClamp =
      String(Math.max(1, Math.floor(wy.h / NOTEBOOK_CAPTION_LINE_PX)));
    place(why, { x: x0 + wy.dx, y: wy.y, w: wy.w, h: wy.h });
    scene.appendChild(why);
    // ONE seeded decoration mark — page chrome in a corner, never over
    // the photo, the title, or the why (D-12). 26.8-05 (sprite pass):
    // the marks render from notebook-decor.png — ONE horizontal 3-cell
    // sheet [stamp | washi | candle], 12x12 scene px per cell, cropped
    // by background-position (an img child would be stretched full-
    // canvas by the .station-scene img rule, so the crop rides a div
    // background). pickBlessingDecoration is untouched: the pick still
    // draws among exactly 3 marks, so the same page wears the same
    // mark + corner forever — re-opens stayed identical across the art
    // landing, exactly as the recorded fallback route promised. The
    // candle mark (cut on the CSS route) renders now that its sprite
    // exists.
    var deco = pickBlessingDecoration(entry.itemId, entry.ms);
    var decoIdx = { stamp: 0, washi: 1, candle: 2 };
    var mark = document.createElement('div');
    mark.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    var corner = g.deco[deco.corner];
    placeNotebookInert(mark, x0 + corner.dx, corner.y, 12, 12);
    mark.style.backgroundImage = 'url("assets/room/notebook-decor.png")';
    mark.style.backgroundSize =
      'calc(36 * var(--k) * 1px) calc(12 * var(--k) * 1px)';
    mark.style.backgroundPosition =
      'calc(' + (decoIdx[deco.mark] * -12) + ' * var(--k) * 1px) 0';
    mark.style.imageRendering = 'pixelated';
    scene.appendChild(mark);
    // 26.9-03: her own layer, painted LAST so it sits above every piece of
    // page furniture above (explicit z-index does the real work; append
    // order alone is what Research Q4 measured as insufficient).
    paintPageDecorations(scene, entry, side, items, filters, guard);
  }

  // 26.91-07 (PART B) — THE ARRIVAL TRACE PAGE. Two inert nodes and
  // nothing else: the day label in the `date` slot and ONE composed
  // sentence in the `whyText` slot, both by reference through
  // NB_TRACE_GEOM.
  //
  // NEITHER NODE IS A BUTTON. Neither wears `.station-fixture`, neither
  // gets a hover ring and neither takes a listener — a shared affordance
  // class on a non-interactive element would be the same law-3 distinction
  // the unlit calendar cells already avoid.
  //
  // FOLDER NAMES ARE USER DATA ON A FRONT-FACING SURFACE, so every string
  // here goes through `textContent` — never `innerHTML`, never an
  // attribute-composed path. This page names a KIND and a PLACE and
  // nothing else: no person, no note title, no item id, no file path.
  //
  // THE PAGE CARRIES NO DECORATIONS, no `NB_PLACE` target and no `arrange`
  // affordance — even on a lit day, where the entry row DOES render
  // because that day holds a decoratable blessing page. `paintPageDecorations`
  // is deliberately NOT called from here (it has exactly one call site, in
  // paintBlessingPage, and that is the second of the two client-side blocks
  // that keep an import-only day un-decoratable).
  //
  // THE COMPOSER MAY ANSWER ''. It does so for a day with no surviving
  // arrivals, and then the sentence node is simply not appended: there is
  // no *the librarian brought in nothing* state and none is authored —
  // that sentence would be law-3 damage and a fence tell at once.
  // ⚠ RETAINED FOR PHASE 26.95 (26.91-17): OFF THE RENDER PATH, KEPT WHOLE.
  // Unreachable from paintNotebookSpread since the owner ruling of
  // 2026-08-09; retained for 26.95 exactly as F-3 shaped it — two inert nodes
  // (now one), textContent only, no button, no `.station-fixture`, no
  // paintPageDecorations, NB_PLACE stays null. GATES THAT PROVE IT STILL
  // FIRES: (G-F3/a2) drives it directly and asserts the almost-empty page;
  // (G-F3/b2) drives it directly and asserts F-3's ban still holds AT THE
  // PAINTER rather than merely at a call site that no longer exists.
  function paintTracePage(scene, page, side) {
    var g = STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM;
    var t = NB_TRACE_GEOM;
    var x0 = g.pageX[side];
    function place(el, r) {
      el.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      el.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      el.style.setProperty('--w', String(r.w));
      el.style.setProperty('--h', String(r.h));
    }
    // the day label — the same `date` slot and the same quiet chrome a
    // blessing page's date line wears. It is the authoritative answer to
    // *why am I on a page for a day the calendar shows as empty?*
    var day = document.createElement('div');
    day.className = 'caption-hand station-caption';
    day.style.fontSize = 'calc(6px * var(--k))';
    day.style.color = 'var(--ink-soft)';
    day.textContent = page.day;
    place(day, { x: x0 + t.day.dx, y: t.day.y, w: t.day.w, h: t.day.h });
    scene.appendChild(day);
    // 26.91-10 (F-3): AND NOTHING ELSE. The composed arrival sentence is
    // OFF the page by owner request — she already knows the librarian syncs
    // when she enters the room, so the line was redundant, and the need it
    // answered belongs to Phase 26.95.
    //
    // THIS IS THE ALMOST-EMPTY PAGE, DESIGNED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED: the
    // day label, in the same `date` slot and the same quiet chrome a
    // blessing page's date line wears, and that is the whole page.
    //
    // NOTHING REPLACES THE SENTENCE. No *the librarian brought in nothing*,
    // no *this day is quiet*, no placeholder art, no count, no empty-state
    // line. Such a sentence would be law-3 damage (it would speak absence)
    // and a law-5 fence tell (it would announce that something was held
    // back) in the same breath.
    //
    // composeArrivalTrace is NOT called from here any more. It is RETAINED,
    // still gated, still fenced — see its own retention notice.
  }

  // ---- the notebook's design mode (26.9-03, SRM-14-EXT-EDITOR) -------------
  //
  // A SECOND design scope, added ALONGSIDE the room's `DESIGN` rather than
  // promoting it to a three-valued scope. CONTEXT D-14 specifies mutual
  // exclusion and D-15 specifies a separate stack with reused key bindings;
  // together those are add-alongside, not promote.
  //
  // ACCEPTED DEBT, STATED: two booleans encode a three-valued state, so the
  // combination {DESIGN: true, NBDESIGN: true} is REPRESENTABLE even though
  // it is unreachable. Nothing in the type system forbids it. What makes
  // that debt safe rather than silent is a check that FAILS — the invariant
  // group in tests/test_blessings_notebook.cjs goes red the instant the two
  // modes could coexist, from BOTH directions.
  //
  // What would force a later promote to a real scope value, named now so
  // nobody has to rediscover it: (1) a third design scope — at three
  // booleans, eight states for four meanings, the boolean set IS the bug;
  // (2) any change that lets DESIGN be truthy while a station is open.
  //
  // D-13: entry is on the DAY, from the open spread. A deliberate departure
  // from 24.1's "entered deliberately from Manage" — the law amendment
  // holds in full (drag only inside an explicit design mode, locked by
  // default, never in the reading loop); only the door moves, because the
  // notebook is its own place and the moment of intent is while looking at
  // the day.

  var NBDESIGN = false;
  // The client-side day map: { '<dayLabel>': { reset, items[] } }. Loaded
  // once when the station raises, written one day at a time at pointerup.
  var DECORATIONS = {};
  // The repaint closure for the open spread — the shipped refill-closure
  // idiom. Set by paintNotebookSpread, so a mode toggle or a placement can
  // re-render without re-fetching anything.
  var NB_REPAINT = null;
  // The day the open spread belongs to; every write is scoped to it.
  var NB_DAY = '';
  var NB_TEXT_CAP = 80;              // the stored string's cap, end-to-end
  var NB_TEXT_BOX = { w: 72, h: 24 };  // the hand-text element's box
  // The SPREAD INTERIOR (UI-SPEC): x in [4, 380], y in [4, 190]. This is
  // the scene's interior box — what the chrome band spans (its width sum
  // identity `376 = 380 - 4 = NB_BOUNDS.x1 - NB_BOUNDS.x0` is stated at
  // NB_BAND), what the pen canvas takes its height from, and what
  // server.py's origin fence mirrors. IT IS UNCHANGED by 26.91-10 and must
  // stay the spread, because the band still spans the whole spread.
  var NB_BOUNDS = { x0: 4, x1: 380, y0: 4, y1: 190 };
  // The gutter — the right page's origin in spread coordinates. Already the
  // number paintNotebookSpread hands NB_PLACE and the number the placement
  // canvas offsets a right-hand page by; named once here so the mark bound
  // below cannot drift from it.
  var NB_GUTTER_X = 192;
  // ---- THE MARK CANVAS — 26.91-10, OWNER RULING `right-page-only` --------
  //
  // **HER CALL, taken 2026-08-08** (recorded verbatim and as HERS in
  // `26.91-CONTEXT.md` A-12, committed before any byte of this file moved):
  // *"Marks live on the right page only."*
  //
  // **26.9 D-05's gutter-straddle clause is RETIRED by that ruling.**
  // `26.9-CONTEXT.md:37` (restated at `26.9-UI-SPEC:531`) reads *"the design
  // unit is THE DAY … decorations may straddle the gutter"*. The emphasised
  // half is retired; the other half — **the design unit is THE DAY, one
  // shared canvas per day** — is UNTOUCHED and still binding.
  //
  // ⚠ **`26.9 D-05` IS WRITTEN WITH ITS PHASE ON PURPOSE, NEVER A BARE
  // `D-05`.** This phase has its OWN D-05 (`26.91-CONTEXT.md:94`, *the
  // naming fallback — an opaque hash must never reach the surface*), which
  // is a LIVE law-5 control that 26.91-10 RETAINS and proves ARMED. An
  // unqualified citation here would leave the next reader resolving it
  // against the wrong phase and reading a live control as retired.
  //
  // WHY: after F-1 the left half of every spread is the month grid — the
  // thing she navigates with, now visible from every day. A mark placed at
  // x < 192 would sit across the day numbers, and a sticker over a control
  // looks like something broken rather than something placed. That is the
  // F-6 shape, in the phase built to fix F-6.
  //
  // WHY A SECOND BOX RATHER THAN MOVING `NB_BOUNDS.x0`: before this ruling
  // the spread interior and the mark canvas were the same box. They are NOT
  // the same box any more, and collapsing them onto one constant would make
  // the band and the pen share a number that no longer means the same thing
  // for both. x1/y0/y1 are taken BY REFERENCE from NB_BOUNDS so only the
  // left bound can differ, and it cannot drift.
  //
  // Both consumers of the mark bound read it: `clampDecoOrigin` (stickers,
  // photographs, placed pictures) and the pen's pointer gate (handwriting).
  // Her ruling names all three kinds of mark, so all three move together.
  var NB_MARK_BOUNDS = { x0: NB_GUTTER_X, x1: NB_BOUNDS.x1,
    y0: NB_BOUNDS.y0, y1: NB_BOUNDS.y1 };
  var NB_ENTRY_ROW = { x: 140, y: 196, w: 104, h: 16 };
  var NB_DRAG_THRESHOLD = 3;         // the shipped moved threshold
  // 26.9 F-11: the Shift-constrain increment while rotating, in degrees.
  // 15 is Photoshop's own, and it is chosen over a coarser 45 or 90 because
  // it CONTAINS them (90 and 180 are both multiples) while still allowing
  // Shift to straighten a photo a little. A 90-only snap would give the
  // named stops and take away the useful ones.
  var NB_ROTATE_SNAP = 15;
  // 48 marks on ONE DAY, mirroring server.py DECOR_CAP. 26.9-03 spelled the
  // literal 48 inline at the one placement path that existed; 26.9-04 added
  // a second placement path (the tray), and TWO copies of a cap is the
  // shape of defect this phase keeps finding. One constant, both paths.
  var NB_DECO_CAP = 48;
  // A placed picture's default box. Not declared in the UI-SPEC (which
  // pins the 44x32 THUMBNAIL, not the placed element), so this is an
  // executor choice: a 4:3 box big enough to read as a photograph on a
  // 192x186 page half without dominating it. She can scale it (D-07).
  var NB_IMG_BOX = { w: 48, h: 36 };

  // 26.9-07 (D-10 tier 2, T-26.9-33): THE FREEHAND PEN'S TWO CEILINGS.
  // MIRRORED, NOT DERIVED, in server.py as DECOR_PTS_CAP / DECOR_STROKE_CAP,
  // and asserted equal from both sides — an unbounded stroke, or an
  // unbounded number of them, is the obvious growth vector on a store that
  // is one JSON file.
  //
  // THE WORST CASE WAS COMPUTED RATHER THAN FELT: 48 groups (NB_DECO_CAP) x
  // 16 strokes x 64 points x 2 coordinates = 98,304 whole numbers, which at
  // three digits and a comma each is roughly 393 KB — inside server.py's
  // 1 MiB MAX_BODY_BYTES with room to spare. That arithmetic is executed in
  // tests/test_server_smoke.py rather than left as a claim here.
  var NB_PEN_PTS_CAP = 64;           // points in ONE stroke
  var NB_PEN_STROKE_CAP = 16;        // strokes in ONE group

  // 26.91-26 (F-23, T-26.91-170): THE PAGE BOUNDS THE SERVER ACTUALLY CHECKS.
  // MIRRORED, NOT DERIVED, from server.py's four DECOR_*; asserted equal both
  // ways by G-25/mirror/constants-agree. They bound every stroke POINT's
  // absolute position, NOT the origin: NB_MARK_BOUNDS is the canvas she drags
  // in, these are the write's fence, and the two are NOT the same rule.
  var NB_DECOR_X_MIN = 4;
  var NB_DECOR_X_MAX = 379;
  var NB_DECOR_Y_MIN = 4;
  var NB_DECOR_Y_MAX = 189;

  // 26.91-30 (F-26): THE EFFECTIVE LEGAL REGION, IN INK SPACE. Argument, both
  // caveats and the three disagreeing right edges: tokens.css .page-deco-region.
  var NB_MARK_REGION = (function () {
    return {
      x0: Math.max(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x0, NB_DECOR_X_MIN),
      x1: Math.min(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x1, NB_DECOR_X_MAX),
      y0: Math.max(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y0, NB_DECOR_Y_MIN),
      y1: Math.min(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y1, NB_DECOR_Y_MAX)
    };
  }());

  // ---- 26.91-38 (D-13 / F-26): ONE REGION BUILDER, TWO CALL SITES ---------
  //
  // THE HONEST LINE — the box her marks must land inside. It was authored at
  // 26.91-30 inline in paintPageDecorations and had exactly one caller; this
  // wave gives it a second (the refusal fallback in renderNotebookBand), so
  // it becomes a NAMED function rather than a second spelling of the same
  // construction. Two copies would agree on the day they were written and
  // diverge the first time either bound moved, silently, because both would
  // keep drawing a plausible box. That drift is this phase's named defect
  // class and this is the worst place in the file for it: the two boxes would
  // be two claims about where a mark may go.
  //
  // ⚠ THE GEOMETRY STAYS DERIVED FROM THE CLAMP'S OWN CONSTANTS AND IS NEVER
  // RE-TYPED. NB_MARK_REGION is computed at 12050 from NB_MARK_BOUNDS and the
  // four NB_DECOR_* fence values — that is wave 30's finding and extracting
  // this block does not undo it. No numeric literal belongs in here except
  // the `+ 1` that turns an INCLUSIVE bound pair into a COUNT (x1 - x0 + 1),
  // which is arithmetic on the bounds rather than a value of its own.
  // Argument, both caveats and the three disagreeing right edges: tokens.css
  // .page-deco-region.
  function nbPaintMarkRegion(scene) {
    var region = document.createElement('div');
    region.className = 'page-deco-region';
    region.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    region.style.setProperty('--x', String(NB_MARK_REGION.x0));
    region.style.setProperty('--y', String(NB_MARK_REGION.y0));
    region.style.setProperty('--w',
      String(NB_MARK_REGION.x1 - NB_MARK_REGION.x0 + 1));
    region.style.setProperty('--h',
      String(NB_MARK_REGION.y1 - NB_MARK_REGION.y0 + 1));
    scene.appendChild(region);
  }

  // 26.9-04 (D-11): the client half of the sticker roster — name -> {x, w}
  // into assets/room/notebook-stickers.png. `x` is the RUNNING PREFIX SUM of
  // the preceding widths and the sheet's scene width is their total (316), so
  // a name resolves to an offset and NOTHING ELSE. No path is ever built from
  // a stored value: the stored field is a name, the name is looked up here,
  // and a miss simply renders nothing. That is strictly stronger than the
  // room's two-layer CAT_SPRITES fence, which still composes a filename.
  //
  // MIRRORED, NOT DERIVED, in two other places: server.py DECOR_SPRITES (the
  // names, for the store's membership refusal) and tools/gen_room_sprites.py
  // NB_STICKERS (the names and widths, which lay the sheet out). Three copies
  // is two too many to keep by eye, so tests/test_sprite_geometry.py asserts
  // agreement in BOTH directions and re-derives every `x` here arithmetically
  // from the widths — the table is never trusted as typed.
  var NB_STICKER_H = 24;             // uniform cell height, scene px
  var NB_STICKERS = {
    // Core 6 — ships with the editor (Tier B)
    'stamp-post':   { x: 0,   w: 24 },
    'washi-stripe': { x: 24,  w: 48 },
    'corner-photo': { x: 72,  w: 16 },
    'ticket':       { x: 88,  w: 32 },
    'moon':         { x: 120, w: 20 },
    'candle-mark':  { x: 140, w: 16 },
    // Richness 4 — first to cut (Tier C)
    'stamp-round':  { x: 156, w: 24 },
    'washi-dot':    { x: 180, w: 48 },
    'tape-clear':   { x: 228, w: 40 },
    'thread':       { x: 268, w: 48 }
  };
  // The sheet's total scene width, DERIVED from the table rather than typed —
  // a typed constant is the fourth copy and the one that would drift silently
  // when Tier C is cut.
  var NB_SHEET_W = (function () {
    var t = 0;
    for (var k in NB_STICKERS) {
      if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(NB_STICKERS, k)) {
        t += NB_STICKERS[k].w;
      }
    }
    return t;
  }());

  // 26.9-05 (D-02): THE SELECTED DECORATION — exactly one, or none.
  //
  // Held as the RECORD ITSELF, never as an index. The reordering gesture
  // below MOVES records inside the array, so an index would point at a
  // different mark the instant she dragged something.
  var NB_SEL = null;

  // 26.9-07 (D-10 tier 2): THE PEN IS A MODE WITHIN THE MODE, not a second
  // mode. It changes what a pointerdown on the page MEANS and nothing else:
  // pen on, you draw; pen off, you arrange. Off on entry, off on exit.
  var NB_PEN = false;
  // 26.91-02: THE ARMED `write` TOOL — the pen's SECOND INSTANCE, not a new
  // idiom. Same shape, same lifetime, same armed look: it changes what a
  // pointerdown on the page MEANS and nothing else. It exists because the
  // band was re-laid and finally has room for it; before that, placing text
  // was an undiscoverable double-click.
  //
  // MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH THE PEN IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. The exclusion is
  // written into BOTH setters rather than into one and hoped for in the
  // other — a single-sided guard holds in whichever direction somebody
  // happened to test and leaks in the other, and two armed tools contending
  // for one pointerdown would mint records she did not ask for.
  var NB_WRITE = false;
  // The group a second drag appends to. Held as the RECORD, for the same
  // reason NB_SEL is: the reorder moves records inside the array.
  var NB_PEN_GROUP = null;
  // The one scene listener the pen ever has. Held so it can be RELEASED
  // rather than merely gated — see attachPenCapture.
  var NB_PEN_HANDLER = null;

  // 26.9-05: THE WHOLE REORDERING GESTURE, and there is no chrome for it.
  // On drag release the dragged record moves to the END of the array, and
  // the array IS the draw order (the painter writes the index into --i and
  // tokens.css turns it into a z-index).
  //
  // SEND-BEHIND-TEXT DOES NOT EXIST, deliberately: with D-02's free overlap
  // and no legibility invariant, a buried element would be undiscoverable,
  // and the day reset must not have to rescue a thing the interface hid.
  function bringDecoToFront(rec) {
    var d = decoDay(NB_DAY);
    var i = d.items.indexOf(rec);
    if (i === -1 || i === d.items.length - 1) { return i; }
    d.items.splice(i, 1);
    d.items.push(rec);
    return d.items.length - 1;
  }

  // ---- 26.9-05: FREE TRANSFORM (D-03/D-07) --------------------------------
  //
  // Rotation is an INTEGER DEGREE and it WRAPS rather than clamping — an
  // angle is cyclic, not bounded, so 360 is 0 and -1 is 359. Integer because
  // sub-degree precision is invisible at scene scale and doubles the stored
  // payload for nothing.
  //
  // Scale is a stored FLOAT with a FLOOR and a CEILING, and it CLAMPS —
  // a size is bounded, not cyclic. The floor is D-07's explicit requirement
  // (a photo cannot be reduced to nothing) and 0.5 is chosen rather than
  // rounded to: it leaves the polaroid at 36x27 scene px, still recognisable.
  //
  // NEITHER IS SNAPPED TO ANYTHING. `.page-deco` is exempt from the
  // integer-at-rest rule, explicitly: the scene never rests at a fractional
  // --k, but a decoration is content she placed by hand and a fractional
  // scale or angle is the entire point. Both bounds are mirrored in
  // server.py validate_decorations — the client clamps and the server
  // refuses, because a hand-edited store file goes through neither UI.
  var NB_A_MOD = 360;                // degrees; the angle wraps modulo this
  var NB_S_MIN = 0.5;                // D-07's floor
  var NB_S_MAX = 2.0;
  var NB_S_DEFAULT = 1;

  // BOTH GUARDS TEST THE TYPE, NOT MERELY THE VALUE, and that is not
  // fussiness: `Number(null)` is 0, so a value-only guard would send a
  // record carrying `s: null` to the FLOOR of 0.5 instead of to its
  // resting 1 — her page would come back a little smaller than she left
  // it, for no visible reason. `typeof` is also the same bool exclusion
  // the server's _decor_num makes, from the other side of the wire.
  function wrapDecoAngle(a) {
    if (typeof a !== 'number' || !isFinite(a)) { return 0; }
    return ((Math.round(a) % NB_A_MOD) + NB_A_MOD) % NB_A_MOD;
  }

  function clampDecoScale(s) {
    if (typeof s !== 'number' || !isFinite(s)) { return NB_S_DEFAULT; }
    return Math.max(NB_S_MIN, Math.min(NB_S_MAX, s));
  }

  // A decoration's UNSCALED box, by kind. One place, because reading the
  // text box for every kind would clamp a 48-wide sticker as though it were
  // 72 wide and stop it eight pixels short of the right edge.
  function decoBox(rec) {
    if (rec && rec.kind === 'sticker' && NB_STICKERS[rec.sprite]) {
      return { w: NB_STICKERS[rec.sprite].w, h: NB_STICKER_H };
    }
    if (rec && rec.kind === 'image') { return NB_IMG_BOX; }
    // 26.9-07: a stroke group's box is DERIVED FROM ITS POINTS, and this is
    // the seam that makes the pen inherit the drag for free — the clamp, the
    // centre the rotation composes about, and the handle wrapper's size all
    // read their box from here. Without this branch a stroke group would be
    // clamped and turned as though it were a 72x24 hand-text element.
    if (rec && rec.kind === 'stroke') { return strokeBox(rec); }
    // 26.9-09 (D-01's other half): the page's OWN polaroid, promoted. The
    // box is READ FROM THE GEOMETRY TABLE rather than typed again — a second
    // copy of 72x54 agrees today and drifts silently the first time the page
    // composition moves. Joining decoBox is the whole of what makes the
    // clamp, the rotation centre and the 12-scene-px handle wrapper correct
    // for a 72x54 box without a line of new geometry, exactly as the pen
    // proved in 26.9-07. At D-07's 0.5 floor this is 36x27 scene px.
    if (rec && rec.kind === 'photo') {
      return { w: STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM.photo.w,
        h: STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM.photo.h };
    }
    return NB_TEXT_BOX;
  }

  // The live preview during a handle drag. The decoration carries the
  // transform; the handle wrapper carries the ROTATION ONLY and is sized to
  // the already-scaled box, which is what keeps the 12-scene-px hit area a
  // constant instead of shrinking with the thing it is there to grow.
  function previewDecoTransform(live, rec, w, h, a, s) {
    if (!live) { return; }
    if (live.deco) {
      live.deco.style.setProperty('--a', String(a));
      live.deco.style.setProperty('--s', String(s));
    }
    if (live.wrap) {
      live.wrap.style.setProperty('--a', String(a));
      live.wrap.style.setProperty('--x', String(rec.x + (w - w * s) / 2));
      live.wrap.style.setProperty('--y', String(rec.y + (h - h * s) / 2));
      live.wrap.style.setProperty('--w', String(w * s));
      live.wrap.style.setProperty('--h', String(h * s));
    }
  }

  // Clamp a decoration's ORIGIN into the MARK CANVAS — the right page, per
  // the owner's `right-page-only` ruling of 2026-08-08 (26.91-CONTEXT.md
  // A-12; **26.9 D-05's** gutter-straddle clause retired by her call). A
  // rotated element's CORNERS may visually exceed this box, and that is
  // accepted — it is what rotation looks like.
  function clampDecoOrigin(x, y, w, h) {
    return {
      x: Math.max(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x0, Math.min(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x1 - w, x)),
      y: Math.max(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y0, Math.min(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y1 - h, y))
    };
  }

  // 26.91-26 (F-23): THE SMALLEST AND LARGEST RELATIVE OFFSET A RECORD OCCUPIES
  // — the off-by-one that IS this defect, in one place. A count-box of width w
  // ends one short of w, so a sticker's largest offset is w-1 while a stroke's
  // is its largest point: strokeBox returns a SPAN, the old clamp took it as a
  // COUNT. The minimum is READ, never assumed 0. KIND-AGNOSTIC like the server,
  // which bounds `pts` on any record carrying them. See M-25.
  function decoPointExtent(rec) {
    var runs = strokeList(rec);
    var e = null;
    for (var i = 0; i < runs.length; i++) {
      for (var j = 0; j + 1 < runs[i].length; j += 2) {
        var px = runs[i][j];
        var py = runs[i][j + 1];
        if (!e) { e = { x0: px, x1: px, y0: py, y1: py }; continue; }
        if (px < e.x0) { e.x0 = px; }
        if (px > e.x1) { e.x1 = px; }
        if (py < e.y0) { e.y0 = py; }
        if (py > e.y1) { e.y1 = py; }
      }
    }
    if (e) { return e; }
    var b = decoBox(rec);
    return { x0: 0, x1: b.w - 1, y0: 0, y1: b.h - 1 };
  }

  // 26.91-26 (F-23): THE RECORD-AWARE ORIGIN CLAMP — ceilinged on the quantity
  // the SERVER checks. Beside clampDecoOrigin, not replacing it: the tray and
  // text placements pass count-boxes and the pen's pin a 1x1 footprint. The
  // floor keeps her `right-page-only` ruling (A-12) by taking the GREATER of
  // the canvas floor and the server's — stricter on x, the intended direction.
  // No legal origin at all? HOLD POSITION: inventing one would move her mark
  // somewhere she did not choose AND still be refused.
  function clampDecoOriginFor(rec, x, y) {
    var e = decoPointExtent(rec);
    var lo = { x: Math.max(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x0, NB_DECOR_X_MIN - e.x0),
      y: Math.max(NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y0, NB_DECOR_Y_MIN - e.y0) };
    var hi = { x: NB_DECOR_X_MAX - e.x1, y: NB_DECOR_Y_MAX - e.y1 };
    return {
      x: lo.x > hi.x ? rec.x : Math.max(lo.x, Math.min(hi.x, x)),
      y: lo.y > hi.y ? rec.y : Math.max(lo.y, Math.min(hi.y, y))
    };
  }

  // The day's record, created empty on first touch. Keyed by dayLabel;
  // each item is keyed by the BLESSING's itemId (D-06), never by a page
  // ordinal — an ordinal shifts when a sibling on the same day is fenced,
  // and itemId does not.
  function decoDay(day) {
    if (!DECORATIONS[day] || typeof DECORATIONS[day] !== 'object') {
      DECORATIONS[day] = { reset: false, items: [] };
    }
    if (!Array.isArray(DECORATIONS[day].items)) {
      DECORATIONS[day].items = [];
    }
    return DECORATIONS[day];
  }

  function decoItemsForPage(day, itemId) {
    return decoDay(day).items.filter(function (r) {
      return r && r.page === itemId;
    });
  }

  // ---- 26.9-09 (D-01's OTHER HALF): THE PAGE'S OWN POLAROID --------------
  //
  // WHY THIS KIND NEEDS TWO HELPERS WHEN NO OTHER KIND NEEDED ANY. Every
  // other mark is BORN from an explicit placement act — a tap on the page
  // interior, a tap on a tray card. The polaroid already exists on the page
  // before it is a record, composed by paintBlessingPage out of the ledger.
  // So it is the only kind that needs (a) a way to ask whether it has been
  // promoted yet and (b) a way to promote it. That asymmetry is the entire
  // delta beyond 26.9-06's measured "a painter branch and a DECOR_KINDS
  // value, nothing more" — which was right about the snapshot and the reset
  // and simply was not measuring the creation of the record.

  // Is this page's polaroid a live record right now? Null means the pinned
  // .station-photo slot paints it exactly as it always has.
  //
  // THIS IS THE ONE NEW READER OF THE RESET FLAG, and that is precisely what
  // makes the day reset able to put the page back IN FULL: on a reset day
  // this answers no, the pinned slot paints, and the moved record sits
  // untouched underneath it (D-15 — the reset is a FLAG and never a delete,
  // and that survival is the only thing that makes a reset undoable).
  function livePagePhoto(day, itemId) {
    var rec = decoDay(day);
    if (rec.reset) { return null; }
    for (var i = 0; i < rec.items.length; i++) {
      var r = rec.items[i];
      if (r && r.kind === 'photo' && r.page === itemId) { return r; }
    }
    return null;
  }

  // The promotion. Runs at the top of the page painter, BEFORE the photo
  // decision, and only while arranging — so entering the mode is what makes
  // the photograph touchable, and NOTHING MOVES at that moment, because the
  // record is created at the pinned slot the polaroid is already sitting in.
  //
  // IT PUSHES NOTHING ONTO THE HISTORY AND POSTS NOTHING. The mode making an
  // existing thing touchable is not an act she performed, and a record that
  // encodes the resting position is not a change she can see. The next real
  // act persists it along with everything else, and one undo after her first
  // move returns the polaroid to rest.
  //
  // THE TRAP THIS SITS ON, WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE IT IS 26.9-05's REJECTED
  // DISCRIMINATOR IN A DIFFERENT COSTUME: livePagePhoto returning nothing is
  // AMBIGUOUS between "she has not promoted it yet" and "the day is reset",
  // and the two need OPPOSITE responses — promote in the first case, do
  // nothing in the second. So this tests the reset flag ITSELF rather than
  // reading the absence as permission. Two readers of that flag exist after
  // this plan and both are new; the shipped early return in
  // paintPageDecorations is left byte-unchanged, because a shipped group
  // pins it.
  //
  // THE OWNERSHIP KEY IS THE ENTRY BEING PAINTED, NEVER NB_PLACE. The
  // polaroid belongs to the page it IS, so on a two-page day the right
  // page's photograph stores the right page's item id. That is what keeps
  // the ownership question WINDOWS row 28 raises for stroke groups (owned by
  // the spread's FIRST page, because that is what the shipped placement path
  // assigns) from reaching this kind at all. D-06's per-page key does not
  // move and D-15's day scope stands, unreconciled.
  function ensurePagePhoto(entry, side) {
    if (!NBDESIGN || !entry || !entry.isImage) { return; }
    var d = decoDay(entry.dayLabel);
    if (d.reset) { return; }
    if (livePagePhoto(entry.dayLabel, entry.itemId)) { return; }
    // The day cap, checked exactly as both shipped placement paths check it:
    // a day at the cap silently declines and keeps working (UI-SPEC A7).
    // Consuming the cap here would make a post refused wholesale and lose a
    // write she cannot see (T-26.9-42).
    if (d.items.length >= NB_DECO_CAP) { return; }
    var g = STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM;
    d.items.push({ page: entry.itemId, kind: 'photo',
      x: g.pageX[side] + g.photo.dx, y: g.photo.y });
  }

  function loadDecorations() {
    return apiGet('/api/decorations').then(function (res) {
      // fail-open, the ledger posture: an unreachable or off-shape read
      // is an undecorated book, never an error surface. The page's
      // auto-composition is a complete page on its own (D-08).
      var days = (res.ok && res.data && res.data.days &&
        typeof res.data.days === 'object') ? res.data.days : {};
      DECORATIONS = days;
      return days;
    }).catch(function () { DECORATIONS = {}; return {}; });
  }

  // ONE write, at release, never mid-drag. LOCAL STATE IS KEPT ON FAILURE
  // AND NEVER REVERTED — a silent loss of handmade work is the outcome
  // this error path exists to prevent. The quiet failure row is UI-SPEC
  // E3/E6 and lands with the chrome band in plan 06; until then a failed
  // write is console-quiet and her marks stay on screen, which is the
  // strictly safer half of that pair.
  function postDecorations(day) {
    if (!day) { return Promise.resolve(); }
    var rec = decoDay(day);
    return apiPost('/api/decorations', {
      version: 1, day: day, reset: !!rec.reset, items: rec.items
    }).then(function (res) {
      // 26.91-27: errorText with NO fallback — absent must arrive as absent.
      if (!res.ok) { nbSaveFailed(errorText(res, null)); }
      return res;
    }).catch(function () { nbSaveFailed(null); });
  }
  // Fail-VISIBLE state for the band that lands in plan 06. Kept as module
  // state rather than swallowed, so the row it feeds is a wiring job and
  // not a re-derivation.
  var NB_SAVE_FAILED = false;
  // 26.91-27 (F-23 b): the server's refusal + its measured cap. nbSaveFailed
  // is the ONE raising path, on both branches; it repaints (M-25: nothing
  // else does) except over a live input, which a repaint would delete.
  var NB_SAVE_REASON = null;
  var NB_SAVE_REASON_CAP = 72;
  function nbSaveFailed(reason) {
    NB_SAVE_FAILED = true;
    NB_SAVE_REASON = reason || null;
    var sc = $('station-scene');
    if (sc && sc.querySelector('.page-deco-input')) { return; }
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // ---- 26.9-06 (D-15): A SECOND UNDO STACK, AND THE SHIPPED KEYS ----------
  //
  // WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, because it looks like a convenience and is not.
  // D-02 gave this canvas fully free overlap with no protected zones, and the
  // consequence was stated and ACCEPTED at decision time: it REMOVES the
  // legibility guarantee. There is no machine-checkable page-is-still-readable
  // rule (see the five-clause note above paintPageDecorations, which refuses
  // to invent one). The nets are these two, and they are BOTH nets, not
  // either: the stack below is the FINE-GRAINED one for a slip, and the day
  // reset is the COARSE one for a day that has gone somewhere she does not
  // want. Removing either leaves the other holding weight it was not sized for.
  //
  // D-15 DIVERGED DELIBERATELY AND ONLY HALFWAY: the STACK is separate, the
  // KEY BINDINGS are reused. Each half has its own reason.
  //
  //   The stack is separate because sharing the room's would let a ⌘Z struck
  //   while arranging a page revert a room drag — two histories with one
  //   spelling.
  //
  //   The bindings are reused because muscle memory is not scoped, and the
  //   reuse is SAFE BY VERIFICATION RATHER THAN BY ASSERTION. Two facts,
  //   both re-read in current source rather than carried from a plan:
  //     (a) the room's design keydown listener OPENS with `if (!DESIGN)
  //         { return; }` — it declines outright whenever room design mode is
  //         off; and
  //     (b) D-14's mutual exclusion means DESIGN is false for the ENTIRE time
  //         a station is open — enforced at setDesign's first statement
  //         (`if (on && stationIsRaised()) { return; }`), which is the one
  //         route into the flag.
  //   Together: the two handlers can never both fire on one keystroke. If
  //   either fact ever stops holding, the shared bindings stop being safe
  //   silently — which is exactly what 26.9-03's trip-wire group guards, and
  //   why it goes red the instant DESIGN could be truthy while a station is
  //   open.
  //
  // BOTH PATHS ARE KEPT AND NEITHER IS DROPPED: the in-scene glyphs are the
  // DISCOVERABLE path (she can see that undo exists without knowing a combo),
  // and the keys are the MUSCLE-MEMORY path. Dropping the glyphs was
  // considered — the band would be two controls quieter — and rejected,
  // because a keystroke nobody can see is not an affordance.
  var NB_UNDO = [];
  var NB_REDO = [];
  // 26.9-06 (D-15/A-3): the reset row's second tap. Armed by the first tap,
  // disarmed by the second (either way) and by leaving the mode or the day —
  // an armed confirmation must never outlive the thing it was about to do.
  var NB_RESET_ARMED = false;
  var NB_RESET_COPY = 'undo everything';
  // The shipped ceiling, copied by value from DESIGN_UNDO's own `> 60`
  // shift. Sixty snapshots of one day's records is a few kilobytes.
  var NB_UNDO_CAP = 60;

  // A snapshot is the WHOLE day record — the `reset` flag AND the items.
  // The flag has to be in here or the reset would not be undoable, which is
  // the entire reason the reset is a flag rather than a delete.
  function nbSnapshot() {
    return JSON.stringify(decoDay(NB_DAY));
  }

  function applyNbSnapshot(s) {
    var rec;
    try { rec = JSON.parse(s); } catch (e) { return; }
    DECORATIONS[NB_DAY] = {
      reset: !!rec.reset,
      items: Array.isArray(rec.items) ? rec.items : []
    };
    // the selection is held as the RECORD, and every record in the array was
    // just replaced by a parsed copy — so the old one now points at nothing
    // on the page. Dropped rather than re-matched: there is no identity to
    // re-match it by that a reorder would not break.
    NB_SEL = null;
    postDecorations(NB_DAY);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // PUSHED BEFORE EVERY MUTATION, never after — the room's own discipline.
  // Redo is cleared on any new act, because a new act forks the history and
  // the branch she did not take is not a thing to offer her.
  function pushNbUndo() {
    NB_UNDO.push(nbSnapshot());
    if (NB_UNDO.length > NB_UNDO_CAP) { NB_UNDO.shift(); }
    NB_REDO.length = 0;
    updateNbButtons();
  }

  function doNbUndo() {
    if (NB_UNDO.length === 0) { return; }
    NB_REDO.push(nbSnapshot());
    applyNbSnapshot(NB_UNDO.pop());
    updateNbButtons();
  }

  function doNbRedo() {
    if (NB_REDO.length === 0) { return; }
    NB_UNDO.push(nbSnapshot());
    applyNbSnapshot(NB_REDO.pop());
    updateNbButtons();
  }

  // ONE rule for "empty means disabled", applied by the band painter at paint
  // time and by updateNbButtons afterwards. Two spellings of one rule is the
  // shape of drift this phase keeps finding, so there is one.
  //
  // DISABLED, NOT HIDDEN. A band whose control COUNT changes as history
  // accrues would reflow the row and move the reset and exit targets under
  // her finger — the two controls she is most likely to be reaching for right
  // after the act that filled the stack.
  // 26.91-02: ONE TYPE REGISTER. undo and redo were the band's last glyph
  // wearers; they are words now, so they join the register every other named
  // band control already wears. `station-nb-glyph` and `station-flip` leave
  // these two nodes — and with .station-fixture goes the coral hover ring,
  // which is correct: the accent list is closed at two and a band control is
  // on neither. Their focus indicator is the --ink ring 26.91-01 added to
  // `.station-caption-add.station-nb-row:focus-visible`.
  function nbGlyphState(el, stack) {
    var off = stack.length === 0;
    el.className = 'station-caption-add station-nb-row' +
      (off ? ' station-nb-off' : '');
    el.setAttribute('data-nb-off', off ? '1' : '0');
  }

  function updateNbButtons() {
    var scene = $('station-scene');
    if (!scene || !scene.querySelectorAll) { return; }
    // 26.91-02: QUERIES THE STRUCTURAL HOOK, NOT THE PRESENTATIONAL CLASS.
    // This read `.station-nb-glyph`, which the re-lay above takes off both
    // nodes — it would have selected NOTHING and updateNbButtons would have
    // silently stopped updating anything, with no error and every suite
    // green. `data-nb-stack` is already set on both nodes by the painter,
    // is what the loop body reads one line down, and describes what the node
    // IS rather than how it looks.
    var g = scene.querySelectorAll('[data-nb-stack]');
    for (var i = 0; i < g.length; i++) {
      nbGlyphState(g[i], g[i].getAttribute('data-nb-stack') === 'redo'
        ? NB_REDO : NB_UNDO);
    }
  }

  // ---- 26.9-06 (D-15, refined by A-3): PUT THIS DAY BACK -----------------
  //
  // REPRESENTATION: A STORED BOOLEAN FLAG, NEVER A DELETE. The reason is not
  // in CONTEXT, so it is written here, because a later reader will otherwise
  // see two implementations that produce the same page and pick the simpler
  // one:
  //
  //   The auto composition is a pure function of (itemId, ledger timestamp),
  //   and BOTH of those live in the ledger rather than in this store — the
  //   timestamp is server-stamped once at append, the id is a content hash,
  //   and pickBlessingDecoration hashes them with no clock and no globals.
  //   So the same page wears the same mark on every open, forever, and a
  //   DELETE would restore the auto page just as exactly as the flag does.
  //   The flag is therefore NOT chosen for correctness.
  //
  //   It is chosen because IT KEEPS THE RECORDS ALIVE UNDERNEATH, and that
  //   is the only thing that makes the reset itself undoable within the
  //   session. D-15 pairs reset with undo for precisely this reason. Replace
  //   the flag with a delete and that property is gone, silently, with every
  //   visible outcome unchanged.
  //
  // SCOPE: THE GESTURE IS THE DAY, THE KEY STAYS THE PAGE. Reset means
  // "clear every decoration whose page belongs to this day" — day-scoped
  // gesture, page-scoped storage key (D-06). These are NOT in conflict and
  // must not be reconciled by moving the ownership key: an ordinal-or-page
  // rekey to match the gesture would break the very property D-06 exists
  // for. The accepted cost is stated by the owner: resetting one messy page
  // also clears the other pages of that day.
  // 26.9 F-9 (the owner, 2026-08-06): "sometimes user press this because they
  // want to start over, however after I tried to start over, all of the old
  // edits are back."
  //
  // THE BUG, AND WHY IT WAS DESIGNED IN. A reset raises a day-level flag and
  // the old records survive underneath it. Both edit paths then cleared that
  // flag on the next mark, which RESURRECTED the whole hidden day. The two
  // sites each argued for it in the same words — "between an invisible write
  // and a visible restoration, take the one that makes the mistake visible"
  // — which is a good rule aimed at the wrong scenario. It protects against
  // writing into a day whose contents are hidden; it did not consider that
  // the reason people press the reset control IS to start over. So the
  // first new mark undid the very thing the gesture was for.
  // (The control is named by ROLE, not by its label: NB_RESET_COPY is the
  // single source of that label and 26.91-11 renamed it once already.)
  //
  // THE FIX: starting over starts from blank. The flag still hides rather
  // than deletes, so an undo inside the session still brings the whole day
  // back untouched — but the moment she makes a NEW mark on a reset day, the
  // superseded records are dropped instead of resurrected. Nothing is
  // deleted at reset time; it is deleted only when she has demonstrably
  // chosen to build something else on that page.
  //
  // Both call sites go through here so they cannot drift apart again.
  function nbClearResetForEdit(d) {
    if (d && d.reset) {
      // She reset the day and is now placing something new: the hidden
      // records are what she asked to be rid of. pushNbUndo() has already
      // snapshotted them at every caller, so one undo still restores them.
      d.items = [];
      d.reset = false;
    }
  }

  // 26.9 F-13 — declining, from any of its three routes (the `no` word, the
  // scrim, Escape). Changes NOTHING: not the records, not the flag, not the
  // history. One function so the three routes cannot diverge, which is the
  // failure the in-place version was one edit away from.
  function nbDisarmReset() {
    if (!NB_RESET_ARMED) { return false; }
    NB_RESET_ARMED = false;
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
    return true;
  }

  function nbResetDay(day) {
    if (!day) { return; }
    // BEFORE the flag goes up, so one undo brings the whole day back.
    pushNbUndo();
    decoDay(day).reset = true;
    NB_SEL = null;
    postDecorations(day);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // The shipped key set, pointed at the notebook's stack. Bound on mode
  // entry and RELEASED on exit — not merely gated by the flag, because a
  // listener that survives its mode is a listener somebody has to remember
  // about. See the safety verification in the block comment above.
  function nbKeydown(e) {
    if (!NBDESIGN) { return; }
    var key = String(e.key || '').toLowerCase();
    // 26.9 F-13: Escape declines the confirmation. A modal that cannot be
    // dismissed from the keyboard is a trap, and Escape must reach it BEFORE
    // any other binding — while the panel is up it is the only thing on
    // screen, so nothing else should be listening.
    if (key === 'escape') {
      if (nbDisarmReset()) { e.preventDefault(); }
      return;
    }
    // While the confirmation is up, the editing keys are inert. Undoing or
    // deleting underneath an open modal edits a page she cannot see.
    if (NB_RESET_ARMED) { return; }
    if ((e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey) && key === 'z') {
      e.preventDefault();
      if (e.shiftKey) { doNbRedo(); } else { doNbUndo(); }
      return;
    }
    if ((e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey) && key === 'y') {
      e.preventDefault();
      doNbRedo();
      return;
    }
    // 26.9 F-10: the ACCESSIBLE half of per-object delete. The handle layer
    // is aria-hidden direct-manipulation chrome, so without this there is no
    // keyboard route to removing a mark at all. Guarded on a live selection:
    // Backspace with nothing selected must stay inert rather than deleting
    // "the last thing", which would be a destructive act nobody aimed.
    if (key === 'delete' || key === 'backspace') {
      if (!NB_SEL) { return; }
      e.preventDefault();
      nbRemoveDeco(NB_SEL);
    }
  }

  // The mode. The FIRST statement is the belt to the shipped station-click
  // guard's suspenders: room design mode wins outright (D-14) and the
  // notebook cannot be entered while it is on.
  function setNotebookDesign(on) {
    if (on && DESIGN) { return; }
    var was = NBDESIGN;
    NBDESIGN = !!on;
    // 26.9-05: leaving the mode drops the selection. A coral outline is
    // design-mode chrome and must never survive into the reading loop.
    if (!NBDESIGN) { NB_SEL = null; }
    if (NBDESIGN && !was) {
      // 26.9-06: a fresh arranging session starts with no history — the
      // room's clear-at-mode-boundary precedent (setDesign zeroes both
      // stacks), so the glyphs are honestly disabled at entry.
      NB_UNDO.length = 0;
      NB_REDO.length = 0;
      document.addEventListener('keydown', nbKeydown);
    }
    if (!NBDESIGN) {
      NB_RESET_ARMED = false;
      // 26.9-07: the pen is a mode WITHIN this mode, so it cannot outlive
      // it. The scene listener is released by attachPenCapture on the
      // repaint below, which sees NBDESIGN false and binds nothing.
      NB_PEN = false;
      // 26.91-02: `write` is a mode within this mode too, and cannot
      // outlive it either. Without this line, exiting and re-entering
      // arranging would land with a tool ALREADY ARMED, and her next tap on
      // the page would mint a record she did not ask for — 26.9 F-7's
      // failure re-created by the control meant to replace it.
      NB_WRITE = false;
      NB_PEN_GROUP = null;
      // 26.91-18: DISARM PATH 1 OF 3 — the armed classes cannot outlive the
      // mode, or a drawing cursor strands on the room screen.
      document.body.classList.remove('nb-armed', 'nb-armed-pen', 'nb-armed-write');
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', nbKeydown);
    }
    // NEVER 'design-mode' — that class slides #catalog-panel open
    // (tokens.css:1007), dropping the room's accessory dock over the
    // station. The notebook's scope gets its own body class.
    document.body.classList.toggle('nb-design', NBDESIGN);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // The page drag. Four of twelve concerns transfer from attachDesignDrag
  // and the rest is written: the 3px moved threshold, reading --k at
  // pointerdown from #station-scene, the free-follow preview, and
  // persist-once-at-release.
  //
  // DELIBERATELY NOT CALLED FROM THIS PATH, and asserted region-scoped in
  // the test because a file-wide negative grep would be satisfied by
  // accident: the room's release-time 12px snap (which is exactly what
  // D-03 rejects for this canvas), the room's undo stack, the room's
  // position recorder, the data-cls seating-class read, the hard-coded
  // room scene root, and the three room-only reconcilers.
  // 26.9-05: THREE GESTURES, ONE FUNCTION AND ONE RELEASE PATH. `mode` is
  // 'move' (the default, the element itself), 'rotate' or 'scale' (a
  // handle). The handles are NOT a second drag implementation: the pointer
  // capture, the 3px moved threshold, the pointerdown --k read and the
  // persist-once-at-release are the SAME code, reached by all three. `live`
  // names the nodes a handle drag must preview onto (the decoration and its
  // handle wrapper), because the element under the pointer is the handle
  // and the thing that must move is not.
  function attachPageDrag(el, rec, mode, live) {
    var gesture = mode || 'move';
    el.addEventListener('pointerdown', function (e) {
      if (!NBDESIGN) { return; }
      // 26.91-20 (A-15 r1): WHILE EITHER TOOL IS ARMED, MARKS ARE LOCKED.
      // Not dragged, and not SELECTED either — NB_SEL is assigned below
      // this return, so ONE guard carries both symptoms. The pen also has
      // attachPenCapture's capture-phase half; `write` has no stroke, so
      // this scene-side guard is the whole of its rule. Driven by G-21.
      if (NB_PEN || NB_WRITE) { return; }
      e.preventDefault();
      e.stopPropagation();
      // Pitfall 6: read the scale at POINTERDOWN from #station-scene.
      // fitStationScale recomputes it on resize, so a module-level cache
      // taken at boot is wrong the first time the window changes.
      var scene = $('station-scene');
      // 26.9-04: starting a drag on the page closes the tray. Dropped as a
      // NODE, not through a repaint — a repaint mid-gesture would detach
      // the very element being dragged and freeze the preview.
      dismissTray(scene);
      // 26.9-05: touching a mark SELECTS it, and selects exactly one.
      // The flag alone here, NEVER a repaint — for the same reason
      // dismissTray drops a node instead: a repaint at pointerdown would
      // detach the element the pointer has just captured. The visual lands
      // at release, which both branches of onUp below reach.
      NB_SEL = rec || null;
      var k = parseInt(
        getComputedStyle(scene).getPropertyValue('--k'), 10) || 1;
      // 26.9-04: a decoration's box is its KIND's box. Reading the text
      // box for every kind would clamp a 48-wide sticker as though it were
      // 72 wide and stop it eight pixels short of the right edge.
      var kbox = decoBox(rec);
      var w = kbox.w;
      var h = kbox.h;
      var startX = rec.x;
      var startY = rec.y;
      var startA = wrapDecoAngle(rec.a);
      var startS = clampDecoScale(rec.s);
      var cur = { a: startA, s: startS };
      var cx0 = e.clientX;
      var cy0 = e.clientY;
      // The element's CENTRE in client px. Rotation and scale are both
      // measured FROM IT, which is the same point the CSS transform
      // composes about — so an element turns in place instead of walking
      // across the page as it is turned.
      var srect = (scene && scene.getBoundingClientRect)
        ? scene.getBoundingClientRect() : { left: 0, top: 0 };
      var ccx = srect.left + (startX + w / 2) * k;
      var ccy = srect.top + (startY + h / 2) * k;
      var deg0 = Math.atan2(cy0 - ccy, cx0 - ccx) * 180 / Math.PI;
      var dist0 = Math.max(1, Math.sqrt((cx0 - ccx) * (cx0 - ccx) +
        (cy0 - ccy) * (cy0 - ccy)));
      var moved = false;
      var last = null;
      // 26.91-30 (F-26): WHY TWO MARKS OF THE SAME SIZE STOP IN DIFFERENT
      // PLACES. The clamp ceilings on the record's INK, never on its box, so
      // this shows the box that actually has to stay inside the drawn line.
      // The extent is READ from decoPointExtent — a second one computed here
      // would be the same defect class as a second copy of a bound.
      var inkbox = null;
      if (gesture === 'move' && scene && scene.appendChild) {
        inkbox = document.createElement('div');
        inkbox.className = 'page-deco-inkbox';
        inkbox.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
        scene.appendChild(inkbox);
      }
      // 26.91-35 (F-28, the owner): THE BOX CARRIES THE MARK'S SCALE AND TURN.
      // `.page-deco` carries `rotate() scale(--s)` about its centre and this
      // box carried NEITHER, so it over-claimed by exactly 1/s — at her 0.5
      // mark, twice too big. THE PLACEMENT IS previewDecoTransform's SHIPPED
      // CENTRING IDIOM, not a second formula that agrees today: with
      // `ie.x0 === 0` the two lines below reduce EXACTLY to that function's
      // `rec.x + (w - w * s) / 2`. The normalised scale and angle are the
      // closure's own (`startS`/`startA`, :12786-12787) — re-normalising here
      // would be a second copy of a bound, which is this phase's named defect
      // class. The last two are the ROTATION ORIGIN: the offset from THIS
      // box's top-left to the MARK's centre, because the ink box is a SUB-box
      // and `center` would turn it about the wrong point.
      function placeInkBox(ox, oy) {
        if (!inkbox) { return; }
        var ie = decoPointExtent(rec);
        var s = startS;
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--x',
          String(Math.round(ox) + ie.x0 * s + (w - w * s) / 2));
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--y',
          String(Math.round(oy) + ie.y0 * s + (h - h * s) / 2));
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--w', String((ie.x1 - ie.x0 + 1) * s));
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--h', String((ie.y1 - ie.y0 + 1) * s));
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--a', String(startA));
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--ox', String((w / 2 - ie.x0) * s));
        inkbox.style.setProperty('--oy', String((h / 2 - ie.y0) * s));
      }
      placeInkBox(startX, startY);
      el.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
      function onMove(ev) {
        if (Math.abs(ev.clientX - cx0) + Math.abs(ev.clientY - cy0) >
            NB_DRAG_THRESHOLD) {
          moved = true;
        }
        if (gesture === 'rotate') {
          // WRAPS, never clamps: an angle is cyclic. 359 + 1 is 0.
          var free = wrapDecoAngle(startA +
            (Math.atan2(ev.clientY - ccy, ev.clientX - ccx) * 180 /
              Math.PI - deg0));
          // 26.9 F-11 (the owner): "when rotating the user can press shift to
          // rotate like 90 degrees or 180 degrees" — the Photoshop
          // constrain-angle behaviour. NB_ROTATE_SNAP is 15°, Photoshop's own
          // increment, which lands exactly on 90 and 180 while still giving
          // the useful in-between stops (30, 45, 60). A coarser 90-only snap
          // would make Shift useless for tilting a photo straight.
          //
          // READ LIVE FROM THE EVENT, never latched at pointerdown: she can
          // press or release Shift MID-DRAG, and the angle must follow. That
          // is what makes it feel like a modifier rather than a mode.
          //
          // Snapping the ABSOLUTE angle, not the delta, so the stops are the
          // ones she can name — 90 means square to the page, not 90 away from
          // wherever this mark happened to be sitting.
          cur.a = ev.shiftKey
            ? wrapDecoAngle(Math.round(free / NB_ROTATE_SNAP) *
              NB_ROTATE_SNAP)
            : free;
          previewDecoTransform(live, rec, w, h, cur.a, cur.s);
          last = cur;
          return;
        }
        if (gesture === 'scale') {
          // CLAMPS at the floor and the ceiling: a size is bounded. The
          // value between them is FREE and is not quantised — a
          // clamp-everything implementation is caught by that, not by this.
          cur.s = clampDecoScale(startS * (Math.max(1, Math.sqrt(
            (ev.clientX - ccx) * (ev.clientX - ccx) +
            (ev.clientY - ccy) * (ev.clientY - ccy))) / dist0));
          previewDecoTransform(live, rec, w, h, cur.a, cur.s);
          last = cur;
          return;
        }
        // 26.91-26 (F-23): the ONE call site that moves a stroke's origin, so
        // the one re-pointed. The other three pass count-boxes or a 1x1 pin.
        var p = clampDecoOriginFor(rec, startX + (ev.clientX - cx0) / k,
          startY + (ev.clientY - cy0) / k);
        // free-follow preview — whole scene px, NO grid snap, NO class
        // snap, nothing seated. D-02/D-03: position is free and pixel
        // level on this canvas.
        el.style.setProperty('--x', String(Math.round(p.x)));
        el.style.setProperty('--y', String(Math.round(p.y)));
        placeInkBox(p.x, p.y);
        last = p;
      }
      // 26.91-35: ONE TEARDOWN, REACHED BY BOTH ENDINGS. Release and cancel
      // end the same gesture, so they may not carry two spellings of ending
      // it — two copies agree today and drift the first time one of them
      // moves, which is this phase's named defect class. Same shape as the
      // catalog drag's `release` (:7432-7436), which is the idiom this file
      // already uses rather than a new one invented here.
      function endGesture() {
        el.releasePointerCapture(e.pointerId);
        el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
        el.removeEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
        el.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onCancel);
        // 26.91-30 (F-26): the explanation belongs to the gesture, so it ends
        // with it — dropped as a NODE (dismissTray's idiom) and BEFORE any
        // branch below repaints, so no repaint ever has to know about it.
        if (inkbox && scene && scene.removeChild) {
          scene.removeChild(inkbox);
          inkbox = null;
        }
      }
      // 26.91-35: A CANCEL IS NOT A RELEASE AND MUST NOT COMMIT. At HEAD
      // nothing listened for this at all, so a press the system interrupted —
      // rather than one she let go of — stranded the ink box for the rest of
      // the session, through a full repaint and through page turns, until the
      // next `pointerup` on a mark. LATENT AND PROVEN: she has never seen it,
      // and answered "gone" about the ordinary release.
      //
      // It takes the NO-COMMIT outcome: the element goes back exactly where
      // it started, one repaint draws it there, and that is all. No
      // `pushNbUndo`, no `postDecorations`, and NEVER the hand-text editor —
      // a typing surface raised by an interruption she did not make is the
      // worst of the four outcomes `onUp` can reach.
      function onCancel() {
        endGesture();
        el.style.setProperty('--x', String(startX));
        el.style.setProperty('--y', String(startY));
        if (gesture !== 'move') {
          previewDecoTransform(live, rec, w, h, startA, startS);
        }
        if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
      }
      function onUp() {
        endGesture();
        if (last && moved && gesture !== 'move') {
          // ONE WRITE, at release, through the same path the move takes.
          // A handle does NOT reorder: turning or resizing a mark is not
          // the reordering gesture, which is a drag of the mark itself.
          // 26.9-06: the snapshot is taken BEFORE the record is touched —
          // a stack pushed after the fact records the state she is already
          // looking at and undoes nothing.
          pushNbUndo();
          rec.a = cur.a;
          rec.s = cur.s;
          postDecorations(NB_DAY);
          if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
        } else if (last && moved) {
          pushNbUndo();
          rec.x = Math.round(last.x);
          rec.y = Math.round(last.y);
          // 26.9-05: the one she just moved is the one in front.
          bringDecoToFront(rec);
          postDecorations(NB_DAY);
          if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
        } else if (gesture !== 'move') {
          // a twitch on a handle restores the transform and does nothing
          // else — there is no editor behind a handle.
          previewDecoTransform(live, rec, w, h, startA, startS);
          if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
        } else {
          // a <=3px twitch is a TAP, not a drag: restore the exact start
          // so nothing shifts under a plain click, then open the editor.
          el.style.setProperty('--x', String(startX));
          el.style.setProperty('--y', String(startY));
          // 26.9-05: a tap does NOT reorder — only a move does. It still
          // repaints, because that is where the selection outline lands.
          if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
          // 26.9-04: only a hand-text element has anything to type into.
          // Tapping a sticker or a picture selects it and does nothing
          // else — raising an <input> over a photograph would be a typing
          // surface with no field behind it.
          // `el` is deliberately unused by the editor (it positions itself
          // from the RECORD), so the repaint above having detached it is
          // harmless — checked, not assumed.
          if (!rec || rec.kind === 'text') { openHandTextEditor(el, rec); }
        }
      }
      el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
      el.addEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
      el.addEventListener('pointercancel', onCancel);
    });
  }

  // Typing into a hand-text element. A real <input maxlength> raised over
  // the element's slot — the house's own end-to-end cap discipline (the
  // blessing why's 280 maxlength is enforced at the input AND at the
  // server, and this is the same pairing at 80).
  //
  // THERE IS NO HTML SINK ON THIS ELEMENT AT ALL. The stored string
  // reaches the DOM through textContent only, and it leaves the input as
  // a plain string value. contenteditable was rejected for exactly this
  // reason: it would put a paste-shaped markup path on her page.
  function openHandTextEditor(el, rec) {
    if (!NBDESIGN) { return; }
    var scene = $('station-scene');
    if (!scene || scene.querySelector('.page-deco-input')) { return; }
    var input = document.createElement('input');
    input.type = 'text';
    input.className = 'page-deco-input caption-hand';
    input.maxLength = NB_TEXT_CAP;
    input.value = rec.text || '';
    input.setAttribute('aria-label', 'write something');
    input.style.setProperty('--x', String(rec.x));
    input.style.setProperty('--y', String(rec.y));
    input.style.setProperty('--w', String(NB_TEXT_BOX.w));
    input.style.setProperty('--h', String(NB_TEXT_BOX.h));
    var done = false;
    function commit() {
      if (done) { return; }
      done = true;
      pushNbUndo();
      rec.text = String(input.value || '').slice(0, NB_TEXT_CAP);
      if (input.parentNode) { input.parentNode.removeChild(input); }
      postDecorations(NB_DAY);
      if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
    }
    input.addEventListener('blur', commit);
    input.addEventListener('keydown', function (ev) {
      if (ev.key === 'Enter' || ev.key === 'Escape') {
        ev.preventDefault();
        commit();
      }
    });
    scene.appendChild(input);
    input.focus();
    input.select();
  }

  // ---- 26.9-04 (D-11/D-16): THE TIN, THE TRAY, AND THE PICKER ------------
  //
  // ONE TIN, TWO TABS, ONE PICKER. D-11 says explicitly that the sticker
  // sheet and the image picker are the SAME affordance, so building the tray
  // twice would be the second implementation of a shipped rule — which this
  // repo treats as a planning error, not a style preference.
  //
  // D-16: the tray is IN-SCENE, above the tin, and is NEVER the room's
  // right-dock #catalog-panel. That is the same in-scene direction 26.5 set
  // for the whole station: resurfacing IS the room. The mode's body class
  // stays `nb-design` for exactly this reason — `design-mode` would slide
  // the accessory dock over the station (tokens.css:1007).
  // 26.91-02: THE TIN JOINS THE BAND LINE, and its 28-tall departure is
  // RETIRED. The shipped justification read: the tin is taller than the band
  // on purpose, because that extra height is what makes it findable as the
  // entry to decoration WITHOUT A LABEL. The owner's 26.9 verdict retired
  // exactly that claim — she could not find the tin at all (F-6: "an
  // empty-looking thin rectangle outline"). It has a label now (`marks`), so
  // the height it was carrying instead of a name is given back.
  var NB_TIN = { x: 28, y: 196, w: 32, h: 16 };
  var NB_TRAY = { x: 24, y: 96, w: 160, h: 84 };

  // ---- 26.9-06: THE BOTTOM CHROME BAND, AS ONE TABLE ----------------------
  //
  // Every control in the band shares y:196, h:16 — the shipped .station-flip
  // geometry. One baseline, one height, no per-element justification.
  //
  // 26.91-02: THE DECLARED DEPARTURE LIST IS NOW ONE, NOT TWO. Only the
  // save-error row departs, and it departs VERTICALLY (y:184, h:12) so it can
  // share the band's horizontal span without colliding with anything on the
  // line. The tin's departure is retired at its declaration above.
  //
  // REFERENCES rather than re-types the four slots that already exist. A
  // second copy of a slot is the drift this phase keeps finding, and a table
  // whose numbers disagree with the painter is worse than no table.
  var NB_BAND = {
    prev: STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM.prev,
    tin: NB_TIN,
    undo: { x: 64, y: 196, w: 28, h: 16 },
    redo: { x: 96, y: 196, w: 28, h: 16 },
    reset: { x: 128, y: 196, w: 76, h: 16 },
    entry: NB_ENTRY_ROW,
    exit: { x: 212, y: 196, w: 76, h: 16 },
    pen: { x: 292, y: 196, w: 24, h: 16 },
    // 26.91-02: THE BAND RE-LAID ONCE, AND WHAT IT COST.
    //
    // This wave gives every control she could not find a visible lowercase
    // word, and words need width. It also lands the armed `write` tool that
    // the debt recorded at the page canvas had been waiting for — that note
    // said outright that a `write` tool needs a band RE-LAYOUT, and this is
    // it. (Phrased rather than quoted: the banner it refers to is itself a
    // comment, so reproducing its text here would leave the retired string
    // in the file and make the gate that removes it self-invalidating.)
    //
    // WHAT WAS SPENT, digit by digit, so the claim is checkable rather than
    // asserted:
    //
    //   widths  shipped -> now
    //     prev  20        20      (by reference, untouched)
    //     tin   24  ->    32      +8   it gains the word `marks`
    //     undo  16  ->    28     +12   it gains the word `undo`
    //     redo  16  ->    28     +12   it gains the word `redo`
    //     reset 76        76      (26.91-02: at its measured floor for the
    //                              label that shipped THEN, which cleared
    //                              68 px of content box by 4 px and had
    //                              nothing left at w:72. See the 26.91-11
    //                              note below — the floor moved, the slot
    //                              did not.)
    //     exit  96  ->    76     -20   the ONE shipped slot that pays
    //     pen   16  ->    24      +8   it gains the word `pen`
    //     write  -  ->    32     +32   NEW
    //     next  20        20      (by reference, untouched)
    //     sum  284  ->   336     +52
    //
    //   gaps    shipped [4,8,4,4,8,32,32] = 92
    //           now     [4,4,4,4,8,4,4,8] = 40      -52
    //
    //   284 + 92 = 336 + 40 = 376 = 380 - 4 = NB_BOUNDS.x1 - NB_BOUNDS.x0.
    //   The band was geometrically full before and is geometrically full
    //   now; the widening was paid for out of the BAND, never out of the
    //   bound. No `x` was chosen — every one is `previous end + gap`.
    //
    // THE EXCEPTION LIST WAS EMPTY BEFORE AND IS EMPTY AFTER. Every gap in
    // both lists is a member of LADDER = [4,8,12,16,24,32,48]. 26.9-07 had
    // just emptied it by spending the 80-wide middle; this wave does not
    // re-open it.
    //
    // THE TWO 8s ARE THE ONLY GAPS SPENT ON SAFETY RATHER THAN RHYTHM, and
    // each buys a named one:
    //   reset -> exit   separates a control that clears a whole day of
    //                   handmade work from the way out (shipped, preserved).
    //   write -> next   keeps an ARMED tool from sitting 4 px from a page
    //                   flip that clears the undo stack on day change (new).
    // They are the LAST width this band may ever spend, not the first — see
    // the owner's answer to UI-SPEC Open Decision #4 (2026-08-07).
    //
    // 26.91-11 (F-2, owner UAT 2026-08-07): THE RESET LABEL WAS RENAMED AND
    // THE SLOT DID NOT MOVE.
    //
    // She asked for plainer words on the reset control. Her first choice was
    // measured against the app's real font and came in 46% OVER the shipped
    // label, which would have forced either a third re-lay of this band or a
    // second band line; it was rejected ON THE MEASUREMENT. The label that
    // ships is SHORTER than the one it replaced, so the ledger above is
    // untouched: the width sum is still 336, the gap list is still
    // [4,4,4,4,8,4,4,8], and the arranging roster is still 9.
    //
    // Measured under the same pessimistic integer-rounded model that decided
    // every width above (per-glyph advances from the vendored WOFF2, each
    // quantised UP to a whole pixel at font-size 6px):
    //
    //   the label that shipped before   64 px need vs 68 px content =  4 px
    //   the label that ships now        57 px need vs 68 px content = 11 px
    //
    // Content box is `w - 8` (2 border + 6 padding); omitting the border
    // term is the error the UI-SPEC records itself making. The margin went
    // UP by 2.75x. This row is now the ROOMIEST it has ever been, not the
    // tightest.
    //
    // AND IT IS STILL NOT SHRUNK TO 72. The freed 4 px is NOT reclaimed.
    // Open Decision #4's answer was `pre-authorize-ladder-only` and the two
    // 8-px gaps above are recorded as the last width this band may ever
    // spend; taking the 4 px back would be the third re-lay D-09 forbids.
    // A shorter label buys MARGIN here, never width for somebody else.
    write: { x: 320, y: 196, w: 32, h: 16 },
    next: STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM.next,
    // 26.91-27: re-laid LIVE — 180 held 27 of 31 bounded refusals, 316 all.
    error: { x: 64, y: 184, w: 316, h: 12 }
  };
  // The controls that share the band line while ARRANGING, left to right.
  // The entry row is not among them: it is the reading-mode row and its slot
  // overlaps both the reset and the exit rows, which is exactly why the
  // overlap check has to be run over a MODE and not over the whole table.
  //
  // 26.91-02: 8 -> 9. `write` is INSERTED beside its sibling tool; the
  // shipped relative order of all eight existing controls is preserved
  // exactly. Nothing is re-ranked.
  var NB_BAND_ARRANGING = ['prev', 'tin', 'undo', 'redo', 'reset', 'exit',
    'pen', 'write', 'next'];
  var NB_TIN_OPEN = false;
  var NB_TIN_TAB = 'marks';
  // Where a tapped card lands: the open spread's first blessing page and the
  // half it is painted on. Set by paintNotebookSpread, because the tin sits
  // outside any one page and a tap must still choose one.
  var NB_PLACE = null;

  // Drop the tray node without a repaint. Used when a drag STARTS on the
  // page: a full repaint mid-gesture would detach the very element being
  // dragged and the preview would freeze.
  function dismissTray(scene) {
    if (!NB_TIN_OPEN) { return; }
    NB_TIN_OPEN = false;
    var t = scene && scene.querySelector('.station-tin-tray');
    if (t && t.parentNode) { t.parentNode.removeChild(t); }
  }

  // A tapped card lands at the centre of the page the spread is showing.
  // The cap refuses the forty-ninth mark SILENTLY — no error row, no
  // message; the tray stays open and the tap simply does not place.
  function placeFromTray(seed) {
    var target = NB_PLACE;
    if (!target || !NB_DAY) { return; }
    var d = decoDay(NB_DAY);
    if (d.items.length >= NB_DECO_CAP) { return; }
    // 26.9-06: after the cap check, because a refused placement is not an
    // act and an undo that gave her back a page she never changed would be
    // a keystroke that appears to do nothing.
    pushNbUndo();
    // 26.9-06 + F-9: placing something new clears the reset flag. It used to
    // bring the day's hidden records back with it; it now DROPS them, because
    // a reset followed by a new mark is someone starting over. See
    // nbClearResetForEdit for the full reasoning. The original concern — a
    // write landing in a day she cannot see — is still answered: the page is
    // blank and her new mark is the only thing on it.
    nbClearResetForEdit(d);
    var box = (seed.kind === 'sticker' && NB_STICKERS[seed.sprite])
      ? { w: NB_STICKERS[seed.sprite].w, h: NB_STICKER_H }
      : NB_IMG_BOX;
    var p = clampDecoOrigin(
      target.x0 + (192 - box.w) / 2,
      NB_BOUNDS.y0 + (NB_BOUNDS.y1 - NB_BOUNDS.y0 - box.h) / 2,
      box.w, box.h);
    var fresh = { page: target.itemId, kind: seed.kind,
      x: Math.round(p.x), y: Math.round(p.y) };
    if (seed.sprite) { fresh.sprite = seed.sprite; }
    if (seed.ref) { fresh.ref = seed.ref; }
    d.items.push(fresh);
    postDecorations(NB_DAY);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // The crop for one sticker cell, off the single sheet. A name resolves to
  // an OFFSET and nothing else — no path is composed from a stored value at
  // any point, which is strictly stronger than the room's two-layer sprite
  // fence. An off-roster name simply finds no cell and renders nothing.
  function paintStickerCrop(el, name) {
    var cell = NB_STICKERS[name];
    if (!cell) { return false; }
    el.style.backgroundImage = 'url("assets/room/notebook-stickers.png")';
    el.style.backgroundSize = 'calc(' + NB_SHEET_W +
      ' * var(--k) * 1px) calc(' + NB_STICKER_H + ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
    el.style.backgroundPosition = 'calc(' + (-cell.x) +
      ' * var(--k) * 1px) 0';
    el.style.imageRendering = 'pixelated';
    return true;
  }

  // The tin and, when it is open, the tray. Rendered only inside the
  // notebook's design mode — LAW 1 IS SATISFIED BY USER INITIATION, and the
  // picker's grid is FOUR deliberate acts deep: open the notebook, enter
  // design mode, open the tin, choose the pictures tab. Opening the notebook
  // does not open the tin, and opening the tin does not open the pictures
  // tab (the tray lands on `marks`).
  function renderTinTray(scene, items, filters) {
    if (!NBDESIGN) { return; }
    function place(el, r) {
      el.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      el.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      el.style.setProperty('--w', String(r.w));
      el.style.setProperty('--h', String(r.h));
    }
    // 26.91-02: THE TIN GETS THE WORD IT NEVER HAD. F-6, verbatim: an
    // "empty-looking thin rectangle outline". It was two defects at once —
    // no fill (26.91-01: `--wood` was never defined) and no name. This is
    // the second half.
    //
    // `marks`, AND THE ACCESSIBLE NAME NARROWS TO MATCH — the trade, written
    // at the site so it reads as a decision:
    //   - The findability gate requires `aria-label === textContent`. An
    //     accessible name that DISAGREES with the visible name is exactly
    //     the degenerate form that gate exists to close: it lets a control
    //     claim to be named while her eye still sees nothing.
    //   - So `your marks and pictures` cannot survive alongside a visible
    //     `marks`. One of the two had to move, and the visible word is the
    //     one the owner's verdict is about.
    //   - Nothing is lost in the narrowing: the two tabs INSIDE the tray are
    //     themselves labelled `marks` and `pictures`, so the full phrase is
    //     still spoken one level in, where it describes real, separate
    //     targets rather than one box.
    //   - `marks` is also a continuity cue rather than a collision:
    //     NB_TIN_TAB defaults to 'marks', so tapping `marks` opens on the
    //     `marks` drawer. The tin names the box; the tabs name the drawers.
    //
    // 26.91-36 (F-24 / D-11, HER RULING AT `A-21` PART 1): ONE TOOL AT A TIME.
    // While the pen or `write` is armed the tin is GREYED AND UNPRESSABLE. She
    // chose that over *pressing the tin disarms the armed tool*, so a press
    // here must leave the armed flag alone and open nothing.
    //
    // DERIVED, NOT TOGGLED, and that is the whole design. The obvious build is
    // to grey the tin at each arm site and un-grey it at each disarm site —
    // and there are FIVE places that write these flags, one of which (the
    // station pop, app.js:5876) deliberately never zeroes them. A chip that
    // greys and never comes back is WORSE than the defect it fixes. Computing
    // the state here, where the tin is painted fresh on every repaint, leaves
    // no residual to strand: stranding is unreachable by construction rather
    // than merely unobserved.
    //
    // BOTH HALVES, AND NEITHER IS REDUNDANT — MEASURED LIVE, NOT ASSUMED.
    // `station-nb-off` is the band's shipped disabled treatment (nbGlyphState;
    // opacity 0.45 + pointer-events none) and it closes the POINTER route
    // only: with the class alone the node still reports `disabled` false and
    // stays keyboard-activatable, a dead-looking control with an open door.
    // The native attribute alone paints NOTHING, because `.station-tin` is not
    // a `.btn` and has no `:disabled` rule. Her word was UNPRESSABLE.
    var tin = document.createElement('button');
    tin.type = 'button';
    var tinOff = !!(NB_PEN || NB_WRITE);
    tin.className = 'station-tin' + (tinOff ? ' station-nb-off' : '');
    tin.setAttribute('data-nb-off', tinOff ? '1' : '0');
    tin.disabled = tinOff;
    tin.setAttribute('aria-label', 'marks');
    tin.textContent = 'marks';
    place(tin, NB_TIN);
    tin.addEventListener('click', function () {
      // tapping the tin again closes the tray
      NB_TIN_OPEN = !NB_TIN_OPEN;
      if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
    });
    scene.appendChild(tin);
    if (!NB_TIN_OPEN) { return; }

    var tray = document.createElement('div');
    tray.className = 'station-tin-tray';
    place(tray, NB_TRAY);
    scene.appendChild(tray);

    var head = document.createElement('div');
    head.className = 'tray-head';
    tray.appendChild(head);
    ['marks', 'pictures'].forEach(function (tab) {
      var b = document.createElement('button');
      b.type = 'button';
      b.className = 'station-caption-add tray-tab' +
        (NB_TIN_TAB === tab ? ' tray-tab-on' : '');
      b.textContent = tab;
      b.setAttribute('aria-label', tab);
      b.addEventListener('click', function () {
        // tapping the SAME tab twice closes the tray
        if (NB_TIN_TAB === tab) { NB_TIN_OPEN = false; } else {
          NB_TIN_TAB = tab;
        }
        if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
      });
      head.appendChild(b);
    });

    // The grid fills IN PLACE and there is no spinner — 26.7's finding that
    // a consent card read as a spinner is the precedent this avoids. Both
    // grids are already-resolved local state; neither issues a fetch.
    var box = document.createElement('div');
    box.className = 'tray-grid';
    tray.appendChild(box);

    if (NB_TIN_TAB === 'marks') {
      // The marks tab CAN NEVER BE EMPTY — it is compiled art with a floor
      // of six core stickers. A 404 on the sheet leaves these names visible
      // over transparent cells: fail-VISIBLE, so the tray stays usable by
      // label (the shipped art-last posture).
      Object.keys(NB_STICKERS).forEach(function (name) {
        var card = document.createElement('button');
        card.type = 'button';
        card.className = 'tray-card';
        card.setAttribute('aria-label', name);
        var art = document.createElement('div');
        art.className = 'tray-card-art';
        art.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
        art.style.width = 'calc(' + NB_STICKERS[name].w +
          ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
        paintStickerCrop(art, name);
        card.appendChild(art);
        var label = document.createElement('span');
        label.className = 'tray-card-name';
        label.textContent = name;
        card.appendChild(label);
        card.addEventListener('click', function () {
          placeFromTray({ kind: 'sticker', sprite: name });
        });
        box.appendChild(card);
      });
      return;
    }

    // THE PICKER. The gate is consumed INSIDE the selector body, and every
    // id it returns is re-checked here through the INDEPENDENT render guard
    // — the shipped renderShelf/renderAlbum D-13 pattern, not a second
    // exclusion rule.
    var store = items || {};
    var ids = StudyCore.pickPickerImages(store, filters, Date.now());
    ids = ids.filter(function (id) {
      var reason = StudyCore.guardSurface(store[id], filters);
      if (reason !== null) {
        console.warn('held back before display (picker, ' + reason + ')');
        recordIncident(id, 'picker', reason);
        return false;
      }
      return true;
    });
    if (ids.length === 0) {
      // fail-open, and the SHIPPED string reused verbatim — an unreachable
      // read and a genuinely empty library look the same here, deliberately:
      // no error chrome, tabs still switchable.
      var quiet = document.createElement('p');
      quiet.className = 'tray-empty';
      quiet.textContent = EMPTY_QUIET_LINE;
      box.appendChild(quiet);
      return;
    }
    ids.forEach(function (id) {
      var cell = document.createElement('button');
      cell.type = 'button';
      cell.className = 'tray-picture';
      cell.setAttribute('aria-label', 'a picture');
      var img = document.createElement('img');
      // NODE-BUILT, NOT AN HTML STRING. The shipped polaroid uses
      // escapeAttr + encodeURIComponent into innerHTML; here setAttribute
      // never parses HTML at all, so the escape half is not merely
      // unnecessary but WRONG (it would corrupt the URL). The encode half
      // is kept and is the whole of the sink.
      img.setAttribute('src', '/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(id));
      img.setAttribute('alt', '');
      img.setAttribute('loading', 'lazy');
      cell.appendChild(img);
      cell.addEventListener('click', function () {
        placeFromTray({ kind: 'image', ref: id });
      });
      box.appendChild(cell);
    });
  }

  // ---- 26.9-06: THE BAND'S ARRANGING CONTROLS ----------------------------
  //
  // The two undo glyphs, in-scene at their pinned slots. They are NOT the
  // room's #design-undo / #design-redo buttons and they are NOT wired to the
  // room's stack — see the D-15 block above setNotebookDesign for why the
  // stack is separate while the keys are shared.
  //
  // They reuse the SHIPPED .station-flip class, so this phase adds no second
  // user of the 700 weight (the UI-SPEC's typography contract closes that at
  // one). .station-fixture carries the positioning and the shipped focus
  // ring; the glyphs carry no data-id, so `body.nb-design
  // .station-fixture[data-id]` leaves them live while arranging.
  function renderNotebookBand(scene) {
    if (!NBDESIGN) { return; }
    function place(el, r) {
      el.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      el.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      el.style.setProperty('--w', String(r.w));
      el.style.setProperty('--h', String(r.h));
    }
    // 26.91-02: THE WORD IS THE LABEL. spec[0] is now both the visible name
    // and the accessible name — the glyph column is gone, and with it the
    // two arrow characters. The `aria-label` had said `undo` / `redo` since
    // 26.9-06; a screen reader always knew what these were and her eye
    // never did. NN/g states the rule outright: icon labels should be
    // visible at all times, without any interaction from the user.
    [['undo', NB_UNDO, doNbUndo],
      ['redo', NB_REDO, doNbRedo]].forEach(function (spec) {
      var b = document.createElement('button');
      b.type = 'button';
      b.setAttribute('aria-label', spec[0]);
      b.setAttribute('data-nb-stack', spec[0]);
      b.textContent = spec[0];
      // ONE rule for the disabled look, shared with updateNbButtons.
      nbGlyphState(b, spec[1]);
      place(b, NB_BAND[spec[0]]);
      b.addEventListener('click', function () { spec[2](); });
      scene.appendChild(b);
    });

    // 26.9-07: THE PEN. A TOGGLE, not a placement — it does not put anything
    // on the page, it changes what a pointerdown on the page means. It wears
    // the same .station-flip glyph face the undo/redo glyphs do and carries
    // no data-id, so `body.nb-design .station-fixture[data-id]` leaves it
    // live while arranging.
    //
    // ITS LIVE STATE IS AN INVERTED CHIP, NEVER THE ACCENT. The UI-SPEC's
    // accent list is closed at two and this is not on it, so `is-on` swaps
    // --ink and --card — two tokens already in the scene rather than a third.
    //
    // 26.91-02: IT IS A WORD NOW, AND station-nb-pen SURVIVES ONLY AS THE
    // is-on HOOK. It joins the band's one type register and loses
    // .station-fixture / .station-flip (and with them the 700 weight and the
    // coral hover ring — the accent list is closed at two and this is on
    // neither). `pen` rather than `draw`: it is the shipped aria-label, so
    // the visible name, the accessible name, the source and the suites stay
    // ONE string. A bare verb is weaker copy than `arrange this day`, and
    // that trade is taken knowingly for all four verbs at once.
    var pen = document.createElement('button');
    pen.type = 'button';
    pen.className = 'station-caption-add station-nb-row station-nb-pen' +
      (NB_PEN ? ' is-on' : '');
    pen.setAttribute('aria-label', 'pen');
    pen.setAttribute('aria-pressed', NB_PEN ? 'true' : 'false');
    pen.textContent = 'pen';
    place(pen, NB_BAND.pen);
    pen.addEventListener('click', function () { setNotebookPen(!NB_PEN); });
    scene.appendChild(pen);

    // 26.91-02: THE ARMED `write` TOOL, immediately after the pen — its
    // sibling in the reading order as well as in behaviour.
    //
    // It follows the pen's node shape EXACTLY: a real <button>, the band's
    // one type register plus an `is-on` hook, aria-pressed, and a click that
    // TOGGLES rather than arms (a control that could only ever be armed
    // would leave her no way back to plain arranging). It shares the pen's
    // `.is-on` inversion rule in tokens.css rather than getting its own —
    // two tools that behave identically must look identical, or the band
    // teaches that they differ.
    //
    // The 8-px gap between this and `next` is deliberate and is one of only
    // two gaps in the band spent on safety rather than rhythm: an ARMED tool
    // must not sit 4 px from a page flip that clears the undo stack on day
    // change.
    var write = document.createElement('button');
    write.type = 'button';
    write.className = 'station-caption-add station-nb-row station-nb-write' +
      (NB_WRITE ? ' is-on' : '');
    write.setAttribute('aria-label', 'write');
    write.setAttribute('aria-pressed', NB_WRITE ? 'true' : 'false');
    write.textContent = 'write';
    place(write, NB_BAND.write);
    write.addEventListener('click', function () {
      setNotebookWrite(!NB_WRITE);
    });
    scene.appendChild(write);

    // THE RESET ROW.
    //
    // 26.9 F-13 (the owner, 2026-08-06): "the yes/no can be shown as a pop up
    // window instead."
    //
    // THIS REVERSES A DOCUMENTED DECISION, DELIBERATELY AND ON THE OWNER'S
    // CALL. The original read: "Two taps, in place: NO MODAL AND NO SCRIM. A
    // modal would be the friction that loses fragile users (loveable over
    // complete)." That reasoning was aimed at SETUP friction — walls of
    // configuration, CLI steps — and it does not transfer to a destructive
    // confirmation, where a moment of friction is the entire point. The
    // in-place flip also cost real usability twice over: it forced the row to
    // reserve width for the armed sentence, which is what made the resting
    // label sit off-centre (F-12), and the confirmation appeared as a few
    // small words inside a control she had just tapped, which is easy to miss.
    //
    // One-tap-destroys stays wrong for the same reason it always was: this
    // clears handmade work across a whole day. So the two-step survives; only
    // its presentation changes.
    //
    // `yes` REMAINS the phase's only use of the destructive colour, so that
    // discipline is carried across rather than dropped in the move.
    var row = document.createElement('div');
    row.className = 'station-caption-add station-nb-row station-nb-reset';
    place(row, NB_BAND.reset);
    function word(text, cls, onTap) {
      var b = document.createElement('button');
      b.type = 'button';
      b.className = 'station-nb-word' + (cls ? ' ' + cls : '');
      b.textContent = text;
      b.setAttribute('aria-label', text);
      b.addEventListener('click', onTap);
      row.appendChild(b);
    }
    function say(text) {
      var s = document.createElement('span');
      s.textContent = text;
      row.appendChild(s);
    }
    // The row is now ALWAYS one tap target carrying one pinned label, armed
    // or not — the confirmation has moved off it and into the panel below.
    word(NB_RESET_COPY, '', function () {
      NB_RESET_ARMED = true;
      if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
    });
    scene.appendChild(row);

    // THE CONFIRMATION PANEL. Painted only while armed, so the calm scene
    // carries no extra node and nothing can be hit by accident.
    if (NB_RESET_ARMED) {
      var scrim = document.createElement('div');
      scrim.className = 'station-nb-scrim';
      // Tapping outside is a DECLINE, never a confirm — the safe reading of
      // an ambiguous gesture. Same treatment as `no`.
      scrim.addEventListener('click', function () { nbDisarmReset(); });

      var panel = document.createElement('div');
      panel.className = 'station-nb-confirm';
      panel.setAttribute('role', 'dialog');
      panel.setAttribute('aria-modal', 'true');
      panel.setAttribute('aria-label', NB_RESET_COPY + '?');
      // A click inside the panel must not reach the scrim behind it.
      panel.addEventListener('click', function (ev) { ev.stopPropagation(); });

      var q = document.createElement('p');
      q.className = 'station-nb-confirm-q';
      // Assembled from the SAME NB_RESET_COPY the row uses, so the question
      // and the control it came from cannot drift apart.
      q.textContent = NB_RESET_COPY + '?';
      panel.appendChild(q);

      var says = document.createElement('p');
      says.className = 'station-nb-confirm-says';
      // 26.91-18: THIS SENTENCE IS HERS, VERBATIM (the owner, 2026-08-09) — her
      // verdict on the shipped copy was "sounds quite weird". Do not reword,
      // re-punctuate or fit it to a house style.
      //
      // OWNER-ACCEPTED COST, SO IT IS NOT RE-ARGUED: the sentence it replaces
      // carried the panel's only statement that the act was RECOVERABLE. She
      // was SHOWN THAT BEFORE CHOOSING and chose this. A deliberate trade,
      // not an omission — the remedy still exists, only the sentence about it
      // is gone. That clause is named BY CONCEPT and NEVER QUOTED: this
      // commit greps this file for it. 26.91-11's naming finding survives it
      // and is carried in the gate's own message.
      says.textContent =
        'are you sure you want to remove all of your edits?';
      panel.appendChild(says);

      var choices = document.createElement('div');
      choices.className = 'station-nb-confirm-choices';
      function choice(text, cls, onTap) {
        var b = document.createElement('button');
        b.type = 'button';
        b.className = 'station-nb-word' + (cls ? ' ' + cls : '');
        b.textContent = text;
        b.setAttribute('aria-label', text);
        b.addEventListener('click', onTap);
        choices.appendChild(b);
        return b;
      }
      choice('yes', 'station-nb-yes', function () {
        NB_RESET_ARMED = false;
        nbResetDay(NB_DAY);
      });
      choice('no', '', function () { nbDisarmReset(); });
      panel.appendChild(choices);

      scrim.appendChild(panel);
      scene.appendChild(scrim);
    }

    // ---- THE ONE PLACE THIS PHASE SPEAKS AN ERROR ----------------------
    //
    // DELIBERATELY LOUDER THAN THE ROOM. The room's postLayout only warns
    // to the console, and that is right for a lamp that can be dragged back
    // in a second. This is her handmade work: losing it quietly is worse
    // than showing an error, because she would have no way to know what was
    // lost or when.
    //
    // LOCAL STATE IS KEPT AND NEVER REVERTED. postDecorations already sets
    // the flag without touching DECORATIONS — this row is the surface that
    // finally consumes it, and 26.9-03 declared the flag pointing here.
    // The WHOLE ROW is the retry target, and the copy is the house's one
    // save-failure line reused VERBATIM: the string already occurs at ten
    // sites in this file, so reusing it is reuse rather than authoring.
    // 26.91-27 (F-23 b): that line is NOT retired, it became the FALLBACK;
    // when the server said WHY the row says it, capped, textContent only.
    if (NB_SAVE_FAILED) {
      var err = document.createElement('button');
      err.type = 'button';
      err.className = 'station-caption-add station-nb-row station-nb-error';
      var says = NB_SAVE_REASON || "couldn't save — try again.";
      if (says.length > NB_SAVE_REASON_CAP) {
        says = says.slice(0, NB_SAVE_REASON_CAP - 1) + '…';
      }
      err.textContent = says;
      err.setAttribute('aria-label', says);
      place(err, NB_BAND.error);
      err.addEventListener('click', function () {
        // clear BOTH first, then retry: either left up repaints a failure
        // that never ends, or speaks the last one's words over the next.
        NB_SAVE_FAILED = false;
        NB_SAVE_REASON = null;
        postDecorations(NB_DAY);
        if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
      });
      scene.appendChild(err);
      // ---- 26.91-38 (D-13 / F-26): AN OUTLINE TO POINT AT ----------------
      //
      // HER RULING (`26.91-CONTEXT.md` `A-26` ruling 3, 2026-08-11): SHOW THE
      // OUTLINE ONLY WHILE THE ROOM IS REFUSING. Chosen over *draw the
      // outline on reset days too* and over *use different words on that one
      // path*, and chosen after she was told this option has the most moving
      // parts of the three.
      //
      // WHAT IT IS FOR. The row directly above can say `move that mark inside
      // the page outline.` on a day where paintPageDecorations returned at its
      // reset guard, so NO page of that day drew an outline — the sentence
      // then names something that is not on the screen. Five steps, all
      // shipped behaviour: a day holds a mark stored outside the page bounds
      // (a record written before wave 26 clamped the origin, or a hand-edited
      // store); she taps reset day; the reset posts the SURVIVING records,
      // out-of-bounds one included; the server refuses; and every page has
      // already returned early.
      //
      // ⚠ THE CONDITION IS A NODE COUNT AND NOT `rec.reset`, DELIBERATELY.
      // The observable fact her sentence needs is *there is no outline on
      // screen*, and that is directly countable. A reset-flag test would
      // close exactly one path; the count closes TWO with one rule, because
      // a page the never-show fence dropped is never painted either — same
      // observable fact, different cause. (A third path stays open and is
      // named rather than narrowed away: a mark on a spread she is NOT
      // looking at leaves an outline on screen that is not the one the
      // message means. No rule here can see it, because the server's refusal
      // carries a coordinate and a bound and never a record identity. It is
      // recorded in deferred-items.md `D-13` and driven by the `G-31`
      // residual rows. (⚠ That last sentence used to name the rows with a
      // trailing star. A star immediately after a slash OPENS A BLOCK COMMENT
      // — and `stripComments`, which every source gate in this repo runs
      // first, pairs it with the next closer and silently swallowed the whole
      // painter. Two shipped positive controls went red on a correct file.
      // Names in comments here are written out, never globbed.)
      //
      // WHY HERE. paintNotebookSpread paints calendar -> pages -> band -> tin
      // — MEASURED by driving, pinned by `G-31/order/band-paints-after-every-
      // page`, never read off the source. The count is FINAL at this point,
      // which is what keeps `G-27/live-app/one-region-while-arranging` true
      // with a second call site: on an ordinary day a region is already here
      // and this adds none. Evaluated earlier it would see zero, append one,
      // and the page painter would append a second — a page making two
      // claims about where a mark may go.
      //
      // IT GOES AWAY BY DERIVATION, NOT BY REMEMBERING. The condition is read
      // at paint time from NB_SAVE_FAILED, so clearing the refusal and
      // repainting removes it with nothing to tear down — the same shape
      // 26.91-36 used for the chip, for the same reason.
      //
      // A RESET DAY STILL LOOKS BLANK. This draws chrome; it does NOT make
      // the page render her marks. Painting them would undo the reset's whole
      // meaning while claiming to help her, and law 3 forbids the room
      // getting worse in the act of explaining itself.
      if (scene.querySelectorAll('.page-deco-region').length === 0) {
        nbPaintMarkRegion(scene);
      }
    }
  }

  // ---- 26.91-02: ONE TEXT-PLACEMENT TAIL, TWO CALLERS ---------------------
  //
  // The armed `write` pointerdown and the shipped `dblclick` both create a
  // text record at a tapped point. They are ONE implementation, not two that
  // agree today: a duplicated creation path would diverge the first time
  // either was touched, and the divergence would be silent because both
  // would keep producing a record.

  // The tapped point, in spread coordinates, clamped into the page interior.
  // `--k` is read from the LIVE computed style rather than assumed, because
  // the scene scales and a hard-coded 1 would place every mark at the wrong
  // point at every zoom except the smallest.
  function nbTextOriginFrom(e, scene, canvas, side) {
    var k = parseInt(
      getComputedStyle(scene).getPropertyValue('--k'), 10) || 1;
    var box = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    return clampDecoOrigin(
      (side === 'right' ? 192 : 0) + (e.clientX - box.left) / k,
      NB_BOUNDS.y0 + (e.clientY - box.top) / k,
      NB_TEXT_BOX.w, NB_TEXT_BOX.h);
  }

  // The creation tail itself. Returns the record, or null if the cap
  // refused it.
  function nbPlaceTextRecord(scene, entry, day, p) {
    var d = decoDay(day);
    // A hit cap silently refuses the next placement rather than erroring —
    // the page keeps working and says nothing (UI-SPEC A7). Shipped
    // behaviour, and it now covers BOTH creation paths rather than one.
    if (d.items.length >= NB_DECO_CAP) { return null; }
    pushNbUndo();
    var fresh = { page: entry.itemId, kind: 'text',
      x: Math.round(p.x), y: Math.round(p.y), text: '' };
    d.items.push(fresh);
    postDecorations(day);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
    // the element persists as a REAL element even while untyped, so it
    // can be selected, moved and (later) undone like any other — it is
    // never a ghost that vanishes if she does not type (UI-SPEC E5).
    var node = scene.querySelector(
      '.page-deco-text[data-deco-idx="' +
      String(d.items.indexOf(fresh)) + '"]');
    if (node) { openHandTextEditor(node, fresh); }
    return fresh;
  }

  // The page canvas's pointerdown, as a NAMED factory rather than an inline
  // closure — so the behaviour it guards can be DRIVEN by a test rather than
  // only greped for. 26.9's lesson was that a painted control wired to
  // nothing leaves every structural assertion green.
  //
  // ORDER IS THE CONTRACT HERE, not an implementation detail:
  //   1. not arranging          -> nothing at all;
  //   2. the pen owns the tap   -> return BEFORE any creation. Two armed
  //                               tools contending for one pointerdown is
  //                               T-26.91-07;
  //   3. the shipped tray close -> runs for every tap, armed or not;
  //   4. `write` armed          -> and only then, one record.
  function nbCanvasPointerHandler(scene, canvas, entry, day, side) {
    return function (e) {
      if (!NBDESIGN) { return; }
      // The pen has its own capture-phase listener and owns the gesture
      // outright. This returns before the tray close as well, deliberately:
      // a stroke that begins over an open tray must not also dismiss it
      // mid-gesture.
      if (NB_PEN) { return; }
      // 26.9-04: a tap on the page closes the tray.
      if (NB_TIN_OPEN) {
        NB_TIN_OPEN = false;
        if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
      }
      if (!NB_WRITE) { return; }
      // 26.91-23 (F-22/A-20). ARMED ONLY HERE, where we are provably inside a
      // pointerdown, so the mousedown it waits for is the one this gesture is
      // about to produce. The reasoning is in tokens.css.
      nbGuardEditorFocus();
      nbPlaceTextRecord(scene, entry, day,
        nbTextOriginFrom(e, scene, canvas, side));
    };
  }

  function nbGuardEditorFocus() {
    document.addEventListener('mousedown', function g(ev) {
      document.removeEventListener('mousedown', g, true);
      var el = document.activeElement;
      if (el && el.classList && el.classList.contains('page-deco-input')) {
        ev.preventDefault();
      }
    }, true);
  }

  // ---- 26.9-05: THE ONE THING IN THIS PHASE THAT CANNOT BE CHECKED -------
  //
  // Written here, in the file, because it is a property of THIS function and
  // whoever next edits it deserves to meet it before they invent a rule to
  // fix it.
  //
  // 1. D-02 REMOVED THE LEGIBILITY GUARANTEE, DELIBERATELY AND ON THE
  //    OWNER'S RECORD. Overlap on this canvas is fully free: anything may
  //    sit over anything.
  // 2. THERE IS THEREFORE NO MACHINE-CHECKABLE RULE THAT THE PAGE IS STILL
  //    READABLE, AND NONE IS INVENTED HERE. Do not add a coverage
  //    percentage, an occlusion ratio, or any other heuristic that guesses
  //    at readability. It would be a machine deciding what only she can
  //    decide, and it would also be this phase's named defect class in its
  //    purest form: a check that looks rigorous and measures nothing.
  // 3. A DECORATION MAY FULLY COVER THE WHY TEXT. That is the accepted
  //    consequence, not an oversight, and it is carried as a flagged
  //    assumption rather than asserted away.
  // 4. THE NET IS THE DAY RESET, which is why the reset's confirmation is
  //    two-tap and its representation is a FLAG rather than a delete — the
  //    records survive underneath a reset day (see the early return below),
  //    and that survival is the only thing that makes a reset undoable.
  //    Nothing here may make the reset unable to restore a page in full.
  // 5. IT IS VERIFIED ONLY BY THE BLOCKING OWNER VERDICT in the phase's
  //    UAT. Her eye is the instrument; there is no other.
  //
  // What CAN be checked is checked, and is: the readable spine never moves
  // (date, title and why keep their STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM slots), and this
  // layer only ever APPENDS nodes — it never rewrites, reflows, summarises
  // or filters the content it sits beside (law 4).
  //
  // Her layer on ONE page. Called from paintBlessingPage, so a page that
  // the fence dropped is never painted and its decorations go silently
  // with it (D-06) — no gap, no placeholder, no explanation field exists
  // in the model at all.
  //
  // 26.9-04: `items`, `filters` and `guard` come IN AS ARGUMENTS, the
  // packBlessingsToc contract verbatim, SO THE FENCE RUNS FRESH ON EVERY
  // RENDER. A placed picture is never resolved once at placement time and
  // trusted afterwards: marking its item never-show removes it from the page
  // at the very next render, silently. Fail-CLOSED on a missing guard — an
  // image record whose fence cannot be consulted does not render.
  function paintPageDecorations(scene, entry, side, items, filters, guard) {
    var day = entry.dayLabel;
    var rec = DECORATIONS[day];
    // D-15: a reset day renders the auto composition and ignores the
    // records, which survive underneath (that survival is the only thing
    // that makes reset undoable, and it is why reset is a FLAG and never
    // a delete — nobody may later "simplify" it).
    if (rec && rec.reset) { return; }
    // The placement canvas: inert while reading, live while arranging.
    // Its half of the spread decides OWNERSHIP (which page a new mark
    // belongs to).
    //
    // 26.91-10: the drag clamp above is NO LONGER spread-wide. Per the
    // owner's `right-page-only` ruling of 2026-08-08 (26.91-CONTEXT.md
    // A-12), which RETIRES **26.9 D-05's** gutter-straddle clause
    // (`26.9-CONTEXT.md:37`), a placed mark's origin cannot be dragged left
    // of the gutter — the left half of every spread is the month grid now.
    // (**26.91 D-05**, the law-5 naming fallback, is a different decision
    // and is not affected.)
    var canvas = document.createElement('div');
    canvas.className = 'page-deco-canvas';
    canvas.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    canvas.style.setProperty('--x', side === 'right' ? '192' : '0');
    canvas.style.setProperty('--y', String(NB_BOUNDS.y0));
    canvas.style.setProperty('--w', '192');
    canvas.style.setProperty('--h', String(NB_BOUNDS.y1 - NB_BOUNDS.y0));
    // 26.9 F-7 (owner-found, 2026-08-06): a PLAIN tap must not create
    // anything. It closes the tray and deselects, nothing more.
    //
    // THE BUG THIS FIXES: 26.9-03 shipped tap-the-page-interior as its
    // PLACEHOLDER text gesture and said so in its own SUMMARY — "Plan 04
    // owns the real affordance and may replace the placement gesture."
    // 26.9-04 shipped the tin and never removed the placeholder, so every
    // deselect tap in design mode minted another empty 'write something'.
    // The owner hit it immediately: six of them on one page, from normal
    // clicking. A creation gesture bound to the most common idle click is
    // the wrong default in any editor. THAT REMAINS TRUE: a plain,
    // unarmed tap still creates nothing, and the assertions in 9o drive
    // that case explicitly rather than trusting it.
    //
    // ---- 26.91-02: THE DEBT CLOSURE -----------------------------------
    //
    // the debt recorded here is PAID BY THE COMMIT CARRYING THIS EDIT.
    //
    // This site used to carry two notes: the double-click rationale, and a
    // debt tied to F-6 saying the gesture was not discoverable and had to
    // be replaced by an armed `write` tool beside the pen once the band was
    // re-laid out. Both are retired, and neither is quoted here on purpose
    // — they were comments, so reproducing their banner text would leave
    // the retired strings in the file and make the gate that removes them
    // agree with itself.
    //
    // 26.91-02 re-laid the band to nine slots and landed that tool. Text
    // placement is now an armed, named, visible control, so the reason the
    // debt existed is gone. A recorded debt is closed in the commit that
    // pays it, and the closure is written down rather than deleted — the
    // next reader gets the history, not a silence where it used to be.
    //
    // ---- 26.91-02 ARGUED THE DOUBLE-CLICK PATH SHOULD BE KEPT ALIVE ----
    //
    // ITS ARGUMENT IS PRESERVED HERE RATHER THAN DELETED, so the next reader
    // meets a claim that was overturned instead of a silence where the
    // history used to be. What it said, in its own terms: the path
    // "costs nothing, it costs no discoverability now that the band names
    // the tool, and removing it would be an unforced behaviour change to a
    // gesture she may already have learned."
    //
    // ---- RETIRED 2026-08-11 (26.91-37, F-25 / D-12) --------------------
    //
    // THE ARGUMENT WAS WRONG ON ITS FIRST CLAUSE, AND A MEASUREMENT SAYS SO
    // RATHER THAN A READING. `G-30` drove BOTH callers across five input
    // shapes before either changed:
    //
    //   write armed  + pointerdown -> 1 record   (a DELIBERATE arming act)
    //   nothing armed+ pointerdown -> 0 records
    //   pen armed    + pointerdown -> 0 records
    //   nothing armed+ dblclick    -> 1 record   <== no arming act at all
    //   pen armed    + dblclick    -> 1 record   <== and mid-drawing too
    //
    // IT DID NOT COST NOTHING. It cost a mark she did not ask for. This
    // listener consulted NEITHER armed flag — only NBDESIGN — so a plain
    // select-then-select, which produces exactly this event shape, minted an
    // empty `write something` record on her page. That is `A-20`'s invariant
    // — the app NEVER mints a mark she did not ask for — failing in the same
    // round that shipped the `first-tap` ruling resting on it. Recorded as
    // `D-12`, found by HER during the fourth seal session.
    //
    // HER RULING (`26.91-CONTEXT.md` `A-26`, ruling 2, 2026-08-11): remove
    // the stray empty mark AND close the path that minted it. Chosen over
    // *leave it, decide later* and over *close the path, leave the mark*.
    //
    // THE ACCEPTED COST, STATED RATHER THAN ABSORBED: a double-click on the
    // page now places nothing. She did not ask for that gesture to go — she
    // asked for the path that mints unasked marks to be closed, and this is
    // which path that turned out to be. It is routed to her at the seventh
    // seal as its own beat.
    //
    // ⚠ `A-20` IS NOT REOPENED. Only a listener REGISTRATION was removed.
    // `nbPlaceTextRecord` and its whole tail — the cap refusal, the undo
    // push, the post and the editor it opens — are byte-unchanged, because
    // the surviving gesture uses every one of them. A placed-but-untyped
    // mark still persists as a real element (UI-SPEC `empty | E4`); this
    // change touches WHICH GESTURE MAY CREATE, never whether a created
    // record survives.
    canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown',
      nbCanvasPointerHandler(scene, canvas, entry, day, side));
    scene.appendChild(canvas);

    // 26.91-30 (F-26): THE HONEST LINE, on its own inert element so the box
    // above — the placement and pen target — does not move with it. INCLUSIVE
    // bounds, so the span is a COUNT: x1 - x0 + 1. Argument in tokens.css.
    // 26.91-38: THE ORIGINAL CALL SITE, unchanged in behaviour — the same
    // condition, the same box, now through the one builder above.
    if (NBDESIGN && side === 'right') { nbPaintMarkRegion(scene); }

    // 26.91-23 (F-22/A-20): her words VERBATIM. Reasoning in tokens.css. The
    // literal sits at its one use site rather than in a named constant: this
    // painter is extracted and evaluated in isolation by the fake-DOM rig, so
    // a module-level name would be a free variable there.
    if (NB_WRITE && !scene.querySelector('.page-deco-prompt')) {
      var prompt = document.createElement('div');
      prompt.className = 'page-deco-prompt caption-hand';
      prompt.textContent = 'tap where you want to write';
      prompt.style.setProperty('--x', String(NB_BOUNDS.x0));
      prompt.style.setProperty('--y', String(NB_BOUNDS.y0));
      prompt.style.setProperty('--w', String(NB_BOUNDS.x1 - NB_BOUNDS.x0));
      prompt.style.setProperty('--h', '12');
      scene.appendChild(prompt);
    }

    var store = items || {};
    var all = decoDay(day).items;
    for (var i = 0; i < all.length; i++) {
      var r = all[i];
      if (!r || r.page !== entry.itemId) { continue; }
      var btn = null;
      var bw = NB_TEXT_BOX.w;
      var bh = NB_TEXT_BOX.h;
      if (r.kind === 'text') {
        btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        // NEVER .station-fixture: that class carries a hover/focus accent
        // ring (tokens.css:1726-1727), and overlapping decorations wearing
        // it would stack accent rings all over the page while editing.
        btn.className = 'page-deco page-deco-text caption-hand';
        // the ONE sink: textContent. The prompt copy shows before she types
        // and is never stored.
        btn.textContent = r.text || 'write something';
        // 26.9-05: THE ONE EXCEPTION to the aria-hidden rule below. This is
        // HER WRITING, not chrome, so it keeps its accessible name.
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', r.text || 'write something');
      } else if (r.kind === 'sticker') {
        var cell = NB_STICKERS[r.sprite];
        if (!cell) { continue; }   // an off-roster name renders nothing
        bw = cell.w;
        bh = NB_STICKER_H;
        btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        // .page-deco-sprite KEEPS pixel rendering, because it IS pixel art.
        // That is the opposite case to a picked photograph below, and the
        // two must stay opposite — do not blanket either rule.
        btn.className = 'page-deco page-deco-sprite';
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', r.sprite);
        paintStickerCrop(btn, r.sprite);
      } else if (r.kind === 'image') {
        // THE FENCE, RE-RESOLVED ON EVERY RENDER, with the guard passed in
        // as an argument. A non-null reason drops the record SILENTLY: no
        // gap node, no placeholder, no count, and no explanation field
        // exists in the model to put one in.
        var reason = (typeof guard === 'function')
          ? guard(store[r.ref], filters) : 'missing';
        if (reason !== null) {
          if (r.ref) { recordIncident(r.ref, 'notebook-page', reason); }
          continue;
        }
        bw = NB_IMG_BOX.w;
        bh = NB_IMG_BOX.h;
        btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        // renders AUTO, never pixelated — content imagery is never
        // filtered (law 4). The rule shipped in 26.9-01.
        btn.className = 'page-deco page-deco-img';
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'a picture');
        var pic = document.createElement('img');
        pic.setAttribute('src', '/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(r.ref));
        pic.setAttribute('alt', '');
        pic.setAttribute('loading', 'lazy');
        btn.appendChild(pic);
      } else if (r.kind === 'photo') {
        // 26.9-09 (D-01's other half): ONE BRANCH, and this is it.
        //
        // THE SOURCE IS BUILT FROM THE ENTRY BEING PAINTED — the page's own
        // item — and NEVER from a stored reference field. That is the whole
        // of this kind's safety claim (T-26.9-41): the record carries no
        // `ref` at all, because the page key already IS the reference, so
        // this adds no new reference surface and the fence family needs no
        // extension. The picture branch above is the ONLY fence call in this
        // function and it stays the only one.
        //
        // THE LAYER CHANGES, DELIBERATELY. The polaroid moves from the
        // furniture layer (.station-photo, z 1) to HER layer
        // (.page-deco, calc(3 + var(--i))), because the array position IS
        // the draw order and the one shipped reordering gesture moves a
        // record to the END of the array. A photograph left on the furniture
        // layer would be the single mark she can move but can never bring to
        // the front — a rule nothing in the interface expresses.
        var pbox = decoBox(r);
        bw = pbox.w;
        bh = pbox.h;
        btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        // renders AUTO, never pixelated — this is the same photograph the
        // pinned slot was showing a moment ago and it is CONTENT (law 4).
        // The modifier declares no rule of its own; it joins two shipped
        // selector lists in tokens.css.
        btn.className = 'page-deco page-deco-photo';
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'the photograph');
        // NODE-BUILT, NOT AN HTML STRING — the tray picker's posture, not
        // the pinned slot's innerHTML one. setAttribute never parses HTML,
        // so encodeURIComponent is the whole of the sink.
        var own = document.createElement('img');
        own.setAttribute('src', '/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(entry.itemId));
        own.setAttribute('alt', '');
        own.setAttribute('loading', 'lazy');
        btn.appendChild(own);
      } else if (r.kind === 'stroke') {
        // 26.9-07 (D-10 tier 2): ONE BRANCH, and this is it. An inline SVG
        // with one <polyline> per stroke — never a <canvas>. A group whose
        // points are gone, or were never valid, renders NOTHING, exactly as
        // an off-roster sprite name does two branches up.
        btn = paintStrokeGroup(scene, r);
        if (!btn) { continue; }
        var sbox = strokeBox(r);
        bw = sbox.w;
        bh = sbox.h;
      } else {
        continue;                  // an unknown kind renders nothing
      }
      // 26.9-05 (LAW 4, EDGE LAW-4/adjacency): a decoration is page CHROME.
      // Every decoration node carries aria-hidden so it is chrome to a
      // screen reader too — the shipped page-painter posture, which the
      // seeded auto mark and the page background already take. The ONE
      // exception is the hand-text element above: that is her writing.
      //
      // `tabindex="-1"` RIDES WITH IT AND IS NOT OPTIONAL. aria-hidden on a
      // FOCUSABLE element is a real defect (WCAG 4.1.2): the node would be
      // tab-reachable and simultaneously invisible to the screen reader
      // announcing the tab stop. These are <button>s, so the two attributes
      // must be set together or neither. Design mode is a pointer gesture
      // end to end — there is no keyboard placement, rotation or scaling in
      // this phase — so nothing is lost by taking them out of the tab order,
      // and the hand-text element (the one thing with a keyboard surface
      // behind it, its <input>) keeps both its name and its tab stop.
      if (r.kind !== 'text') {
        btn.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
        btn.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');
      }
      btn.setAttribute('data-deco-idx', String(i));
      btn.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      btn.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      btn.style.setProperty('--w', String(bw));
      btn.style.setProperty('--h', String(bh));
      // draw order = array position; the station scene has NO shipped z
      // rule (Research Q4), so this is required, not optional.
      btn.style.setProperty('--i', String(i));
      // 26.9-05 (D-03/D-07): the stored transform, read through the
      // normalisers so a hand-edited store file that slipped past the
      // server still renders inside the bounds — and so a record written
      // BEFORE this wave, carrying neither field, reads at the resting
      // defaults and is byte-identically the page it was.
      btn.style.setProperty('--a', String(wrapDecoAngle(r.a)));
      btn.style.setProperty('--s', String(clampDecoScale(r.s)));
      // 26.9-05: the selected mark, and only ever one. The class is the
      // WHOLE of the selection treatment — a 1px coral outline, which is
      // the FIRST of the accent's closed two-item list. Selection STATE,
      // never an action.
      if (r === NB_SEL) { btn.className += ' is-selected'; }
      attachPageDrag(btn, r);
      scene.appendChild(btn);
      // the handles, on the SELECTED element only — never a shared gizmo.
      // Appended after the mark so they sit over it.
      if (r === NB_SEL) { paintDecoHandles(scene, btn, r, bw, bh); }
    }
  }

  // 26.9-05: the two handles. Rotate at the bounding box's top-right,
  // scale at the bottom-right; unselected elements show none.
  //
  // They live in a wrapper that carries the element's ROTATION but never
  // its SCALE, and whose box is the element's box already multiplied by the
  // scale. A wrapper that also scaled would shrink a 12-scene-px target to
  // 6 CSS px at the floor — exactly when she is reaching for it to undo
  // having made the thing tiny.
  //
  // aria-hidden AND tabindex=-1, together, for the reason the decoration
  // nodes carry them: these are pointer gizmos with no keyboard gesture
  // behind them, and a focusable node hidden from the screen reader
  // announcing its tab stop is a defect rather than a courtesy.
  function paintDecoHandles(scene, deco, rec, w, h) {
    var s = clampDecoScale(rec.s);
    var a = wrapDecoAngle(rec.a);
    var wrap = document.createElement('div');
    wrap.className = 'page-deco-handles';
    wrap.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    var live = { deco: deco, wrap: wrap };
    previewDecoTransform(live, rec, w, h, a, s);
    // 26.9 F-11: each grip names its own verb. The cursor does the work on
    // hover (see tokens.css), and the title is the words behind it — three
    // identical squares cannot say what they each do, and a 12px control has
    // no room for a label of its own.
    var GRIP_TITLE = {
      rotate: 'drag to rotate · hold shift to snap to 15°, 90°, 180°',
      scale: 'drag to resize'
    };
    ['rotate', 'scale'].forEach(function (which) {
      var grip = document.createElement('button');
      grip.type = 'button';
      grip.className = 'page-deco-handle page-deco-' + which;
      grip.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      grip.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');
      grip.setAttribute('data-handle', which);
      grip.title = GRIP_TITLE[which];
      attachPageDrag(grip, rec, which, live);
      wrap.appendChild(grip);
    });
    // 26.9 F-10 (the owner, 2026-08-06): "I may want to delete a sticker which I
    // did a while ago but this current workflow only have the option to undo
    // and redo, this will get rid of all of the other changes I want to keep."
    //
    // Exactly right, and it was a real hole: there was NO delete anywhere —
    // not a handle, not a key (nbKeydown bound only undo/redo). So removing
    // one early mark meant undoing every good change made after it. Undo is a
    // TIME control; this is an OBJECT control, and one cannot substitute for
    // the other.
    //
    // A CLICK, not a drag, so it is not attached through attachPageDrag: the
    // other two grips adjust continuously, this one acts once.
    var kill = document.createElement('button');
    kill.type = 'button';
    kill.className = 'page-deco-handle page-deco-remove';
    kill.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    kill.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');
    kill.setAttribute('data-handle', 'remove');
    // The pointer path is aria-hidden like its siblings — this whole layer is
    // direct-manipulation chrome. The ACCESSIBLE path is the Delete/Backspace
    // binding in nbKeydown, which acts on the same selection.
    kill.title = 'remove this';
    kill.addEventListener('click', function (ev) {
      ev.preventDefault();
      ev.stopPropagation();
      nbRemoveDeco(rec);
    });
    wrap.appendChild(kill);
    scene.appendChild(wrap);
  }

  // 26.9 F-10 — remove ONE decoration, leaving every other change alone.
  // Undoable like every other act: pushNbUndo fires BEFORE the mutation, the
  // shipped rule, so one undo puts it back without disturbing anything else.
  function nbRemoveDeco(rec) {
    if (!rec || !NB_DAY) { return; }
    var d = decoDay(NB_DAY);
    var i = d.items.indexOf(rec);
    // Not in the day: nothing to remove, and pushing history for a no-op
    // would be a keystroke that appears to do nothing (the placeFromTray
    // rule, applied here for the same reason).
    if (i === -1) { return; }
    pushNbUndo();
    d.items.splice(i, 1);
    if (NB_SEL === rec) { NB_SEL = null; }
    // The same orphan trap the pen guards: a stale group reference would let
    // the next stroke append ink to a record nothing renders.
    if (NB_PEN_GROUP === rec) { NB_PEN_GROUP = null; }
    postDecorations(NB_DAY);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // ---- 26.9-07 (D-10 TIER 2): THE FREEHAND PEN ---------------------------
  //
  // THE PEN IS A KIND OF DECORATION, NOT A SECOND EDITOR. That sentence is
  // the whole design, and it is also what makes this wave the phase's
  // designated CUT. A stroke group is a `.page-deco` like any other: the
  // same class, the same slot, the same z from array position, the same
  // selection outline, the same two handles, the same drag, the same undo
  // stack, the same day reset, the same day record. The pen adds ONE `kind`
  // value and ONE render branch, and nothing else in this file references
  // either — which is a property to PRESERVE, not a coincidence to hope for,
  // and it is asserted as an equality rather than described here.
  //
  // AN INLINE <svg>, NEVER A <canvas>. A raster canvas would be a second
  // rendering system, a second persistence shape and a second thing to
  // rotate and scale — three new problems for a feature that is one
  // <polyline> per stroke. The UI-SPEC states this contract so that a cut
  // cannot strand it and so that nobody reaches for a canvas; the two
  // declarations that carry it (`stroke-linecap: square` and
  // `shape-rendering: crispEdges`) live in tokens.css and are what make a
  // vector polyline read as PIXEL WORK beside pixel washi.
  //
  // STORAGE — one record per group, in the day record every other mark is
  // already stored in:
  //
  //     { page, kind: 'stroke', x, y, pts: [[x,y,x,y,...], ...] }
  //
  // `pts` is a list of strokes; each stroke is a flat run of WHOLE scene-px
  // pairs held RELATIVE to the record's (x, y), which is the group's
  // bounding-box origin. Relative rather than absolute for one reason:
  // MOVING a group is then the same two-field edit every other decoration's
  // move already is, so attachPageDrag needs no stroke branch at all and
  // there is no per-point rewrite on a drag.
  var SVG_NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';

  // Every run in a group that is actually a stroke: an even number of
  // coordinates, and at least two points. A ONE-POINT RUN IS NOT A STROKE —
  // a polyline with a single point draws nothing, and storing one would be
  // the capture-everything implementation the tap counter-case exists to
  // refuse.
  function strokeList(rec) {
    var out = [];
    var all = (rec && Array.isArray(rec.pts)) ? rec.pts : [];
    for (var i = 0; i < all.length; i++) {
      var run = all[i];
      if (Array.isArray(run) && run.length >= 4 && run.length % 2 === 0) {
        out.push(run);
      }
    }
    return out;
  }

  // The group's box, DERIVED from its points rather than stored. A stored
  // box would be a second copy of a number the points already carry, and the
  // two would drift the first time a stroke was appended.
  //
  // The origin is the record's own (x, y) — the client rebases every append
  // so the smallest point is 0 — so only the far edge is measured here. It
  // FLOORS AT 1 in each axis, because a perfectly straight horizontal or
  // vertical line has no extent in one direction and would otherwise be a
  // zero-size element: unselectable, undraggable, and invisible.
  function strokeBox(rec) {
    var runs = strokeList(rec);
    var maxX = 0;
    var maxY = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < runs.length; i++) {
      for (var j = 0; j + 1 < runs[i].length; j += 2) {
        if (runs[i][j] > maxX) { maxX = runs[i][j]; }
        if (runs[i][j + 1] > maxY) { maxY = runs[i][j + 1]; }
      }
    }
    return { w: Math.max(1, Math.round(maxX)),
      h: Math.max(1, Math.round(maxY)) };
  }

  function penFlatten(pts) {
    var out = [];
    for (var i = 0; i < pts.length; i++) { out.push(pts[i].x, pts[i].y); }
    return out;
  }

  function penShift(run, dx, dy) {
    var out = [];
    for (var i = 0; i + 1 < run.length; i += 2) {
      out.push(run[i] + dx, run[i + 1] + dy);
    }
    return out;
  }

  // ONE <polyline> PER STROKE, inside ONE inline <svg> whose view box is the
  // group's own box in SCENE px — so one user unit is one scene px, and the
  // stylesheet's `stroke-width: 1` is a one-scene-px line at every --k,
  // exactly like the 1px hairlines the rest of this scene is drawn with.
  //
  // NOTHING HERE PARSES MARKUP (T-26.9-35). Every node is made through
  // element APIs, and the points attribute is assembled from numbers that
  // have been through Math.round — no stored string reaches this overlay at
  // any point, so there is no injection surface to sanitise rather than a
  // sanitiser to trust.
  //
  // Returns the node WITHOUT appending it and WITHOUT setting --x/--y/--i:
  // the two call sites (the page painter and the live preview) place it, and
  // one of them is placing something that is not in the store yet.
  function paintStrokeGroup(scene, rec) {
    var runs = strokeList(rec);
    if (!runs.length) { return null; }
    var doc = (scene && scene.ownerDocument) || document;
    var box = strokeBox(rec);
    var btn = doc.createElement('button');
    btn.type = 'button';
    btn.className = 'page-deco page-deco-stroke';
    var svg = doc.createElementNS(SVG_NS, 'svg');
    svg.setAttribute('viewBox', '0 0 ' + box.w + ' ' + box.h);
    for (var i = 0; i < runs.length; i++) {
      var pairs = [];
      for (var j = 0; j + 1 < runs[i].length; j += 2) {
        pairs.push(Math.round(runs[i][j]) + ',' +
          Math.round(runs[i][j + 1]));
      }
      var line = doc.createElementNS(SVG_NS, 'polyline');
      line.setAttribute('points', pairs.join(' '));
      svg.appendChild(line);
    }
    btn.appendChild(svg);
    btn.style.setProperty('--w', String(box.w));
    btn.style.setProperty('--h', String(box.h));
    return btn;
  }

  // A completed stroke joins the day record. THE TAP COUNTER-CASE IS THE
  // FIRST LINE: a pointerdown and pointerup with no movement is ONE point
  // and records NOTHING. A capture-everything implementation would fill the
  // store with empty polylines, one per stray tap on the page.
  function commitStroke(abs) {
    if (!abs || abs.length < 2 || !NB_DAY) { return; }
    var target = NB_PLACE;
    if (!target) { return; }
    var d = decoDay(NB_DAY);
    var g = NB_PEN_GROUP;
    // The group is only still the group if it is still IN the day. An undo,
    // a day reset restored, or a day flip replaces the records wholesale,
    // and a stale reference would append ink to a record nothing renders.
    if (g && d.items.indexOf(g) === -1) { g = null; NB_PEN_GROUP = null; }
    if (!g && d.items.length >= NB_DECO_CAP) { return; }
    if (g && strokeList(g).length >= NB_PEN_STROKE_CAP) { return; }
    // AFTER both refusals, never before — the shipped placeFromTray rule: a
    // refused mark is not an act, and an undo that handed back a page she
    // never changed would be a keystroke that appears to do nothing.
    pushNbUndo();
    // F-9: drawing on a reset day clears the flag and DROPS the superseded
    // records rather than resurrecting them — the same choice, for the same
    // reason, that placeFromTray now makes. See nbClearResetForEdit.
    nbClearResetForEdit(d);
    // F-9 trap: that clear may have just dropped the very group this stroke
    // would extend (a pen group from before the reset survives under the
    // flag, so the staleness check above found it and left `g` set). Re-run
    // the same check now, or the ink appends to a record nothing renders —
    // which is precisely what the check above exists to prevent.
    if (g && d.items.indexOf(g) === -1) { g = null; NB_PEN_GROUP = null; }
    if (!g) {
      g = { page: target.itemId, kind: 'stroke', x: 0, y: 0, pts: [] };
      d.items.push(g);
      NB_PEN_GROUP = g;
    }
    // REBASE, so the record's (x, y) stays the bounding-box origin and every
    // stored point stays non-negative: lift the existing runs back to
    // absolute, add the new one, then take the new origin off all of them.
    var runs = strokeList(g).map(function (run) {
      return penShift(run, g.x, g.y);
    });
    runs.push(penFlatten(abs));
    var minX = runs[0][0];
    var minY = runs[0][1];
    runs.forEach(function (run) {
      for (var i = 0; i + 1 < run.length; i += 2) {
        if (run[i] < minX) { minX = run[i]; }
        if (run[i + 1] < minY) { minY = run[i + 1]; }
      }
    });
    g.x = minX;
    g.y = minY;
    g.pts = runs.map(function (run) { return penShift(run, -minX, -minY); });
    postDecorations(NB_DAY);
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // THE CAPTURE. Bound to the station scene in the CAPTURE PHASE, and bound
  // AT MOST ONCE: this function releases the previous listener before it
  // considers binding a new one, so a repaint cannot stack handlers and
  // turning the pen off RELEASES the binding rather than merely gating it.
  // That is nbKeydown's discipline, for nbKeydown's reason — a listener that
  // outlives its mode is a listener somebody has to remember about — and the
  // release hands back THE SAME FUNCTION REFERENCE, because a remove with a
  // fresh closure detaches nothing at all.
  //
  // CAPTURE PHASE, NOT BUBBLE, and that is load-bearing: a decoration's own
  // pointerdown calls stopPropagation, so a stroke begun on top of an
  // existing mark would otherwise become a drag of that mark. attachPageDrag
  // declines outright while the pen is on, which is the same rule said from
  // the other side.
  //
  // THE CHROME BAND IS NOT A DRAWING SURFACE, and it is excluded
  // GEOMETRICALLY rather than by sniffing at the target's classes: a point
  // outside the page interior belongs to the band, and this handler returns
  // WITHOUT TOUCHING THE EVENT, so the pen glyph, undo, redo, the reset row
  // and the page flips all keep working while the pen is live. A class-based
  // exclusion would have to be extended by hand every time the band grows.
  function attachPenCapture(scene) {
    if (!scene || !scene.addEventListener) { return null; }
    if (NB_PEN_HANDLER) {
      scene.removeEventListener('pointerdown', NB_PEN_HANDLER, true);
      NB_PEN_HANDLER = null;
    }
    if (!NBDESIGN || !NB_PEN) { return null; }
    NB_PEN_HANDLER = function (e) {
      // Pitfall 6, the same read every other gesture in this phase makes:
      // --k at POINTERDOWN, from #station-scene, never cached at boot.
      var k = parseInt(
        getComputedStyle(scene).getPropertyValue('--k'), 10) || 1;
      var box = scene.getBoundingClientRect
        ? scene.getBoundingClientRect() : { left: 0, top: 0 };
      function at(cx, cy) {
        return { x: (cx - box.left) / k, y: (cy - box.top) / k };
      }
      var raw = at(e.clientX, e.clientY);
      // 26.91-10: the MARK CANVAS, not the spread interior. Handwriting is
      // one of the three kinds of mark the owner's `right-page-only` ruling
      // names (26.91-CONTEXT.md A-12, 2026-08-08), so the pen's gate moves
      // with the sticker clamp rather than being left behind on the old
      // bound — a pen that could still write across the month grid would
      // deliver half her ruling.
      if (raw.x < NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x0 || raw.x > NB_MARK_BOUNDS.x1 ||
          raw.y < NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y0 || raw.y > NB_MARK_BOUNDS.y1) { return; }
      e.preventDefault();
      e.stopPropagation();
      // EVERY POINT GOES THROUGH THE SHIPPED CLAMP, at a 1x1 footprint —
      // because a point IS a one-scene-px footprint. So nothing can run
      // under the chrome band, and there is still exactly ONE clamp rather
      // than two hand-typed tables.
      //
      // 26.91-10: THE CLIENT IS NOW STRICTER THAN THE SERVER ON x, AND THAT
      // IS THE INTENDED DIRECTION. server.py fences an origin at (4-379,
      // 4-189) — the SPREAD; the client now fences a mark at (192-379,
      // 4-189) — the RIGHT PAGE, per the owner's `right-page-only` ruling
      // (26.91-CONTEXT.md A-12). The two no longer agree by construction on
      // x, and they do not need to: the server's job is to refuse what is
      // off the spread, and a client that refuses MORE than the server can
      // never produce a payload the server rejects. y still agrees exactly.
      //
      // WHOLE SCENE PX ONLY. The scene cannot show sub-pixel precision, so
      // storing it would grow the payload by noise and soften the line.
      //
      // 26.91-26 (F-23): THIS PIN IS ALREADY RIGHT AND IT PROVES THE NEW
      // CEILING IS — a 1x1 footprint ceilings at exactly the server's max,
      // because a count of 1 and an extent of 0 are the same number.
      function pin(p) {
        var c = clampDecoOrigin(p.x, p.y, 1, 1);
        return { x: Math.round(c.x), y: Math.round(c.y) };
      }
      var abs = [pin(raw)];
      // The live preview is painted through THE SAME painter the store uses,
      // fed a provisional record at the scene origin with absolute points —
      // so what she sees while drawing and what lands afterwards cannot be
      // two different renderings of the same stroke.
      var live = { kind: 'stroke', x: 0, y: 0, pts: [] };
      var node = null;
      function drop() {
        if (node && node.parentNode) { node.parentNode.removeChild(node); }
        node = null;
      }
      function draw() {
        drop();
        live.pts = [penFlatten(abs)];
        node = paintStrokeGroup(scene, live);
        if (!node) { return; }
        node.style.setProperty('--x', '0');
        node.style.setProperty('--y', '0');
        node.style.setProperty('--i', String(NB_DECO_CAP));
        scene.appendChild(node);
      }
      function onMove(ev) {
        if (abs.length >= NB_PEN_PTS_CAP) { return; }
        var p = pin(at(ev.clientX, ev.clientY));
        var tail = abs[abs.length - 1];
        if (p.x === tail.x && p.y === tail.y) { return; }
        abs.push(p);
        draw();
      }
      function onUp() {
        scene.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
        scene.removeEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
        drop();
        commitStroke(abs);
      }
      scene.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
      scene.addEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
    };
    scene.addEventListener('pointerdown', NB_PEN_HANDLER, true);
    return NB_PEN_HANDLER;
  }

  // The toggle. A fresh pen session always starts a FRESH GROUP: continuing
  // the previous one would be wrong the moment she turned or resized it in
  // between, because a group's transform composes about its CENTRE and ink
  // appended afterwards would land where the untransformed record says
  // rather than where she drew it.
  function setNotebookPen(on) {
    if (on && !NBDESIGN) { return; }
    NB_PEN = !!on;
    // 26.91-02: THE RECIPROCAL HALF of the pen/write exclusion. Arming the
    // pen disarms `write`. The matching line lives in setNotebookWrite, and
    // BOTH are required: exclusivity enforced in one setter and hoped for
    // in the other is not exclusivity — it holds in whichever direction
    // somebody happened to drive it and leaks in the other.
    if (on) { NB_WRITE = false; }
    NB_PEN_GROUP = null;
    nbSyncArmedClass();
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // 26.91-18 F-17 (the owner, 2026-08-09): "when pen is armed it is hard to
  // notice i am starting to use pen, so i think we need to change the cursor
  // when the pen is armed."
  //
  // HOOKED AT THE BODY — never at the chip: while drawing she looks at the
  // PAGE, and `is-on` exists only on the band. Sibling of the `nb-design`
  // mode class, same toggle shape. The rule it feeds and the whole argument
  // live at the armed-tool cursor rules in tokens.css and are not restated
  // here.
  // 26.91-22: ONE SHARED HOOK PLUS ONE PER TOOL. Whole argument in tokens.css.
  // 26.91-36 (F-24 / D-11): AND ARMING A TOOL PUTS THE TRAY AWAY.
  // MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED: greying the tin left its TRAY up behind it —
  // 1 tray node, 10 live sticker cards, and pressing one still placed a mark.
  // That is the same two-modes-at-once defect one level in, INSIDE the fix
  // for it, and it would have made her ruling read as applied while it was
  // not. The idiom is the page canvas's own (app.js:13738-13739), not a new
  // one, and it lives HERE because this is the single point BOTH setters
  // reach — two copies, one per setter, is the drift shape this phase keeps
  // finding.
  // The repaint the canvas pairs with it is already the next statement in
  // both callers, so it is not repeated.
  function nbSyncArmedClass() {
    document.body.classList.toggle('nb-armed', !!(NB_PEN || NB_WRITE));
    document.body.classList.toggle('nb-armed-pen', !!NB_PEN);
    document.body.classList.toggle('nb-armed-write', !!NB_WRITE);
    if ((NB_PEN || NB_WRITE) && NB_TIN_OPEN) { NB_TIN_OPEN = false; }
  }

  // 26.91-02: THE `write` TOOL'S SETTER — setNotebookPen's mirror.
  //
  // It carries no NB_PEN_GROUP reset because `write` has no group to
  // continue: every armed tap creates one independent record, where a pen
  // session accumulates strokes into one group. That asymmetry is the only
  // difference between the two setters and it is stated so a later reader
  // does not "restore symmetry" by adding a line that means nothing here.
  function setNotebookWrite(on) {
    if (on && !NBDESIGN) { return; }
    NB_WRITE = !!on;
    if (on) { NB_PEN = false; }
    // 26.91-18: set from BOTH setters, never one — a hook driven from a
    // single setter is right in whichever direction somebody drove it and
    // silently wrong in the other, the asymmetry the line above refuses.
    nbSyncArmedClass();
    if (NB_REPAINT) { NB_REPAINT(); }
  }

  // The positive-only month calendar (D-13) — the left page of view 0.
  // A plain month grid where ONLY days holding a surviving blessing
  // light up as buttons; empty days are bare inert numbers. No weekday
  // header, no week numbers, no today mark, no counts (law 3: the
  // lit/unlit distinction is the whole vocabulary).
  function paintNotebookCalendar(scene, entries, roster, spreads) {
    var g = STATION_NOTEBOOK_GEOM;
    var month = STATION_NOTEBOOK.month ||
      (roster.length ? roster[0] : '');
    var grid = blessingsMonthGrid(entries, month);
    if (!grid) { return; }
    function place(el, r) {
      el.style.setProperty('--x', String(r.x));
      el.style.setProperty('--y', String(r.y));
      el.style.setProperty('--w', String(r.w));
      el.style.setProperty('--h', String(r.h));
    }
    // the month label — the shipped cover-meta register.
    var parts = month.split('-');
    var label = document.createElement('div');
    label.className = 'caption-hand station-caption';
    label.style.fontSize = 'calc(6px * var(--k))';
    label.style.color = 'var(--ink-soft)';
    label.style.textAlign = 'center';
    label.textContent = new Date(parseInt(parts[0], 10),
      parseInt(parts[1], 10) - 1, 1).toLocaleDateString(undefined,
      { month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });
    place(label, g.monthLabel);
    scene.appendChild(label);
    // the ‹/› month nav beside the label cycles ONLY the populated-
    // month roster — an empty month does not exist as a view (D-13),
    // so the doors render only when a populated neighbor exists. The
    // roster runs newest first: ‹ walks to the earlier populated
    // month, › back toward the newest. Law-3-neutral accessible names.
    var idx = roster.indexOf(month);
    if (idx !== -1 && idx + 1 < roster.length) {
      var earlier = document.createElement('button');
      earlier.type = 'button';
      earlier.className = 'station-fixture station-flip';
      earlier.setAttribute('aria-label', 'earlier month');
      earlier.textContent = '‹';
      place(earlier, g.navPrev);
      earlier.addEventListener('click', function () {
        STATION_NOTEBOOK.month = roster[idx + 1];
        paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
      });
      scene.appendChild(earlier);
    }
    if (idx > 0) {
      var later = document.createElement('button');
      later.type = 'button';
      later.className = 'station-fixture station-flip';
      later.setAttribute('aria-label', 'later month');
      later.textContent = '›';
      place(later, g.navNext);
      later.addEventListener('click', function () {
        STATION_NOTEBOOK.month = roster[idx - 1];
        paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
      });
      scene.appendChild(later);
    }
    // the day grid — 7 columns, weekday-aligned.
    grid.days.forEach(function (cell) {
      var slot = grid.firstWeekday + cell.day - 1;
      var col = slot % 7;
      var row = Math.floor(slot / 7);
      var x = g.grid.x0 + col * g.grid.pitchX;
      var y = g.grid.y0 + row * g.grid.pitchY;
      if (cell.lit) {
        // a lit day: a real button — --btn-bg fill, --ink number, the
        // station-fixture focus grammar; tap → the view holding that
        // day's first page.
        var day = document.createElement('button');
        day.type = 'button';
        day.className = 'station-fixture';
        day.style.background = 'var(--btn-bg)';
        day.style.color = 'var(--ink)';
        day.style.font = 'inherit';
        day.style.fontSize = 'calc(6px * var(--k))';
        day.textContent = String(cell.day);
        place(day, { x: x, y: y, w: g.grid.cellW, h: g.grid.cellH });
        day.addEventListener('click', function () {
          // anchor on the DAY (D-C): open to that day's spread — the
          // first day-aligned spread carrying this local day. The label
          // is chrome, built from the displayed month + this cell's day
          // (no item-derived string reaches a sink; textContent-only).
          var mm = month.split('-');
          var lbl = mm[1] + '/' + (cell.day < 10 ? '0' : '') + cell.day +
            '/' + mm[0];
          var target = 0;
          for (var si = 0; si < spreads.length; si++) {
            if (spreads[si].day === lbl) { target = si; break; }
          }
          STATION_NOTEBOOK.view = target;
          paintNotebookSpread(scene, entries, roster);
        });
        scene.appendChild(day);
      } else {
        // an unlit day: a bare inert number — no fill, no border, no
        // listener, no cursor, no hover, no tooltip; built by this one
        // code path so every unlit day is byte-identical (D-13/D-35).
        var bare = document.createElement('span');
        bare.textContent = String(cell.day);
        bare.style.color = 'var(--ink-soft)';
        bare.style.fontSize = 'calc(6px * var(--k))';
        bare.style.textAlign = 'center';
        bare.style.lineHeight =
          'calc(' + g.grid.cellH + ' * var(--k) * 1px)';
        bare.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
        placeNotebookInert(bare, x, y, g.grid.cellW, g.grid.cellH);
        scene.appendChild(bare);
      }
    });
  }

  function showReflectionVerbatim(item, filters, returnTo, opts) {
    opts = opts || {};
    if (!item) { return; }
    // 26.4-10 (Change B): the surface guard still refuses a fenced item for
    // EVERY auto-surface (spines, proposals). It is bypassed ONLY for a
    // deliberate owner override — a toggle-on wikilink click the owner made by
    // hand — and even then the render is read-only (no state write).
    if (!opts.ownerOverride && StudyCore.guardSurface(item, filters) !== null) {
      return;
    }
    ROOM.panelReturn = returnTo;
    // 26.5-01 (SC-1 / T-26.5-02): flag on → the in-scene spread presents
    // the reflection. The guardSurface re-check above already ran; the
    // fence-aware reflectionBodyForDisplay composition rides through the
    // ONE shared builder inside openSpread (opts.reflection — no second
    // content path, frontmatter never rendered, no reaction line;
    // 26.5-09 UAT — the owner: the notes box DOES render on reflections
    // now, superseding the 26.4 no-notes parity). Flag off → the
    // Phase-25 reader below, unchanged but for the same notes box
    // (trivially shared: the one renderReaderComments call).
    if (DIEGETIC_ROOM_ENABLED) {
      if (item.type === 'image') {
        openSpread(item, null, { reflection: true });
        return;
      }
      fetch('/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(item.id) + '?t=' + Date.now(),
        { cache: 'no-store' }).then(function (r) {
          if (!r.ok) { throw new Error('missing'); }
          return r.text();
        }).then(function (md) {
          openSpread(item, md, { reflection: true });
        }).catch(function () {
          openSpread(item, 'this one could not be opened.',
            { reflection: true });
        });
      return;
    }
    var titleEl = $('reader-title');
    if (titleEl) { titleEl.innerHTML = escapeHtml(item.title || ''); }
    var note = $('reader-note');
    if (note) { note.innerHTML = ''; }
    var bar = $('reaction-bar');
    if (bar) { bar.innerHTML = ''; }
    // 26.5-09 UAT — the owner: notes belong on reflections too (supersedes
    // the 26.4 no-notes parity); trivially shared with the legacy reader
    // — the one shipped renderReaderComments call into its own container
    // (this also stops a previous item's notes box lingering here).
    renderReaderComments(item, $('reader-comments'));
    var box = $('reader-content');
    if (!box) { return; }
    if (item.type === 'image') {
      box.innerHTML = '<img src="/lib/' +
        escapeAttr(encodeURIComponent(item.id)) +
        '" alt="" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';
      if (bar) {
        bar.innerHTML = '<div class="heavy-reader-note"></div>';
        wireHeavyQuietLink(item.id, bar,
          bar.querySelector('.heavy-reader-note'));
      }
      showScreen('reader');
      return;
    }
    box.innerHTML = '';
    fetch('/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(item.id) + '?t=' + Date.now(),
      { cache: 'no-store' }).then(function (r) {
        if (!r.ok) { throw new Error('missing'); }
        return r.text();
      }).then(function (md) {
        var parts = StudyCore.splitFrontmatter(md == null ? '' : md);
        // 26.4-09 (Pitfall 1): the frontmatter (with its reflects: [Memoir/…])
        // is dropped by splitFrontmatter and never rendered; the body renders
        // verbatim EXCEPT that any fenced-roster wikilink/path is de-linked to
        // inert text (never a door back to the raw journal).
        // 26.4-10 (Change A): seal the ## Related leak — a bare Related link
        // ([[日记 …]], no folder prefix) to a fenced note that was never
        // imported slips both the roster prefix check and the library resolver.
        // collectFencedBasenames (over the FULL md, frontmatter included) adds
        // the reflection's OWN referenced Memoir/… basenames so that door is
        // inert too, exactly like the body treatment.
        // 26.4-10 (Change B): the de-link is gated by the LOCAL display toggle
        // — OFF (default) de-links every fenced door; ON leaves them live for
        // a deliberate owner click. The gate is a pure display decision; the
        // cloud + surfacing fences never consult it.
        box.innerHTML = renderMarkdown(reflectionBodyForDisplay(
          md, StudyCore.rewriteAttachmentRefs(item, parts.body),
          reflectionWikilinkFenced, displayFenceOpen()));
      }).catch(function () {
        box.innerHTML = '<p>' +
          escapeHtml('this one could not be opened.') + '</p>';
      });
    showScreen('reader');
  }

  // The shared insight-book builder — ONE source of truth read by BOTH the
  // diegetic shelf and the D-14 Manage fallback, so the insights are never
  // hostage to the shelf sprite. Draws every item through the shipped fence
  // (surfacePool) FIRST (D-19), then groups. Book order (fill order): the
  // allowed connections lead (the affirmed threads — the point), then the
  // three deterministic observation books. A truly empty / all-fenced library
  // yields [] — the honest empty shelf.
  function buildInsightBooks(itemsMap, filters, books) {
    var now = Date.now(); // app.js is the only clock reader (D-02)
    var pool = StudyCore.surfacePool(itemsMap || {}, filters || [], now);
    var out = [];
    // (d) one book per allowed connection — books.json, written ONLY by the
    // user's own Allow (promote). This shelf never writes it.
    (books || []).forEach(function (b) {
      if (!b || typeof b !== 'object') { return; }
      out.push({ kind: 'thread', title: String(b.title || ''),
        why: typeof b.why === 'string' ? b.why : '',
        connected_ids: Array.isArray(b.connected_ids) ? b.connected_ids : [],
        id: b.id });
    });
    // (a) saved but never opened — last_opened_ms is null (deterministic,
    // renders with the librarian off).
    var never = pool.filter(function (it) {
      return it && it.last_opened_ms == null;
    });
    if (never.length > 0) {
      out.push({ kind: 'never', title: INSIGHT_TITLE_NEVER,
        ids: never.map(function (it) { return it.id; }) });
    }
    // (b) what the library is made of — {theme: count} (deterministic).
    var themes = {};
    var themeOrder = [];
    pool.forEach(function (it) {
      insightThemeKeys(it).forEach(function (k) {
        if (!(k in themes)) { themes[k] = 0; themeOrder.push(k); }
        themes[k] += 1;
      });
    });
    if (themeOrder.length > 0) {
      out.push({ kind: 'themes', title: INSIGHT_TITLE_THEMES,
        themes: themes, order: themeOrder });
    }
    // (c) what was saved, season by season — {theme: {season: count}} from
    // each item's OWN save stamp (deterministic; never a visit or absence).
    var seasons = {};
    var seasonOrder = [];
    pool.forEach(function (it) {
      var season = insightSeasonOf(it);
      if (season === null) { return; }
      insightThemeKeys(it).forEach(function (k) {
        if (!(k in seasons)) { seasons[k] = {}; seasonOrder.push(k); }
        seasons[k][season] = (seasons[k][season] || 0) + 1;
      });
    });
    if (seasonOrder.length > 0) {
      out.push({ kind: 'seasons', title: INSIGHT_TITLE_SEASONS,
        seasons: seasons, order: seasonOrder });
    }
    return out;
  }

  // The insight-library screen is created HERE (this plan touches app.js
  // only, the manageStatsBox posture) — a panel over the dimmed room with
  // the shipped back-to-the-room chrome (showScreen toggles the scrim + back
  // link for every non-room panel). Idempotent: found again once made.
  function insightScreen() {
    var el = $('screen-insights');
    if (el) { return el; }
    el = document.createElement('section');
    el.id = 'screen-insights';
    el.className = 'screen';
    el.innerHTML = '<h2 id="insights-heading"></h2>' +
      '<div id="insights-shelf"></div>';
    document.body.appendChild(el);
    return el;
  }

  // The bookshelf's own click lands here (D-24 / UNIFY): one read of the
  // allowed-connection store + the store items, then the shelf renders —
  // empty when there is nothing yet, spines as it fills. Nothing is written;
  // a books.json read failure is the empty list, never an error (D-05).
  function openInsightLibrary() {
    insightScreen();
    ROOM.panelReturn = 'room';
    apiGet('/api/items').then(function (res) {
      var itemsMap = (res.ok && res.data && res.data.items) || {};
      var meta = (res.ok && res.data && res.data.meta) || {};
      INSIGHTS.items = itemsMap;
      INSIGHTS.filters = Array.isArray(meta.filters) ? meta.filters : [];
      apiGet('/api/librarian/books').then(function (bres) {
        var books = (bres.ok && bres.data &&
          Array.isArray(bres.data.books)) ? bres.data.books : [];
        INSIGHTS.list = buildInsightBooks(INSIGHTS.items, INSIGHTS.filters,
          books);
        renderInsightShelf();
        showScreen('insights');
      }).catch(function () {
        INSIGHTS.list = buildInsightBooks(INSIGHTS.items, INSIGHTS.filters,
          []);
        renderInsightShelf();
        showScreen('insights');
      });
    }).catch(function () {
      showScreen('room');
    });
  }

  // Zero → the empty-shelf truth (heading + body, the exact copy); one → a
  // single spine; many → spines fill left-to-right and the shelf scrolls
  // within its own bounds (UI-SPEC zero-one-many). NEVER a count, never an
  // "N books" label (law 3). The spine title truncates by CSS only (single
  // line, ellipsis — the long-title backstop); the full title renders in the
  // reader header when the book opens.
  function renderInsightShelf() {
    insightScreen();
    var head = $('insights-heading');
    var shelf = $('insights-shelf');
    if (!shelf) { return; }
    if (INSIGHTS.list.length === 0) {
      if (head) { head.textContent = EMPTY_SHELF_HEAD; }
      shelf.className = '';
      shelf.innerHTML = '<p class="empty-shelf-body">' +
        escapeHtml(EMPTY_SHELF_BODY) + '</p>';
      return;
    }
    if (head) { head.textContent = ''; }
    shelf.className = 'insight-shelf';
    shelf.innerHTML = INSIGHTS.list.map(function (book, i) {
      return '<button type="button" class="book-spine" data-i="' + i +
        '"><span class="book-spine-title">' + escapeHtml(book.title) +
        '</span></button>';
    }).join('');
    shelf.querySelectorAll('.book-spine').forEach(function (btn) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
        openInsightBook(INSIGHTS.list[Number(btn.getAttribute('data-i'))]);
      });
    });
  }

  // Opening a book re-shows its insight in the reader. An allowed thread
  // (kind d) shows the connection ("why") + the connected past material
  // VERBATIM, each piece guarded fail-closed immediately before display
  // (law 5). The deterministic observation books (a/b/c) show the
  // library-composition observation — counts/observations ABOUT the library,
  // never a judgment of the user (SC6). The FULL title renders in the reader
  // header untruncated (the spine ellipsis is CSS-only — the long-title
  // backstop). A book carries no reaction line: an observation is not judged.
  function openInsightBook(book) {
    if (!book) { return; }
    INSIGHTS.openBook = book;
    ROOM.panelReturn = 'insights';
    var titleEl = $('reader-title');
    if (titleEl) { titleEl.innerHTML = escapeHtml(book.title); }
    var note = $('reader-note');
    if (note) { note.innerHTML = ''; }
    var bar = $('reaction-bar');
    if (bar) { bar.innerHTML = ''; }
    var box = $('reader-content');
    if (!box) { return; }
    if (book.kind === 'thread') {
      box.innerHTML = (book.why ? '<p>' + escapeHtml(book.why) + '</p>' : '') +
        book.connected_ids.map(function (cid) {
          var item = INSIGHTS.items[cid];
          // law-5 belt: a since-fenced connected id renders NOTHING — not even
          // its title (mirrors the never-book guard below). An allowed book
          // whose connected item was later fenced/retired shows no leak.
          if (!item ||
              StudyCore.guardSurface(item, INSIGHTS.filters) !== null) {
            return '';
          }
          var t = item.title ? item.title : String(cid);
          return '<div class="insight-piece"><p class="card-title" ' +
            'style="margin:0 0 4px">' + escapeHtml(t) + '</p>' +
            '<div class="insight-note" data-cid="' +
            escapeAttr(String(cid)) + '"></div></div>';
        }).join('');
      fillInsightPieces(box);
    } else if (book.kind === 'never') {
      box.innerHTML = '<p class="insight-observe">' +
        escapeHtml('Things here still waiting for a first read — open one ' +
          'when you like.') + '</p>' +
        book.ids.map(function (id) {
          var item = INSIGHTS.items[id];
          if (!item ||
              StudyCore.guardSurface(item, INSIGHTS.filters) !== null) {
            return '';
          }
          return '<p style="margin:0 0 8px"><button type="button" ' +
            'class="insight-item-open" data-id="' + escapeAttr(id) +
            '" style="background:none;border:none;color:var(--ink);cursor:' +
            'pointer;font:inherit;padding:0;text-align:left;text-decoration:' +
            'underline">' + escapeHtml(item.title) + '</button></p>';
        }).join('');
      box.querySelectorAll('.insight-item-open').forEach(function (btn) {
        btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
          openInsightItem(btn.getAttribute('data-id'));
        });
      });
    } else if (book.kind === 'themes') {
      box.innerHTML = '<p class="insight-observe">' +
        escapeHtml('What your library holds, by the tags and folders your ' +
          'things already carry:') + '</p>' +
        book.order.map(function (k) {
          return '<p style="margin:0 0 4px">' + escapeHtml(k) + ' — ' +
            escapeHtml(String(book.themes[k])) + '</p>';
        }).join('');
    } else if (book.kind === 'seasons') {
      box.innerHTML = '<p class="insight-observe">' +
        escapeHtml('What you saved, grouped by the season each thing came ' +
          'from:') + '</p>' +
        book.order.map(function (k) {
          var buckets = book.seasons[k];
          var parts = Object.keys(buckets).map(function (s) {
            return escapeHtml(s) + ' — ' + escapeHtml(String(buckets[s]));
          }).join('; ');
          return '<p style="margin:0 0 4px"><strong>' + escapeHtml(k) +
            '</strong>: ' + parts + '</p>';
        }).join('');
    }
    showScreen('reader');
  }

  // Each connected piece renders VERBATIM (law 4) through the ONE markdown
  // seam, guarded fail-closed immediately before display (law 5) exactly like
  // the desk proposal pieces — the server already fenced generation; this is
  // the belt to the server's braces.
  function fillInsightPieces(box) {
    if (!box) { return; }
    var notes = box.querySelectorAll('.insight-note');
    Array.prototype.forEach.call(notes, function (slot) {
      var cid = slot.getAttribute('data-cid');
      var item = INSIGHTS.items[cid];
      if (!item || StudyCore.guardSurface(item, INSIGHTS.filters) !== null) {
        return;
      }
      if (item.type === 'image') {
        slot.innerHTML = '<img src="/lib/' +
          escapeAttr(encodeURIComponent(item.id)) +
          '" alt="" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';
        return;
      }
      fetch('/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(item.id) + '?t=' + Date.now(),
        { cache: 'no-store' }).then(function (r) {
          if (!r.ok) { throw new Error('missing'); }
          return r.text();
        }).then(function (md) {
          var parts = StudyCore.splitFrontmatter(md == null ? '' : md);
          // 26.88-02 (D-05): the ONE saved-body wrapper, then the shipped
          // attachment rewrite. The fail-closed guard above still runs
          // FIRST, textually and at run time — the wrapper is inserted
          // BELOW it and never above it (law 5).
          slot.innerHTML =
            renderMarkdown(StudyCore.rewriteAttachmentRefs(
              item, renderSavedBody(item, parts.fm, parts.body)));
        }).catch(function () {
          slot.innerHTML = '<p>' +
            escapeHtml('this one could not be opened.') + '</p>';
        });
    });
  }

  // A never-opened item, resurfaced in place: VERBATIM (law 4), guarded
  // fail-closed (law 5). This is the item-resurfacing loop folded INSIDE the
  // insight book (UNIFY). No open is recorded — a re-show is not a judgment,
  // and the observation stays honest across visits.
  function openInsightItem(id) {
    var item = INSIGHTS.items[id];
    if (!item || StudyCore.guardSurface(item, INSIGHTS.filters) !== null) {
      return;
    }
    ROOM.panelReturn = 'insights';
    var titleEl = $('reader-title');
    if (titleEl) { titleEl.innerHTML = escapeHtml(item.title); }
    var note = $('reader-note');
    if (note) { note.innerHTML = ''; }
    var bar = $('reaction-bar');
    if (bar) { bar.innerHTML = ''; }
    var box = $('reader-content');
    if (!box) { return; }
    if (item.type === 'image') {
      box.innerHTML = '<img src="/lib/' +
        escapeAttr(encodeURIComponent(item.id)) +
        '" alt="" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';
      if (bar) {
        bar.innerHTML = '<div class="heavy-reader-note"></div>';
        wireHeavyQuietLink(item.id, bar,
          bar.querySelector('.heavy-reader-note'));
      }
      showScreen('reader');
      return;
    }
    box.innerHTML = '';
    fetch('/lib/' + encodeURIComponent(item.id) + '?t=' + Date.now(),
      { cache: 'no-store' }).then(function (r) {
        if (!r.ok) { throw new Error('missing'); }
        return r.text();
      }).then(function (md) {
        var parts = StudyCore.splitFrontmatter(md == null ? '' : md);
        // 26.88-02 (D-05): the ONE saved-body wrapper, then the shipped
        // attachment rewrite. The second use of the body on the next line
        // takes the ORIGINAL: an attachment reference has to be looked up
        // in what the file actually says, not in the laid-out copy.
        box.innerHTML =
          renderMarkdown(StudyCore.rewriteAttachmentRefs(
            item, renderSavedBody(item, parts.fm, parts.body))) +
          StudyCore.unreferencedAttachments(item, parts.body).map(
            function (rel, i) {
              return (i === 0 ? ATTACHMENT_SEP : '') +
                '<img class="att-pic" src="' +
                escapeAttr(StudyCore.attachmentUrl(item.id, rel)) +
                '" alt="" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;' +
                'margin:0 0 1em">';
            }).join('');
        // F-04 (owner ruling 2026-08-14): her tappable row, on the insight
        // book's own reader too — one fold, wired at every note sink.
        foldShotsIntoStrip(item, box);
        dimSeamRepeats(item, box);
      }).catch(function () {
        quietError(box, 'This one could not be opened.');
      });
    showScreen('reader');
  }

  // ---- 26.4-04 (D-14): the Manage "about your library" fallback --------------
  //
  // The SAME insight data as the shelf, rendered as a plain Manage section —
  // built DECOUPLED from the shelf sprite. If the diegetic bookshelf art ever
  // slips, the insights still render HERE; the insights are never cut, only
  // the diegetic wrapper (D-14). Uses buildInsightBooks (the one source of
  // truth) over the manage store snapshot, so the fence (D-19) holds
  // identically. Every row is an observation ABOUT the library, never a
  // judgment of the user (SC6): no "you should", no score, no report card.

  var MANAGE_LIBRARY = { books: [] };

  function manageLibraryBox() {
    var box = $('manage-sec-library');
    if (box) { return box; }
    var anchor = $('manage-sec-stats') || $('manage-sec-room');
    if (!anchor || !anchor.parentNode) { return null; }
    box = document.createElement('div');
    box.id = 'manage-sec-library';
    anchor.parentNode.insertBefore(box, anchor.nextSibling);
    return box;
  }

  // One plain observation line per insight book (no user-judgment, no
  // shelf-level count — the diegetic wrapper's rules hold here too).
  function libraryRowObservation(book) {
    if (book.kind === 'thread') {
      return book.why ? book.why : 'a thread you allowed onto the shelf';
    }
    if (book.kind === 'never') {
      return 'things still waiting for a first read';
    }
    if (book.kind === 'themes') {
      return 'the tags and folders your things already carry';
    }
    if (book.kind === 'seasons') {
      return 'what you saved, grouped season by season';
    }
    return '';
  }

  // ⭐ 26.99955-05 — THE APP'S VERSION NUMBER, AND ITS ONE SOURCE.
  // Her own instruction, mid-sitting on 2026-08-26, verbatim: "also need to
  // include the version number"; asked WHERE, she picked "In 'about your
  // D-03 / UPD-03 (26.9996): publish-only date stamp via /api/status
  // `release_date` → APP.releaseDate. Dev trees omit the line entirely;
  // released trees show the stamped YYYY-MM-DD beside the word she kept.
  // ⭐ THE WORD `version` BESIDE THE DATE IS HERS — she ruled "Keep
  // 'version'" (26.99955-06). ⛔ do NOT reword it, shorten it, or drop it
  // for bare digits. The hand-edited APP_VERSION semver constant is gone.

  function renderLibrarySection() {
    var box = manageLibraryBox();
    if (!box) { return; }
    var open = MANAGE.sectionOpen.library === true;
    var list = buildInsightBooks(MANAGE.items,
      Array.isArray(MANAGE.meta.filters) ? MANAGE.meta.filters : [],
      MANAGE_LIBRARY.books);
    // The rows are plain observation strings — the empty shelf reads as its
    // own truth line, each book as "<title> — <observation>". Rendered
    // inline through the escapeHtml seam (the renderStatsSection posture).
    var rows = list.length === 0
      ? [EMPTY_SHELF_HEAD + ' ' + EMPTY_SHELF_BODY]
      : list.map(function (book) {
          return book.title + ' — ' + libraryRowObservation(book);
        });
    // Stamp-or-omit: only when APP.releaseDate is a non-empty string from
    // the one status reader. Digits are a technical fact; no invented prose.
    var stampLine = (typeof APP.releaseDate === 'string' &&
      APP.releaseDate) ?
      ('<p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:14px;' +
        'margin:8px 0 0">' + escapeHtml('version ' + APP.releaseDate) +
        '</p>') : '';
    box.innerHTML = '<h3 style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--wood-deep);' +
      'color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--font-pixel);font-size:24px;' +
      'font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin:24px 0 8px;' +
      'padding:0 0 4px"><button type="button" class="manage-toggle" ' +
      'style="background:none;border:none;color:var(--ink);cursor:pointer;' +
      'font:inherit;padding:0;text-decoration:underline">about your library' +
      '</button></h3>' +
      (!open ? '' : rows.map(function (line) {
        return '<p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:14px;' +
          'margin:0 0 4px">' + escapeHtml(line) + '</p>';
      }).join('') + stampLine);
    var toggle = box.querySelector('.manage-toggle');
    if (toggle) {
      toggle.addEventListener('click', function () {
        MANAGE.sectionOpen.library = !open;
        renderLibrarySection();
      });
    }
  }

  // ---- 26.4-01: the whole-vault import + fence-disclosure screen (D-06, SC3)
  //
  // The screen renders BEFORE any read of the vault begins: it reads
  // meta.fenced_roster (the shipped default when the store carries none yet)
  // and lists the fenced folders in plain words. The roster is adjustable
  // HERE, and every edit is written through the SAME roster route the
  // born-flag reads (/api/librarian/roster) so the stamp uses exactly the
  // roster she confirmed on screen. The whole-vault (vault-root) import
  // fires ONLY from confirmVaultImport — never at render. No count, no
  // time-gap copy anywhere (law 3).

  // The shipped default roster, mirrored for the case where meta carries no
  // fenced_roster key yet (a store missing it behaves like a fresh one).
  // 26.91-39 (A-27): invented EXAMPLES, renamed out of the shipped source.
  // This one already had the stored-value precedence at both its call sites
  // (the import screen and the config card), so it was a fallback in fact as
  // well as in name — which is exactly what REFLECTION_FENCED_ROSTER was not.
  var VAULT_DEFAULT_ROSTER = ['Journal', 'personnel notes',
    'billing & insurance notes', 'appraisal record'];

  // ⛔ HER FRAMING SENTENCE HAS ONE SOURCE (26.96-10, WR-05). It was typed
  // TWICE — once in the Manage pane and once on the import screen — and the
  // second copy was pinned by nothing, so a reword of either could diverge
  // silently. That is exactly how already-approved copy rots, one surface at a
  // time, and this phase's own copy record carries the incident: a candidate
  // annotated "your existing sentence" turned out to be a REWORD, and she had
  // to be asked a second time.
  //
  // ⚠ The import screen appends its OWN trailing clause — about beginning,
  // which is true there and false in settings. That clause stays a separate
  // literal because it genuinely belongs to that one host. ⛔ The bytes ABOVE
  // are hers and an agent may not alter one of them.
  var ROSTER_FRAMING = 'These folders stay private. The ' +
    'librarian never reads them, and nothing from them appears in the ' +
    'room until you choose to release a specific item.';

  // ✅ SHE RULED THIS, 2026-08-20, AT THE OWNER SITTING. The seat is no longer
  // empty and this comment is no longer "awaiting her".
  //
  // WHAT THE CASE IS. When the pane cannot read her list it must not state a
  // protection it has not read — that subtraction is this constant's whole
  // job, and it still ships. Before the fix the pane showed FOUR FOLDER NAMES
  // IT HAD MADE UP underneath her own sentence promising those folders stay
  // private, so a person who had cleared her list was promised something she
  // did not have.
  //
  // ⚠ PROVENANCE — TIER 2, APPROVED-AS-SHOWN. Four candidates were offered
  // and she chose B1, which deliberately echoes the shape of the failure line
